(window.QUIZ_CHUNKS=window.QUIZ_CHUNKS||{})["e1c.d3514c6c98.js"]={"41":[{"id":"sdl41_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly identifies the three interrelated pathogenic processes that together produce the characteristic features of systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Bacterial infection, neutrophilic abscess formation in the dermis, and Type I collagen degradation by matrix metalloproteinases","B":"Type II complement-mediated hypersensitivity, endothelial lysis, and granulomatous replacement of dermal collagen by macrophages","C":"B-cell immune complex deposition in vessel walls, fibrinoid necrosis of arterioles, and consequent fibroblast inhibition","D":"Mast cell degranulation with eosinophil recruitment, dermis destruction by major basic protein, and TGF-alpha-driven re-epithelialization","E":"Autoimmune inflammation, widespread microvascular damage, and progressive interstitial and perivascular fibrosis in the skin and multiple organs"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Systemic sclerosis results from three interrelated processes: (1) autoimmune inflammation driven by CD4+ T cells and humoral immunity, (2) widespread microvascular damage affecting small blood vessels, and (3) progressive fibrosis of the skin and viscera. These three processes are interdependent — immune activation drives vascular injury, and both together trigger the fibrogenic response. The other answer choices describe mechanisms of bacterial infection, hypersensitivity vasculitis, immune complex disease, and eosinophilic syndromes, none of which are the primary pathogenic triad in systemic sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc pathogenic triad = autoimmunity + microvascular damage + fibrosis; all three operate simultaneously. COMLEX: Systemic sclerosis is a systemic disease — skin is most visible but GI, lung, kidney, and heart are frequently involved.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which CD4+ T helper cell subtype has been isolated from the skin of patients with systemic sclerosis and is considered responsible for driving the fibrogenic cytokine response?","choices":{"A":"Th2 cells, which produce TGF-beta and IL-13 that stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe collagen and fibronectin genes throughout the dermis","B":"Th1 cells, which produce IFN-gamma that activates macrophages to deposit compact collagen bundles throughout the dermis","C":"Th17 cells, which produce IL-17 that triggers fibroblast hyperproliferation and elastin degradation in the deep reticular dermis","D":"Th22 cells, which produce IL-22 that directly activates dermal fibroblasts to deposit Type IV collagen in the basement membrane zone","E":"Regulatory T cells, which produce TGF-beta to suppress local inflammation while paradoxically driving collagen overproduction in fibroblasts"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Th2 cells have been isolated from the skin of systemic sclerosis patients. These cells produce TGF-beta and IL-13, both of which stimulate fibroblasts to transcribe genes encoding collagen and other extracellular matrix proteins such as fibronectin. Th1-derived IFN-gamma would typically suppress fibrosis rather than promote it. Th17 and Th22 cells play primary roles in other autoimmune conditions. Regulatory T cells produce TGF-beta in an anti-inflammatory context but are not the dominant effector cells accumulating in SSc skin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc fibrosis driven by Th2 cytokines TGF-beta and IL-13 stimulating fibroblast collagen gene transcription. COMLEX: TGF-beta is the master fibrogenic cytokine; it also drives perivascular fibrosis from platelet release during endothelial injury.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies is highly specific for systemic sclerosis and is associated with diffuse disease, pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral vascular disease?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA (Ro), which is the most specific serologic marker for diffuse systemic sclerosis with aggressive visceral involvement","B":"Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, which is elevated in systemic sclerosis during flares of scleroderma renal crisis","C":"Anti-centromere antibody, which is specifically associated with diffuse systemic sclerosis with early and rapid visceral involvement","D":"Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70), specific for diffuse systemic sclerosis and associated with pulmonary fibrosis and vascular disease","E":"Anti-U1-RNP antibody, which is highly specific for systemic sclerosis when found in isolation without features of mixed connective tissue disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70) is highly specific for systemic sclerosis. It is detected in 10-20% of patients with diffuse SSc by the Robbins Ch 6 text (Robbins Table 6.10 gives 30-70%; course SDL 44 says up to 40%) and correlates with pulmonary fibrosis and peripheral vascular disease. Anti-centromere antibody, not anti-Scl 70, is associated with limited disease and the CREST syndrome. Anti-SSA/Ro is the serologic marker for Sjogren syndrome. Anti-dsDNA is specific for SLE. Anti-U1-RNP characterizes mixed connective tissue disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Scl 70 = diffuse SSc + pulmonary fibrosis; anti-centromere = limited SSc + CREST; both are highly specific for SSc. COMLEX: The presence of anti-Scl 70 indicates higher risk for pulmonary and vascular complications requiring closer monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Systemic sclerosis autoimmunity involves CD4+ Th2 cells releasing TGF-beta and IL-13 that drive fibroblast collagen overproduction in a Type IV hypersensitivity pattern."},{"id":"sdl41_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the typical distribution and progression of skin involvement in diffuse systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Diffuse sclerotic atrophy starting in the fingers and distal arms, then extending proximally to the upper arms, shoulders, and face","B":"Diffuse inflammatory plaques beginning on the trunk and proximal extremities, then progressing distally to the fingertips over months","C":"Patchy erythematous rash following a dermatomal distribution on the face and scalp, with sparing of the fingers and distal extremities","D":"Symmetric scaling plaques concentrated on extensor surfaces of the elbows and knees, sparing the face and distal fingers entirely","E":"Violaceous papules at the knuckles and proximal nail folds progressing to scalp alopecia and periorbital edema over time"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In diffuse systemic sclerosis, sclerotic atrophy begins distally in the fingers and moves proximally up the upper extremities to the upper arms, shoulders, neck, and face. This distal-to-proximal pattern is characteristic of SSc and helps distinguish it from other fibrosing conditions. The description of inflammatory plaques on the trunk is not typical of SSc. Scaling extensor plaques suggest psoriasis. Violaceous knuckle papules with periorbital edema suggest dermatomyositis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc skin distribution = distal to proximal; starts in fingers, extends toward trunk. COMLEX: Sclerodactyly (skin tightening over fingers) + Raynaud in any patient warrants SSc workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A skin biopsy from a patient with advanced systemic sclerosis would be expected to show which of the following histologic findings?","choices":{"A":"Neutrophilic infiltration of the dermis with microabscess formation and enzymatic destruction of dermal collagen bundles","B":"Eosinophilic infiltration of the deep dermis with sclerosing lipogranulomas and hypertrophic collagen bundles in a fasciitis pattern","C":"Intense perivascular lymphoplasmacytic infiltrate with flame figures and focal necrosis of dermal appendages in a rosette pattern","D":"Lichenoid lymphocytic infiltration at the dermal-epidermal junction with basal cell destruction and colloid body formation","E":"Increased compact dermal collagen, thin epidermis without rete pegs, atrophic adnexa, and hyaline thickening of arteriolar walls"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Advanced SSc skin biopsy shows extensive deposition of compact collagen in the dermis with thinning of the epidermis, loss of rete pegs, atrophy of dermal appendages including hair follicles and sebaceous glands, and hyaline thickening of arteriole and capillary walls. The epidermis is thinned, not thickened. Neutrophilic microabscesses suggest acute infection or Sweet syndrome. Eosinophilic fasciitis is a separate entity. Lichenoid infiltrate with basal destruction suggests lichen planus or lupus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc biopsy = compact collagen deposits + thinned epidermis + lost rete pegs + atrophied appendages + thickened vessel walls. COMLEX: Loss of dermal appendages on biopsy means permanent loss of sweat glands and hair follicles in affected skin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In patients with the CREST variant of systemic sclerosis, what does the C in the CREST acronym represent, and where do these lesions most commonly occur?","choices":{"A":"Calreticulin deposits within the nucleus of epidermal keratinocytes that cause rete peg loss and thinning of the epidermis","B":"Focal and diffuse subcutaneous calcifications, especially in CREST syndrome, felt as firm nodules beneath the skin at pressure points","C":"Calcium pyrophosphate crystal deposition in the synovium of interphalangeal joints causing chondrocalcinosis and an inflammatory arthropathy","D":"Calcification of the internal elastic lamina of digital arteries causing critical limb ischemia and digital gangrene","E":"Hydroxyapatite crystal accumulation within the tendon sheaths of the flexor tendons of the hand causing tenosynovitis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In the CREST acronym, C stands for calcinosis — focal and diffuse subcutaneous calcifications that develop particularly in patients with limited systemic sclerosis and the CREST syndrome. These calcifications are composed of calcium hydroxyapatite and occur in soft tissues, especially at pressure points. They are visible on imaging and can be palpated as firm nodules. The other options describe calreticulin deposits, CPPD disease, vessel wall calcification, and tendon sheath disease, none of which represent the C in CREST.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CREST = Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasia; associated with anti-centromere antibody and limited SSc. COMLEX: CREST patients have a more benign course than diffuse SSc; visceral involvement occurs late if at all.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — TGF-beta and IL-13 produced in systemic sclerosis skin directly stimulate fibroblast collagen gene transcription, representing the effector cytokine function that drives dermal fibrosis."},{"id":"sdl41_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Raynaud phenomenon is present in virtually all patients with systemic sclerosis and may precede other symptoms. Which of the following best describes the pathophysiologic mechanism of Raynaud phenomenon in SSc?","choices":{"A":"IgG immune complex deposition in digital vessel walls activates complement and triggers neutrophilic vasculitis that causes episodic luminal occlusion","B":"Anti-endothelial cell antibodies directly lyse endothelial cells, causing structural vessel collapse and obliterative endarteritis of digital arteries","C":"Mast cell degranulation in the adventitia of digital arteries releases histamine, causing episodic vasodilation alternating with reactive vasoconstriction","D":"Endothelial injury releases platelet PDGF and TGF-beta, causing perivascular fibrosis and adrenergic receptor upregulation that drives vasoconstriction","E":"Th1-mediated macrophage activation releases reactive oxygen species that oxidize nitric oxide in digital arteries, causing irreversible capillary bed obliteration"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In SSc, repeated cycles of endothelial injury are followed by platelet aggregation. Activated platelets release PDGF and TGF-beta, which trigger perivascular fibrosis. Vascular smooth muscle cells also show increased expression of adrenergic receptors, making them hyperresponsive to vasoconstrictive stimuli. This combination of structural narrowing and functional hyperreactivity produces the episodic vasoconstriction of Raynaud phenomenon. Immune complex vasculitis and macrophage-mediated destruction are not the primary mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Raynaud in SSc = endothelial injury cycle → platelet PDGF/TGF-beta release → perivascular fibrosis + adrenergic receptor upregulation → vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Raynaud is often the first symptom of SSc; present in virtually all patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Scleroderma renal crisis involves a characteristic pattern of vascular injury restricted to a specific caliber of renal vessel. Which of the following correctly identifies the vessel size affected and the histologic change in scleroderma renal disease?","choices":{"A":"Interlobular arteries show intimal thickening from deposition of mucinous or finely collagenous material, with concentric proliferation of intimal cells","B":"Glomerular capillaries show subendothelial immune complex deposits with complement activation causing membranoproliferative glomerulonephritis with red cell casts","C":"Large renal arteries show granulomatous inflammation with giant cells and intimal hyperplasia similar to the renovascular lesion of Takayasu arteritis","D":"Small renal veins develop fibrin thrombi and venous congestion causing cortical necrosis from venous outflow obstruction in the medullary rays","E":"Arterioles throughout the cortex show eosinophilic infiltration with fibrinoid necrosis identical in distribution to ANCA-associated pauci-immune vasculitis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"In scleroderma renal disease, the characteristic vascular changes are found in the interlobular arteries (Robbins Ch 6). These vessels show intimal thickening from deposition of mucinous or finely collagenous material (histochemically positive for glycoprotein and acid mucopolysaccharides) with concentric intimal cell proliferation. There are no specific glomerular changes. In malignant hypertension from scleroderma renal crisis, fibrinoid necrosis, thrombosis, and infarction are superimposed on these pre-existing vascular changes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Scleroderma renal crisis = interlobular arteries with mucinous intimal thickening; malignant hypertension adds fibrinoid necrosis; accounts for ~50% of SSc deaths historically. COMLEX: Treat scleroderma renal crisis with ACE inhibitors; they are the only therapy shown to improve outcomes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension is a major complication of systemic sclerosis. Which of the following best describes the initiating mechanism of pulmonary hypertension in this disease?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies deposit in pulmonary vessel walls, directly activating complement and causing fibrinoid necrosis of pulmonary arterioles","B":"Granulomatous inflammation centered on the elastic lamina of pulmonary arteries mirrors the GCA mechanism and narrows the pulmonary arterial lumen","C":"Pulmonary endothelial dysfunction causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels, a Raynaud-like phenomenon that raises pulmonary artery pressure","D":"Diffuse alveolar hemorrhage from leukocytoclastic vasculitis of pulmonary capillaries causes reactive pulmonary arterial muscularization","E":"Immune complex deposition in pulmonary arterioles triggers Type III hypersensitivity fibrinoid necrosis of the entire pulmonary vascular bed"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction in SSc causes vasospasm of pulmonary vessels, which is considered the initiating pathogenic event in pulmonary arterial hypertension in this disease. This is conceptually analogous to Raynaud phenomenon, where endothelial injury and dysfunction of the systemic microvasculature causes episodic vasospasm. Over time, structural remodeling adds to the functional vasospasm, producing sustained pulmonary hypertension. SSc is not an ANCA-associated or immune complex disease in the pulmonary circulation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc pulmonary HTN = endothelial dysfunction causing vasospasm in the pulmonary circulation; pulmonary involvement now exceeds renal disease as the leading cause of SSc death. COMLEX: Screen all SSc patients annually for pulmonary hypertension with echocardiography and PFTs.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 53 → Obj 1 — Raynaud phenomenon in systemic sclerosis arises from microvascular endothelial injury and adrenergic receptor upregulation in smooth muscle, the same vasospastic mechanism studied in isolated Raynaud."},{"id":"sdl41_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the pathologic mechanism by which Sjögren syndrome produces keratoconjunctivitis sicca and xerostomia?","choices":{"A":"Immune complex deposition in the ductal basement membrane of lacrimal and salivary glands triggers complement-mediated acinar cell necrosis and ductal obliteration","B":"CD4+ helper T cells and B cells including plasma cells infiltrate the lacrimal and salivary glands, causing immunologically mediated destruction of these exocrine glands and reducing secretion","C":"Autoantibodies against the M3 muscarinic receptor block acetylcholine-mediated secretion by acinar cells, reducing tear and saliva production without causing gland destruction","D":"Granulomatous inflammation centered on the salivary duct epithelium causes ductal obstruction and secondary acinar atrophy through backpressure injury","E":"NK cell-mediated cytotoxicity against ductal epithelial cells expressing the SS-A antigen destroys the duct lining and prevents secretion of tears and saliva"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sjögren syndrome results from lymphocytic infiltration of the lacrimal and salivary glands. The infiltrate contains predominantly activated CD4+ helper T cells and B cells including plasma cells. This immunologically mediated destruction progressively impairs secretory function, causing dry eyes and dry mouth. Immune complex deposition is not the primary mechanism. Anti-M3 muscarinic receptor antibodies may contribute in some patients but are not the primary lesion. Granulomatous and NK cell mechanisms are not characteristic of Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren = CD4+ T cell and B cell infiltration of lacrimal and salivary glands causing exocrine destruction; dry eyes and dry mouth are the hallmark symptoms. COMLEX: Sjögren is the most common autoimmune exocrinopathy; female-to-male ratio is markedly skewed toward women.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the earliest histologic finding in the salivary glands of a patient with Sjögren syndrome, before disease has reached an advanced stage?","choices":{"A":"Dense germinal center formation throughout the gland parenchyma with obliteration of acinar structures and fibrotic replacement of secretory tissue","B":"Acinar atrophy with fat replacement and scattered residual lymphocytes without active inflammatory infiltrate indicating late-stage burnout","C":"Periductal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltrates as the initial lesion, before extensive inflammation and germinal centers develop","D":"Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells surrounding the ductal epithelium in a noncaseating sarcoid-like pattern","E":"Diffuse neutrophilic infiltration of the acini with microabscess formation and suppurative ductal destruction as the acute-phase reaction"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The earliest histologic finding in Sjögren syndrome-affected salivary glands is periductal and perivascular lymphocytic infiltration. This progresses over time to extensive lymphocytic infiltration with lymphoid follicle and germinal center formation, ductal epithelial hyperplasia, acinar atrophy, fibrosis, and eventual fat replacement. Dense germinal centers and acinar atrophy with fat replacement represent advanced stages, not early disease. Granulomatous and neutrophilic patterns do not characterize Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren earliest histology = periductal/perivascular lymphocytic infiltration; advanced = germinal centers, acinar atrophy, fibrosis, fat replacement. COMLEX: Minor salivary gland biopsy (lower lip) is the standard diagnostic procedure.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 55-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome develops a painless enlarging parotid gland mass over several months. Biopsy reveals a monoclonal B-cell population with marginal zone morphology. Which of the following best describes the mechanism by which Sjögren syndrome predisposes to this malignant complication?","choices":{"A":"Chronic CD8+ T cell cytotoxicity against ductal epithelium releases neoantigens that drive sustained NK cell activation toward lymphomagenesis in the gland","B":"Anti-SSB antibodies bind to a B-cell surface receptor and constitutively activate the BCR signaling pathway, bypassing normal proliferation checkpoints","C":"Viral integration into the salivary gland genome by HTLV or HIV triggers direct oncogenic transformation of ductal epithelial cells into B-cell lymphoma","D":"Ongoing polyclonal B-cell stimulation in the inflamed gland lets single clones acquire mutations and a growth advantage, producing marginal zone lymphoma","E":"Hypergammaglobulinemia from sustained polyclonal B-cell activation overwhelms somatic hypermutation proofreading, activating BCL-2 and c-Myc"},"correct":"D","explanation":"About 5% of Sjögren syndrome patients develop B-cell lymphoma, an incidence 40-fold higher than the general population. The mechanism is prolonged polyclonal B-cell stimulation within the chronically inflamed gland. Over time, individual B-cell clones acquire somatic mutations that confer a proliferative advantage, eventually producing a monoclonal marginal zone lymphoma — the same mechanism that drives lymphoma in other chronic B-cell stimulation states such as Hashimoto thyroiditis. HTLV, HIV, and hepatitis C are associated with Sjögren-like disease but are not established causes of the lymphoma complication.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren syndrome is associated with a 40-fold increased risk of marginal zone B-cell lymphoma arising from chronic polyclonal B-cell stimulation. COMLEX: Parotid mass in a Sjögren patient warrants biopsy to exclude lymphoma; emergence of a dominant clone signals malignant transformation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Sjögren syndrome exemplifies autoimmune exocrine gland destruction driven by self-antigen-specific CD4+ T cells that escaped central tolerance."},{"id":"sdl41_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following autoantibodies are considered the most important serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome and can be detected in up to 90% of patients using sensitive assay techniques?","choices":{"A":"Anti-double-stranded DNA and anti-Smith antibodies, which are highly specific for SLE and elevated in secondary Sjögren syndrome","B":"Anti-centromere and anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70), which together are specific for Sjögren syndrome complicated by concurrent scleroderma","C":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La), two ribonucleoprotein autoantibodies that are considered the diagnostic serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome","D":"Anti-Jo-1 and anti-Mi-2, which are the primary serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome when complicated by inflammatory myopathy","E":"ANCA in a perinuclear pattern, which is elevated in Sjögren syndrome when it is associated with glomerulonephritis and systemic vasculitis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La) are ribonucleoprotein autoantibodies that serve as the major serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome. They are detectable in up to 90% of patients using sensitive techniques. Anti-dsDNA and anti-Smith are markers for SLE. Anti-centromere and anti-Scl 70 are markers for systemic sclerosis. Anti-Jo-1 and anti-Mi-2 are markers for inflammatory myopathies. ANCA is associated with vasculitides, not Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren serologic markers = anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La); detectable in up to 90% by sensitive techniques. COMLEX: Anti-SSA/SSB can cross the placenta and cause neonatal lupus and congenital heart block; test pregnant women with these antibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Approximately what percentage of patients with Sjögren syndrome are rheumatoid factor positive, even in the absence of concurrent rheumatoid arthritis, and what is the mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Fewer than 10%, because RF is produced predominantly by synovial plasma cells in RA and does not arise from exocrine gland inflammation","B":"Approximately 75%, because chronic B-cell activation and polyclonal B-cell stimulation in Sjögren syndrome commonly produce RF, which is an antibody reactive with self-IgG","C":"Approximately 20%, because only the subset of Sjögren patients with secondary disease and concurrent RA produce detectable rheumatoid factor","D":"Over 95%, because RF production is obligatory in any autoimmune exocrinopathy driven by CD4+ T cells and plasma cell infiltration of salivary glands","E":"Approximately 40%, because RF elevation requires glomerulonephritis-associated immune complex deposition to sustain the B-cell activation needed for RF production"},"correct":"B","explanation":"About 75% of Sjögren syndrome patients are rheumatoid factor positive, even without concurrent rheumatoid arthritis. RF is an antibody reactive with self-IgG, and its production reflects the chronic polyclonal B-cell activation that is a hallmark of Sjögren syndrome. This high RF prevalence underscores that RF is not specific for RA. The polyclonal B-cell stimulation in Sjögren syndrome also drives ANA positivity, hypergammaglobulinemia, and ultimately the increased risk of B-cell lymphoma.","boardPrep":"Step 1: RF positive in ~75% of Sjögren patients regardless of RA; RF is not RA-specific. COMLEX: High RF in Sjögren reflects chronic polyclonal B-cell activation — the same process that drives the 40-fold increased lymphoma risk.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"High-titer anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies in a Sjögren syndrome patient correlate specifically with which of the following clinical manifestations?","choices":{"A":"Accelerated development of marginal zone lymphoma through direct BCR crosslinking by the Ro ribonucleoprotein antigen complexed with anti-SSA","B":"Early and severe salivary gland enlargement, because anti-SSA directly targets the M3 acetylcholine receptor on the surface of salivary acinar cells","C":"Glomerulonephritis through immune complex deposition in the glomerular basement membrane, similar to anti-SSA-mediated lupus nephritis","D":"Accelerated destruction of the corneal epithelium through complement-mediated lysis of lacrimal acinar cells bearing the SSA antigen","E":"Extraglandular manifestations such as synovitis, pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy, which are more common with high-titer anti-SSA"},"correct":"E","explanation":"High-titer anti-SSA (Ro) antibodies in Sjögren syndrome are specifically associated with extraglandular disease manifestations, including synovitis, diffuse pulmonary fibrosis, and peripheral neuropathy. These are seen in approximately one-third of Sjögren patients, and their presence correlates with elevated anti-SSA. In contrast, glomerular lesions are extremely rare in Sjögren syndrome (unlike SLE). Anti-SSA does not directly accelerate lymphomagenesis or cause gland destruction through receptor blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: High-titer anti-SSA in Sjögren = extraglandular manifestations: synovitis, pulmonary fibrosis, peripheral neuropathy. COMLEX: Anti-SSA also causes neonatal lupus and congenital complete heart block in offspring of mothers with high-titer anti-SSA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are diagnostic serologic markers for Sjögren syndrome within the spectrum of autoantibodies used in rheumatic disease evaluation."},{"id":"sdl41_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 49-year-old woman develops skin thickening that spreads within 4 months from her fingers to her forearms, upper arms, and anterior chest. She then presents with headache, a blood pressure of 196/112 mm Hg, and a rise in serum creatinine from 0.8 to 2.6 mg/dL over one week. Which autoantibody is most strongly associated with this acute-onset presentation?","choices":{"A":"Anti-centromere antibody, directed against the centromeric proteins CENP-A, CENP-B, and CENP-C","B":"Anti-RNA polymerase III antibody, directed against a nuclear enzyme that transcribes RNA","C":"Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, directed against native DNA in nuclear chromatin","D":"Anti-Ro (SS-A) antibody, directed against a ribonucleoprotein antigen in the cell","E":"Anti-Jo-1 antibody, directed against the enzyme histidyl-transfer RNA synthetase"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-RNA polymerase III antibodies, found in about 15–20% of patients with systemic sclerosis, are associated with acute onset of disease, scleroderma renal crisis, and cancer. This patient has rapidly spreading diffuse skin thickening followed by abrupt severe hypertension and rising creatinine, the picture of scleroderma renal crisis, which is treated with ACE inhibitors. Anti-centromere antibodies mark limited skin disease, ischemic digital loss, and pulmonary hypertension, not a rapidly spreading diffuse onset. Anti-dsDNA antibodies are specific for SLE and its nephritis, not for sclerosis of the skin. Anti-Ro (SS-A) is the serologic marker of Sjögren syndrome and, in SLE, of neonatal lupus and congenital heart block. Anti-Jo-1 defines antisynthetase syndrome, with myositis, interstitial lung disease, and arthritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc antibodies: anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) = diffuse skin + lung fibrosis; anti-centromere = limited/CREST, digital loss, pulmonary hypertension; anti-RNA polymerase III = acute onset, renal crisis, cancer. COMLEX: Scleroderma renal crisis (abrupt malignant hypertension with rising creatinine) is treated with ACE inhibitors.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman has had episodes of cold-induced white-then-blue fingers for 3 years. She has no skin thickening, digital ulcers, dysphagia, or rash. Antinuclear antibody testing on HEp-2 cells shows a centromere pattern, and anti-centromere antibody is confirmed. Which interpretation is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"The antibody confirms primary Raynaud disease, which carries no added risk of developing a connective tissue disease","B":"The centromere pattern reflects antibodies to double-stranded DNA and indicates early systemic lupus erythematosus","C":"The antibody establishes diffuse systemic sclerosis and predicts an imminent episode of scleroderma renal crisis","D":"The antibody is directed against histones and indicates drug-induced lupus caused by a vasodilator medication","E":"In isolated Raynaud phenomenon, anti-centromere antibody marks an increased risk of developing systemic sclerosis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Patients with isolated Raynaud phenomenon and anti-centromere antibodies are at increased risk for developing systemic sclerosis, and anti-centromere antibodies characterize the limited form with CREST features. The antibody is therefore a warning marker, not proof that the Raynaud is primary and harmless; FA reserves Raynaud disease for the idiopathic form and calls Raynaud secondary to CREST or another disease Raynaud syndrome. A centromere pattern comes from antibodies to CENP-A, CENP-B, or CENP-C, whereas antibodies to double-stranded DNA give a homogeneous nuclear pattern and point to SLE. Anti-centromere antibodies go with limited, not diffuse, skin disease, in which visceral involvement comes late or not at all. Drug-induced lupus is marked by antihistone antibodies, not anti-centromere antibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Centromere ANA pattern = anti-CENP-A/B/C → limited SSc/CREST (also primary biliary cholangitis, Sjögren); isolated Raynaud + anti-centromere = increased risk of SSc. Homogeneous pattern = anti-dsDNA/chromatin. COMLEX: Raynaud disease = primary/idiopathic; Raynaud syndrome = secondary (SLE, MCTD, CREST), where digital ulceration can occur; treat with calcium channel blockers.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the contribution of autoimmunity to the pathogenesis of systemic sclerosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has a positive ANA in a nucleolar staining pattern and negative anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere antibodies. She asks about the significance of her ANA test. Which of the following most accurately characterizes the ANA findings in systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Virtually all patients with systemic sclerosis have ANAs; specific antibodies such as anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere add diagnostic and prognostic information","B":"ANA positivity in a nucleolar pattern is specific for systemic sclerosis and indicates that visceral organ involvement has already begun at the time of first detection","C":"ANAs are absent in systemic sclerosis and their presence in this patient indicates a co-existing diagnosis of SLE or mixed connective tissue disease","D":"The nucleolar ANA pattern is caused exclusively by anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies and predicts the rapid development of Sjogren overlap syndrome","E":"ANA positivity is found in fewer than 30% of systemic sclerosis patients and its detection is a late marker of severe diffuse visceral involvement"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Virtually all systemic sclerosis patients have positive ANAs. The role of these ANAs in disease pathogenesis is unclear, but they provide diagnostic and prognostic information. Specific ANAs carry distinct implications: anti-Scl 70 indicates diffuse disease with pulmonary fibrosis risk, while anti-centromere indicates limited disease/CREST. A nucleolar ANA pattern is common in SSc but is not specific for it, nor does it exclusively indicate anti-SSA/anti-SSB. The absence of specific antibodies does not reduce the likelihood of SSc in a clinically compatible case.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANAs are found in virtually all SSc patients; specific antibodies (anti-Scl 70, anti-centromere) provide clinically actionable subtype and prognostic information. COMLEX: High-titer nucleolar ANA pattern should prompt specific SSc antibody testing even if anti-Scl 70 and anti-centromere are negative.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis has progressive difficulty opening her mouth fully, tapered clawlike finger deformities, and multiple small ulcerations at her fingertips. Skin biopsy confirms extensive dermal fibrosis. Which of the following best explains why the fingertip ulcerations develop in this clinical setting?","choices":{"A":"Anti-endothelial cell antibodies cause leukocytoclastic vasculitis in the dermal capillaries of the fingertips, producing immune-mediated digital infarction","B":"Subcutaneous calcification from CREST syndrome erodes through the thinned skin surface at digital tips, creating sterile ulcerations from calcium hydroxyapatite deposition","C":"Vascular obliteration from repeated endothelial injury and perivascular fibrosis progressively reduces blood supply, causing ischemic ulceration at the digital tips","D":"Collagen deposition compresses dermal lymphatics in the fingertips, causing lymphedema and secondary skin breakdown from impaired wound healing","E":"Dense dermal fibrosis physically abrades the overlying thinned epidermis during hand movement, producing pressure-induced frictional ulcerations at sites of stress"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Fingertip ulcerations in systemic sclerosis result from progressive vascular disease. Repeated cycles of endothelial injury and platelet aggregation, followed by PDGF/TGF-beta release, lead to perivascular fibrosis and eventual obliteration of the microvascular bed in the digital tips. This ischemia causes cutaneous ulcerations and, in severe cases, autoamputation of terminal phalanges. Leukocytoclastic vasculitis, calcinosis erosion, and lymphedema are not the primary mechanism of digital ulceration in SSc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Digital ulcers in SSc = ischemia from microvascular obliteration; severe disease leads to autoamputation of terminal phalanges. COMLEX: Nailfold capillaroscopy shows capillary loop distortion and obliteration early in SSc — a useful clinical tool.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing systemic sclerosis develops progressive dysphagia to solid foods and then liquids. Esophageal manometry reveals markedly reduced lower esophageal sphincter pressure with absent peristalsis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus. Which of the following best explains the pathologic basis for her esophageal dysfunction?","choices":{"A":"Atrophy and collagenous fibrous replacement of the muscularis in the lower two-thirds of the esophagus impair peristalsis and lower sphincter pressure","B":"CD4+ T cell infiltration of the myenteric plexus causes autoimmune destruction of Auerbach ganglia, producing a pattern identical to primary achalasia","C":"Anti-centromere antibodies bind to smooth muscle contractile proteins in the esophageal wall, directly inhibiting peristaltic contractions through receptor blockade","D":"Fibrinoid necrosis of esophageal submucosal arterioles from malignant hypertension causes ischemic smooth muscle loss and aperistalsis throughout the esophagus","E":"Granulomatous inflammation of the esophageal submucosa causes concentric stricture formation, producing obstructive dysphagia without motility loss"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The GI tract is affected in approximately 90% of SSc patients. The esophagus is the most severely affected segment. Progressive atrophy and collagenous fibrous replacement of the muscularis causes loss of peristaltic function in the lower two-thirds and reduces lower esophageal sphincter pressure. This leads to gastroesophageal reflux, Barrett esophagus risk, and eventually rubber-hose-like inflexibility of the esophageal wall. The mechanism is fibrosis of smooth muscle, not ganglion cell destruction (as in achalasia) or antibody receptor blockade.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc esophageal disease = collagenous replacement of muscularis causes aperistalsis and low LES pressure; gastroesophageal reflux in most patients. COMLEX: SSc is the most common systemic disease causing aperistalsis; management includes proton pump inhibitors and prokinetics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Recognize the typical skin changes in systemic sclerosis and the pathologic changes which cause these changes.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 59-year-old woman has skin tightening confined to her fingers and forearms that has progressed slowly over 5 years. She also has telangiectasias, calcinosis cutis at her fingertips, and episodic digital color changes with cold exposure. Her anti-centromere antibody is positive. Which of the following best characterizes her disease subset and expected clinical course?","choices":{"A":"She has diffuse scleroderma with rapid visceral progression; anti-centromere positivity indicates imminent scleroderma renal crisis and early pulmonary fibrosis","B":"She has limited scleroderma with CREST syndrome; skin involvement is confined to the fingers, forearms, and face, with late or absent visceral involvement","C":"She has the overlap syndrome of systemic sclerosis and SLE; anti-centromere is a specific marker for this overlap and indicates risk of lupus nephritis","D":"She has eosinophilic fasciitis, a variant of systemic sclerosis with anti-centromere positivity that predominantly affects the fascia rather than the dermis","E":"She has the diffuse form of systemic sclerosis presenting with a deceptively limited initial skin distribution; anti-centromere indicates visceral disease will not occur"},"correct":"B","explanation":"This patient has limited systemic sclerosis with CREST syndrome: Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, and Telangiectasia. Anti-centromere antibody is found in 20-30% of SSc patients (Robbins Ch 6 text; Table 6.10 gives 20-40% and course SDL 44 up to 40%) and is characteristically associated with this limited subset. Skin involvement in CREST is confined to the fingers, forearms, and face. Visceral involvement, including esophageal disease, pulmonary hypertension, and biliary cirrhosis, may occur late or not at all. The course is relatively benign compared with diffuse SSc, and scleroderma renal crisis is uncommon in this subset.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-centromere = CREST syndrome = limited SSc = better prognosis. COMLEX: CREST patients still need monitoring for pulmonary hypertension and biliary cirrhosis, which are late complications specific to this subset.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl41_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman with early systemic sclerosis is enrolled in a study of the vascular lesion of the disease. Which set of findings is the expected evidence of the microvascular injury that is present early in her disease?","choices":{"A":"Distorted nailfold capillary loops, raised von Willebrand factor levels, and increased circulating platelet aggregates","B":"Low serum C3 and C4 levels with granular immune-complex deposits in the walls of the small digital arteries","C":"High-titer myeloperoxidase ANCA with necrotizing neutrophilic capillaritis in the skin of the fingertips","D":"Circulating cryoglobulins that precipitate in the cold and plug the digital capillaries during each attack","E":"Granulomatous inflammation with multinucleated giant cells that fragments the internal elastic lamina"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Microvascular disease is consistently present early in systemic sclerosis and may be the initial lesion. Nailfold capillary loops are distorted early and obliterated later; injured, activated endothelium raises von Willebrand factor levels, and platelet activation increases circulating platelet aggregates. Repeated cycles of endothelial injury and platelet aggregation release PDGF and TGF-β, which drive perivascular fibrosis and progressive narrowing of the microvasculature, and FA describes the result as a noninflammatory vasculopathy. Low complement with granular immune-complex deposits is the pattern of immune-complex disease such as SLE. MPO-ANCA with necrotizing capillaritis is an ANCA-associated small-vessel vasculitis. Cryoglobulin precipitation is not part of the SSc vascular lesion. Granulomas with giant cells that disrupt the elastic lamina define giant cell arteritis, not the intimal proliferation of SSc.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc vasculopathy is noninflammatory: endothelial injury (↑von Willebrand factor), platelet activation (↑platelet aggregates, PDGF/TGF-β), intimal proliferation, nailfold capillaries distorted early then obliterated. COMLEX: Nailfold capillary distortion in a patient with Raynaud phenomenon points to structural vascular disease such as SSc.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 53-year-old woman with systemic sclerosis and Raynaud phenomenon asks why her fingers turn white, then blue, then red during cold exposure. Her nailfold capillaroscopy shows distorted and obliterated capillary loops. Which of the following most accurately describes the microvascular sequence producing a Raynaud episode in systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Cold exposure triggers anti-endothelial antibody complement activation, causing transient capillary occlusion followed by reactive hyperemia as complement becomes depleted","B":"Histamine release from perivascular mast cells in the cold induces initial vasodilation, then reflex vasoconstriction, then secondary vasodilation during rewarming","C":"Cold exposure causes PDGF-mediated smooth muscle contraction followed by passive filling of the dilated capillary bed, then reactive vasodilation from prostaglandin E2 release","D":"Cold triggers vasoconstriction of digital arteries and arterioles via upregulated adrenergic receptors, causing pallor and cyanosis, then reactive hyperemia on rewarming","E":"Cold directly induces TGF-beta-mediated endothelial cell apoptosis, releasing von Willebrand factor that causes transient thrombotic microangiopathy in digital capillaries"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Raynaud phenomenon in SSc is caused by episodic vasoconstriction of digital arteries and arterioles. In SSc, repeated endothelial injury has led to perivascular fibrosis and upregulation of adrenergic receptors on vascular smooth muscle. Cold exposure, which is a normal physiologic trigger for adrenergic vasoconstriction, produces an exaggerated vasoconstrictive response. This causes pallor from cutoff of blood flow, then cyanosis from deoxygenated stagnant blood, then reactive erythema from hyperemia on rewarming. The underlying structural vessel abnormality in SSc makes the episodes more severe and prolonged than in primary Raynaud.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Raynaud triple color sequence = white (vasospasm/pallor) → blue (cyanosis from stagnant blood) → red (reactive hyperemia); adrenergic receptor upregulation from SSc vascular disease amplifies cold-triggered vasoconstriction. COMLEX: Primary Raynaud has no structural disease; secondary Raynaud (as in SSc) has nailfold capillaroscopy abnormalities.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Understand the pathology of vasculopathy and the mechanism of organ damage occurring in Raynaud's phenomenon, scleroderma renal crisis, and pulmonary artery hypertension in scleroderma.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Two women with systemic sclerosis develop exertional dyspnea. Patient 1 has diffuse skin disease with anti-topoisomerase I (anti-Scl 70) antibodies. Patient 2 has had limited skin disease with CREST features for 15 years and has anti-centromere antibodies. Which pairing best matches each patient with her characteristic pulmonary complication?","choices":{"A":"Patient 1, pulmonary arterial hypertension from pulmonary vasospasm; Patient 2, interstitial fibrosis like idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","B":"Patient 1, granulomatous arteritis of the pulmonary trunk; Patient 2, diffuse alveolar hemorrhage from neutrophilic capillaritis","C":"Both patients, necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis of the small pulmonary arteries, the shared lung lesion of systemic sclerosis","D":"Both patients, immune-complex alveolitis, because both antibodies deposit in the alveolar walls and activate complement there","E":"Patient 1, interstitial fibrosis like idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis; Patient 2, pulmonary arterial hypertension from pulmonary vasospasm"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Pulmonary involvement occurs in more than half of patients with systemic sclerosis and takes two main forms. Anti-topoisomerase I antibodies mark diffuse disease and a higher likelihood of pulmonary fibrosis, which is indistinguishable from idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Anti-centromere antibodies mark limited disease with CREST features, in which pulmonary hypertension tends to appear late; it arises from vasospasm secondary to pulmonary vascular endothelial dysfunction. The reversed pairing assigns each lesion to the wrong antibody. Granulomatous arteritis and neutrophilic capillaritis with alveolar hemorrhage are lesions of the vasculitides, not of systemic sclerosis, and necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis is not a lung lesion of SSc. SSc lung disease is not an immune-complex alveolitis; whether its antibodies cause tissue damage at all is unclear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SSc lungs (>50% of patients): anti-Scl-70/diffuse → interstitial fibrosis like IPF; anti-centromere/limited → late pulmonary arterial hypertension from endothelial dysfunction and vasospasm. COMLEX: Pulmonary disease, not renal crisis, is now the leading cause of death in SSc.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 53 Obj 1 — Pathogenesis of Raynaud Phenomenon"},{"id":"sdl41_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with primary Sjögren syndrome reports nasal crusting with recurrent nosebleeds, repeated episodes of bronchitis, and painful intercourse from vaginal dryness. ANCA testing is negative, and she takes no medications. Which best explains these additional symptoms?","choices":{"A":"Necrotizing granulomatous vasculitis of the upper and lower airways that erodes the nasal septum and the bronchial walls","B":"Lymphocytic destruction of exocrine glands that extends beyond the lacrimal and salivary glands to airway and vaginal glands","C":"Immune-complex deposition in mucosal capillaries that causes ischemic atrophy of the nasal, bronchial, and vaginal mucosal linings","D":"Th17-driven neutrophilic invasion of mucosal surface epithelium that ulcerates the nasal, bronchial, and vaginal linings","E":"HTLV-1 infection of mucosal epithelium that directly lyses the glandular cells of the nose, bronchi, and genital tract"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Sjögren syndrome targets the lacrimal and salivary glands most heavily, but other exocrine glands, including those lining the respiratory and gastrointestinal tracts and the vagina, may be involved by the same lymphocytic infiltration. Dryness and crusting of the nose can lead to ulceration and even perforation of the nasal septum, airway involvement causes recurrent bronchitis and pneumonitis, and vaginal dryness causes dyspareunia. Necrotizing granulomatous airway vasculitis is the lesion of granulomatosis with polyangiitis, an ANCA-associated vasculitis that her negative ANCA argues against. Sjögren gland injury is lymphocyte-mediated, not ischemia from immune-complex deposition. The infiltrate is lymphocytic, with activated CD4+ T cells and B cells, and no T-cell subset has been established as the driver, so a Th17 neutrophilic process does not fit. A Sjögren-like disease occurs in some patients with HTLV, HIV, or hepatitis C infection, but the link is obscure and viral lysis does not explain primary Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren = lymphocytic exocrinopathy; beyond the eyes and mouth it dries the nose (crusting, septal perforation), airways (bronchitis, pneumonitis), and vagina (dyspareunia). COMLEX: Parotid enlargement occurs in about half of patients; persistent parotid enlargement can also signal MALT lymphoma.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with 12 years of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis develops a gritty sensation in her eyes and a persistently dry mouth. A labial salivary gland biopsy shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis. Which best describes her sicca disease?","choices":{"A":"Primary Sjögren syndrome, the form defined by sicca that occurs together with another established autoimmune disease","B":"Methotrexate-induced sicca, in which the drug blocks folate metabolism in acinar cells without any lymphocytic infiltrate","C":"Secondary Sjögren syndrome, autoimmune gland destruction that accompanies another autoimmune disease, most often RA","D":"Granulomatous sialadenitis of sarcoidosis, in which noncaseating granulomas replace the acini of the salivary glands","E":"Rheumatoid nodules that form within the salivary glands and compress the excretory ducts, blocking the flow of saliva"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Sjögren syndrome occurs either as an isolated disorder (the primary form, also called the sicca syndrome) or, more often, in association with another autoimmune disease (the secondary form). Rheumatoid arthritis is the most common associated disease, and more than half of patients have an accompanying autoimmune disorder. Focal lymphocytic sialadenitis on labial gland biopsy confirms the diagnosis. Sicca accompanying established RA is therefore secondary Sjögren syndrome; calling the coexisting form primary inverts the definitions. A drug effect without lymphocytic infiltration cannot account for focal lymphocytic sialadenitis, the immune-mediated lesion seen here. Noncaseating granulomas are the lesion of sarcoidosis, and her biopsy shows lymphocytes, not granulomas. Rheumatoid nodules (palisading histiocytes around fibrinoid necrosis) form in subcutaneous tissue and lung and do not produce lymphocytic gland destruction.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Primary Sjögren = sicca alone; secondary Sjögren = sicca + another autoimmune disease (RA most common; also SLE, SSc). Focal lymphocytic sialadenitis on labial gland biopsy confirms. COMLEX: RF is positive in about 75% of Sjögren patients with or without RA, so RF cannot separate primary from secondary disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Define the underlying pathologic cause of Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with primary Sjögren syndrome develops non-anion gap metabolic acidosis, glucosuria with normal serum glucose, and phosphaturia. Urinalysis shows no casts or significant proteinuria. Which of the following best explains this pattern of renal involvement in Sjögren syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA antibodies cause immune complex glomerulonephritis with subepithelial deposits, heavy proteinuria, and nephrotic syndrome in primary Sjögren syndrome","B":"Anti-SSB antibodies deposit in glomerular capillary walls, causing membranous nephropathy and subsequent secondary tubular dysfunction from nephrotic-range proteinuria","C":"Granulomatous interstitial nephritis caused by salivary gland-reactive T cells destroys glomerular podocytes, producing mixed nephrotic-nephritic syndrome","D":"Lymphocytic infiltration of the renal interstitium causes tubulointerstitial nephritis with tubular dysfunction such as renal tubular acidosis and glucosuria","E":"Fibrinoid necrosis of renal arterioles from ANCA-mediated vasculitis causes ischemic tubular injury and non-anion gap acidosis without glomerular involvement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Glomerular lesions are extremely rare in Sjögren syndrome, unlike SLE. Instead, the dominant renal manifestation is tubulointerstitial nephritis caused by lymphocytic infiltration of the renal tubular interstitium. This produces tubular dysfunction including renal tubular acidosis (type I or II), glucosuria, phosphaturia, and uricosuria — a Fanconi syndrome-like pattern. The absence of casts and significant proteinuria points to tubular rather than glomerular disease. ANCA vasculitis and immune complex GN are not features of primary Sjögren syndrome.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren renal disease = tubulointerstitial nephritis, NOT glomerulonephritis; tubular dysfunction includes RTA, glucosuria, phosphaturia. COMLEX: Contrast with SLE, where glomerulonephritis is the dominant renal lesion; Sjögren spares glomeruli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl41_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 50-year-old woman has had dry eyes, dry mouth, and intermittent parotid swelling for a year. A labial salivary gland biopsy shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis. Her antinuclear antibody test by immunofluorescence is negative. Which interpretation of the negative ANA is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"It excludes Sjögren syndrome, because antinuclear antibodies are detected in virtually every patient with the disease","B":"It favors systemic sclerosis as the cause of her sicca, because antinuclear antibodies are usually absent in that disease","C":"It does not exclude Sjögren syndrome, because ANA is found in only 50–80% of patients; anti-SS-A and anti-SS-B should be tested","D":"It defines seronegative Sjögren syndrome, in which anti-SS-A and anti-SS-B antibodies by definition cannot be present","E":"It points to SLE with secondary sicca, because a negative antinuclear antibody is the typical finding in untreated lupus"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Antinuclear antibodies are detected in only 50% to 80% of patients with Sjögren syndrome by immunofluorescence, whereas antibodies to the ribonucleoproteins SS-A (Ro) and SS-B (La) can be found in as many as 90% with sensitive techniques. A negative ANA therefore does not exclude the disease, particularly when the biopsy already shows focal lymphocytic sialadenitis, and specific anti-SS-A and anti-SS-B testing is the next serologic step. ANA in virtually every patient is a feature of systemic sclerosis, not Sjögren syndrome, so a negative ANA argues against SSc rather than for it. A negative immunofluorescence ANA does not rule out anti-SS-A or anti-SS-B, the main serologic markers of the disease. Generic ANAs are positive in 95–100% of patients with SLE, so a negative ANA makes lupus unlikely.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sjögren serology: anti-SS-A/SS-B up to 90% (sensitive assays), RF ~75%, ANA only 50–80%; SSc and SLE are ANA positive in virtually all patients. COMLEX: A negative ANA does not rule out Sjögren syndrome; order anti-SS-A and anti-SS-B and rely on the labial gland biopsy when serology is unrevealing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl41_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with primary Sjögren syndrome is 22 weeks pregnant. Fetal echocardiography shows complete atrioventricular block with a ventricular rate of 55/min. Which maternal antibody is most likely responsible, and how does it reach the fetus?","choices":{"A":"Rheumatoid factor, an IgM antibody that crosses the placenta and binds the developing cardiac conduction tissue","B":"Anti-centromere antibody, an IgG that crosses the placenta and binds the kinetochores of dividing cardiomyocytes","C":"Anti-double-stranded DNA antibody, an IgG that crosses the placenta and forms immune complexes in the fetal heart","D":"Anti-topoisomerase I antibody, an IgG that crosses the placenta and induces fibrosis of the fetal conduction system","E":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) antibody, an IgG that crosses the placenta and is associated with neonatal lupus and heart block"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Anti-SS-A (Ro) and anti-SS-B (La) antibodies are associated with congenital heart block and neonatal lupus. In an anti-SS-A–positive pregnant patient, the newborn is at increased risk of neonatal lupus, with congenital heart block, rash, transaminitis, and cytopenias, and FA lists this among the complications of Sjögren syndrome. The maternal antibody reaches the fetus because IgG is the only isotype that crosses the placenta. Rheumatoid factor is an IgM antibody, so it cannot cross. Anti-centromere antibodies are linked to limited systemic sclerosis, not to fetal heart block. Anti-dsDNA antibodies are specific for SLE and linked to nephritis. Anti-topoisomerase I antibodies mark diffuse systemic sclerosis and lung fibrosis. None of these three is the antibody tied to congenital heart block.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Maternal anti-SS-A (Ro)/anti-SS-B (La) IgG → neonatal lupus: congenital heart block, rash, transaminitis, cytopenias. IgG is the only isotype that crosses the placenta; RF is IgM. COMLEX: Anti-SS-A status matters in any woman with Sjögren syndrome or SLE who is pregnant or planning pregnancy, because the risk falls on the fetus even when the mother is well.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl41_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Recognize the typical antibody profile of patients with Sjögren's syndrome.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 57-year-old woman with Sjögren syndrome is counseled that anti-SSA and anti-SSB antibodies are not entirely specific for her condition. Which of the following best explains this diagnostic limitation of these serologic markers?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are also found in all patients with systemic sclerosis and are used as required diagnostic criteria for the CREST variant of SSc","B":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are identical in molecular target to anti-Smith antibodies, which are also elevated in both SLE and Sjögren syndrome and cannot distinguish between the two","C":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are detectable in all autoimmune diseases because they target ubiquitous intracellular RNA-binding proteins expressed in every cell type of the body","D":"Anti-SSA and anti-SSB also occur in a minority of SLE patients, so they are diagnostic markers for Sjögren syndrome but cannot alone exclude other autoimmune diseases","E":"Anti-SSA is found exclusively in secondary Sjögren syndrome associated with RA, while anti-SSB is found exclusively in primary Sjögren syndrome without concurrent autoimmune disease"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-SSA (Ro) and anti-SSB (La) antibodies are the most important serologic markers of Sjögren syndrome but are not entirely specific. They are also present in a smaller percentage of SLE patients. Therefore, their presence alone does not exclude SLE or confirm Sjögren syndrome. The full clinical picture, including the sicca syndrome, salivary gland biopsy findings, and exclusion of SLE, is required to make a definitive Sjögren diagnosis. Anti-SSA and anti-SSB are not markers of SSc, are not identical to anti-Smith, and are not present in all autoimmune conditions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-SSA and anti-SSB = best serologic markers for Sjögren but also seen in a subset of SLE patients; use clinical context and biopsy for definitive diagnosis. COMLEX: The combination of positive anti-SSA/anti-SSB with characteristic biopsy findings meets classification criteria for Sjögren syndrome.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"}],"42":[{"id":"sdl42_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which genetic factor in SLE most directly impairs clearance of apoptotic nuclear debris, thereby sustaining the autoantigen burden that drives anti-dsDNA production?","choices":{"A":"Deficiency of early complement components such as C1q, C2, or C4, which are required for removal of apoptotic bodies and immune complexes","B":"HLA-DQ allelic variation linked to anti-Sm and antiphospholipid antibody production via altered peptide presentation in thymic selection","C":"Genome-wide association loci encoding proteins involved in lymphocyte signaling that lower the threshold for autoreactive B-cell activation","D":"Defective peripheral tolerance checkpoints for autoreactive CD4+ helper T cells that escape deletion in lymph node germinal centers","E":"Overexpression of TLR7 and TLR9 on plasmacytoid dendritic cells that amplifies downstream interferon responses to self nucleic acids"},"correct":"A","explanation":"C1q normally opsonizes apoptotic bodies for phagocytic clearance; its deficiency allows nuclear autoantigens to persist, engage TLRs on B cells and DCs, and sustain anti-DNA production. HLA-DQ variation influences autoantibody specificity but not clearance. Non-MHC GWAS loci affect signaling thresholds but are not clearance mechanisms. Peripheral T-cell tolerance defects drive amplification downstream. TLR overexpression amplifies responses that depend on antigen persistence already established by complement deficiency.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Complement deficiency (C1q, C2, C4) is the classic genetic predisposition to SLE; C1q knockout mice develop lupus-like disease. COMLEX: C4A null allele is a key genetic risk factor — know the complement-clearance mechanism.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which environmental trigger exacerbates SLE by simultaneously inducing cellular apoptosis, modifying nuclear DNA to enhance TLR recognition, and stimulating keratinocytes to release pro-inflammatory IL-1?","choices":{"A":"Chronic intracellular bacterial infections that produce molecular mimics of nuclear antigens and trigger polyclonal B-cell activation via superantigen-like mechanisms","B":"Viral triggers including Epstein-Barr virus that activate latent autoreactive B cells through molecular mimicry of Sm antigen ribonucleoprotein complexes","C":"Estrogen and other sex hormones that promote B-cell survival and activation in female patients of reproductive age, explaining the 9:1 female-to-male ratio","D":"Ultraviolet light, which drives keratinocyte apoptosis releasing nuclear autoantigens, alters DNA to enhance TLR7 and TLR9 recognition, and induces keratinocyte IL-1","E":"Drug exposures including hydralazine and procainamide that induce histone hypomethylation and interfere with central tolerance by altering thymic antigen presentation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"UV light exacerbates SLE through three mechanisms: inducing apoptosis in skin cells that releases nuclear autoantigens, altering DNA in ways that enhance TLR recognition by innate immune sensors, and stimulating keratinocytes to produce IL-1, which amplifies local inflammation. Bacterial molecular mimicry is not a named SLE trigger. EBV is proposed but the UV triad is the testable mechanism. Sex hormones contribute to sex bias but are not environmental exacerbants per se. Drug-induced lupus from hydralazine and procainamide involves histone modification, not the UV pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UV light is the classic SLE environmental trigger — exacerbates via apoptosis, TLR-modified DNA, and IL-1. Malar rash worsens with sun exposure. COMLEX: Counsel SLE patients on sun avoidance and sunscreen use.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"TLR stimulation by self nucleic acids contributes to SLE pathogenesis primarily by activating which two cell populations to perpetuate a cycle of autoantibody production and type I interferon secretion?","choices":{"A":"NK cells and mast cells, which release cytotoxic granules and histamine upon TLR stimulation by self DNA, producing widespread tissue injury in SLE target organs","B":"Nuclear antigen-specific B cells and plasmacytoid dendritic cells: TLR7/9 signals from internalized nucleic acids drive autoantibody production and pDC IFN-alpha release","C":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells and plasma cells, which mediate direct cellular killing of self-reactive targets and secrete high-affinity autoantibodies respectively via TLR-independent pathways","D":"Neutrophils and tissue macrophages, which phagocytose apoptotic debris via TLR4 engagement and present nuclear antigens on MHC class II to stimulate naive autoreactive T cells","E":"Regulatory T cells and follicular helper T cells, which lose suppressive control via TLR9 engagement and then co-stimulate autoreactive B cells in germinal centers via CD40L"},"correct":"B","explanation":"B cells specific for nuclear antigens internalize immune complexes through surface immunoglobulin, and the nucleic acid components engage TLR7 and TLR9 to provide a second activating signal driving autoantibody production. Simultaneously, plasmacytoid DCs activated by self nucleic acids via TLRs produce IFN-alpha that further activates B cells and autoreactive T cells, creating an amplification loop. NK cells and mast cells are not the central TLR-driven players in SLE. CD8+ T cells contribute but are not the primary TLR responders. Neutrophils and macrophages clear debris but B cells and pDCs are the critical amplifiers. Treg loss is downstream of TLR-driven activation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The type I IFN signature (high serum IFN-alpha) is a biomarker of SLE disease activity; correlates with severity. TLR7 and TLR9 on B cells and pDCs are activated by self RNA and DNA from apoptotic nuclear debris. COMLEX: TLR dysregulation bridges innate and adaptive autoimmunity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — SLE exemplifies autoimmune failure of self-tolerance driven by genetic complement deficiencies, TLR hyperactivation by self nucleic acids, and type I IFN amplification of autoreactive lymphocyte responses."},{"id":"sdl42_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The renal disease in SLE results from which pathogenic mechanism, and what laboratory finding at the time of a flare directly reflects this mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Type II cytotoxic antibodies that bind fixed glomerular basement membrane collagen antigens and activate complement locally, producing linear IgG deposits and fibrinoid necrosis on biopsy","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity in which CD4+ T cells infiltrate the glomerulus and release IFN-gamma, causing tubulointerstitial nephritis without antibody involvement or complement deposition","C":"Type I IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in glomerular capillaries releasing vasoactive mediators that produce transient proteinuria without structural glomerular damage","D":"Direct CD8+ T-cell cytotoxicity against renal tubular epithelial cells that express aberrant MHC class I, causing tubular injury and cast nephropathy without glomerular involvement","E":"Type III immune complex hypersensitivity: DNA-anti-dsDNA complexes deposit in glomeruli and fix complement via the classical pathway, causing nephritis and hypocomplementemia"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity example. DNA-anti-DNA complexes circulate, deposit in the glomerular mesangium and capillary walls, and fix complement via C1q, consuming C3 and C4 and producing hypocomplementemia during active flares. Rising anti-dsDNA and falling C3 and C4 together are the classic flare markers. Type II anti-GBM disease produces linear IF deposits, not granular. Type IV is cell-mediated without complement deposition. Type I is IgE-mast cell mediated. CD8+ tubular cytotoxicity is not the primary mechanism of lupus nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lupus nephritis = Type III hypersensitivity = granular IF pattern + hypocomplementemia + anti-dsDNA. Class IV (diffuse proliferative) is the most severe and common nephritis class. COMLEX: Low C3/C4 during SLE flare is the clinical pearl for immune complex-mediated disease.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Renal biopsy from an SLE patient with proteinuria and RBC casts shows granular deposits of IgG and C3 on immunofluorescence. Which of the following best explains the granular rather than linear deposit pattern?","choices":{"A":"Antibodies bind directly and continuously to fixed glomerular basement membrane antigens along the entire GBM, which creates the granular appearance due to uneven antigen density across the membrane","B":"Circulating immune complexes trap nonuniformly along the glomerulus, producing discontinuous granular deposits consistent with Type III immune complex hypersensitivity rather than antibody binding to a fixed uniform antigen","C":"IgE antibodies attach to Fc epsilon receptors on mesangial cells and activate embedded mast cells that release granule contents, forming discrete granular deposits visible on immunofluorescence","D":"CD4+ T cells infiltrate the glomerulus and deposit cytokine-laden granules at random intervals along the capillary wall, visible as discontinuous granular deposits on immunofluorescence","E":"ANCA-directed antibodies against myeloperoxidase deposit in capillary loops at sites of neutrophil degranulation, producing a scattered granular staining pattern on direct immunofluorescence"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Granular IF staining indicates immune complex deposition (Type III). Complexes form in circulation or in situ and trap at irregular intervals along the glomerular capillary wall and mesangium, creating a discontinuous granular pattern. This is the hallmark of lupus nephritis. In contrast, anti-GBM disease (Goodpasture, Type II) produces linear staining because antibodies bind uniformly along the entire GBM surface of the NC1 domain of type IV collagen. IgE does not produce glomerular IF deposits. T cells produce no visible IF pattern. ANCA-associated GN is characteristically pauci-immune with negative or near-negative IF staining.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Granular IF = immune complex (Type III); Linear IF = anti-GBM (Type II). This distinction is a classic board question. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis Class IV shows full-house granular IF with IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best distinguishes lupus nephritis from anti-GBM disease in terms of the underlying immunopathologic mechanism and its laboratory reflection?","choices":{"A":"Lupus nephritis involves circulating immune complex deposition with granular IF and hypocomplementemia, while anti-GBM disease involves antibodies to a fixed NC1 antigen with linear IF","B":"Lupus nephritis is caused by Type IV hypersensitivity with glomerular CD4+ T cell infiltrates, while anti-GBM disease is a Type III immune complex disorder with granular subendothelial deposits on EM","C":"Lupus nephritis causes nephrotic syndrome exclusively via podocyte injury from subepithelial immune complexes, while anti-GBM disease causes nephritic syndrome via direct complement-mediated GBM destruction","D":"Lupus nephritis requires inherited complement deficiency to initiate glomerulonephritis, while anti-GBM disease develops only in patients with normal complement and HLA-DR2 or DR15 genotype","E":"Lupus nephritis is driven by anti-Sm antibodies depositing within the GBM matrix, while anti-GBM disease is driven by anti-dsDNA antibodies binding podocyte surface proteins"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis (Type III) results from circulating DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes depositing in the glomerulus, causing granular IF staining and complement consumption (low C3, C4). Anti-GBM disease (Type II, Goodpasture) results from antibodies directed against the fixed alpha-3 chain of type IV collagen in the GBM, producing linear IF staining and typically normal complement levels because the antigen is structural and not circulating to consume complement. The other options reverse or misattribute the hypersensitivity types and antibody specificities involved in each disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Goodpasture = linear IgG + normal complement + anti-GBM antibody + HLA-DR2/DR15 association. Lupus nephritis = granular IF + low complement + anti-dsDNA. COMLEX: Both can cause rapidly progressive (crescentic) GN but by different mechanisms.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Lupus nephritis is the canonical Type III hypersensitivity lesion: DNA–anti-DNA immune complexes deposit in glomeruli, fix complement, and drive mesangial and endothelial injury."},{"id":"sdl42_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An SLE patient develops anemia with a positive direct Coombs test and elevated indirect bilirubin. Which mechanism best accounts for the reduction in circulating RBCs?","choices":{"A":"Type III immune complex deposition on red cell membranes activates the terminal complement pathway, driving osmotic intravascular lysis of erythrocytes in the systemic circulation","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity in which macrophage-derived cytokines such as TNF-alpha suppress erythropoiesis in the bone marrow, reducing red cell production without peripheral destruction","C":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation in the bone marrow sinusoids releases histamine that directly damages erythrocyte precursors, reducing reticulocyte output in a Type I reaction","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies binding red cell membrane phospholipids trigger platelet aggregation and mechanical fragmentation of erythrocytes in narrowed microvasculature","E":"Type II antibody-mediated hypersensitivity: autoantibodies coat red cell surface antigens, promoting opsonization and extravascular phagocytic destruction of RBCs by splenic macrophages bearing Fc receptors"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Autoimmune hemolytic anemia in SLE is a Type II (antibody-mediated) cytotoxic hypersensitivity reaction. Warm-reactive IgG autoantibodies coat red cell surface antigens; these antibody-coated cells are recognized by Fc receptors on splenic macrophages and destroyed by extravascular phagocytosis, producing spherocytes, elevated indirect bilirubin, low haptoglobin, and a positive direct Coombs test. Type III immune complexes cause glomerulonephritis and vasculitis, not primarily hemolysis. Type IV is cell-mediated. IgE and mast cells mediate Type I allergy. Antiphospholipid antibodies cause thrombosis via different mechanisms, not mechanical fragmentation hemolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warm AIHA = IgG opsonization = positive direct Coombs = extravascular hemolysis = spherocytes, no schistocytes. SLE is a leading cause of warm AIHA. COMLEX: Direct Coombs positive = antibody on RBC surface; indirect Coombs = free antibody in serum.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"SLE-associated thrombocytopenia is classified under which hypersensitivity type, and what is the primary mechanism of platelet destruction?","choices":{"A":"Type II hypersensitivity: antiplatelet IgG autoantibodies opsonize platelets for phagocytic destruction by the mononuclear phagocyte system, predominantly via Fc receptor-mediated clearance in the spleen","B":"Type III hypersensitivity: circulating immune complexes deposit on platelet membranes and activate the terminal complement pathway, causing osmotic lysis of platelets in the peripheral blood","C":"Type I hypersensitivity: IgE antibodies bound to platelet surface Fc epsilon receptors trigger degranulation and platelet aggregation via cross-linking of adjacent receptor-antibody complexes","D":"Type IV hypersensitivity: sensitized CD8+ T cells release perforin and granzyme B to directly kill platelets that aberrantly upregulate MHC class I presentation of self peptides","E":"Mixed Type II and Type III: antiphospholipid antibodies simultaneously opsonize platelets and form immune complexes that activate the classical complement pathway on the platelet surface"},"correct":"A","explanation":"SLE-associated thrombocytopenia is Type II (antibody-mediated) cytotoxic hypersensitivity, analogous to immune thrombocytopenic purpura (ITP). Autoantibodies against platelet surface glycoproteins (particularly GPIIb/IIIa) coat platelets, which are then phagocytosed by macrophages in the spleen via Fc receptors. This is the same mechanism as RBC destruction in autoimmune hemolytic anemia, applied to platelets. Type III involves immune complex deposition in vessel walls or glomeruli, not the primary platelet destruction mechanism. Type I requires IgE and mast cells. Type IV is cell-mediated without antibody opsonization. Antiphospholipid antibodies can cause thrombocytopenia but through a somewhat different mechanism than direct opsonization.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE causes thrombocytopenia (Type II) AND can cause antiphospholipid syndrome (separate mechanism of platelet consumption via thrombosis). Know which is which. Bone marrow biopsy in immune thrombocytopenia shows megakaryocyte hyperplasia. COMLEX: Splenomegaly in SLE reflects hyperactive splenic phagocytosis of opsonized blood cells.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"An SLE patient has a positive direct Coombs test and hemoglobin of 7.8 g/dL. Her peripheral smear shows spherocytes but no schistocytes. Which finding most confirms that the hemolysis is extravascular rather than intravascular or microangiopathic?","choices":{"A":"Reduced serum C3 and C4 consistent with complement consumption from immune complex deposition directly on red cell membranes driving classical pathway-mediated lysis within the circulation","B":"Elevated indirect bilirubin and undetectable haptoglobin, reflecting splenic phagocytosis of antibody-coated RBCs, with haptoglobin consumed by released hemoglobin","C":"Positive antiphospholipid antibodies with prolonged PTT confirming that phospholipid-reactive antibodies are primarily responsible for coating and lysing red cell membranes","D":"Thrombocytopenia and leukopenia on CBC confirming immune complex deposition targeting all hematopoietic cell lines simultaneously via a Type III mechanism","E":"Normal serum complement with elevated anti-dsDNA titers confirming that anti-dsDNA antibodies cross-react with red cell surface antigens and mediate complement-independent lysis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Extravascular hemolysis (Type II AIHA) produces elevated indirect bilirubin from macrophage catabolism of phagocytosed RBCs and low haptoglobin from binding free hemoglobin released during partial phagocytosis. Spherocytes form when macrophages partially remove RBC membrane during incomplete phagocytosis. Schistocytes are absent because there is no mechanical shear force fragmenting cells. Reduced complement is characteristic of active SLE (Type III) glomerulonephritis, not the mechanism of hemolysis. Antiphospholipid antibodies cause thrombosis, not primary hemolysis. Pancytopenia with low complement suggests global immune activation, not confirmation of extravascular hemolysis mechanism. Normal complement with anti-dsDNA does not diagnose the hemolysis mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Warm AIHA lab triad = elevated indirect bilirubin + low haptoglobin + positive direct Coombs. No schistocytes = extravascular process. COMLEX: Contrast with cold agglutinin disease (IgM + complement; mostly extravascular C3b-mediated clearance, with some intravascular lysis).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Autoantibodies against RBCs, WBCs, and platelets in SLE drive Type II cytotoxic destruction via opsonization and Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen."},{"id":"sdl42_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old female with SLE has two unexplained DVTs and three first-trimester miscarriages. Her PTT is prolonged but her PT is normal. Antiphospholipid antibodies are positive. Which best explains the paradox of in vitro anticoagulation but in vivo thrombosis?","choices":{"A":"Anticardiolipin antibodies activate factor XII of the intrinsic pathway in vivo but inhibit the same factor in vitro because phospholipid micelles in the PTT reagent sequester factor XII away from the antibody","B":"The prolonged PTT reflects true systemic anticoagulation that prevents venous thrombosis but paradoxically allows arterial thrombosis through platelet-dependent pathways independent of the coagulation cascade","C":"Antiphospholipid antibodies compete with clotting factors for phospholipid surfaces in vitro, prolonging PTT, but in vivo they bind beta2-glycoprotein I and directly activate endothelial cells and platelets","D":"Lupus anticoagulant antibodies form immune complexes with factor VIII in the circulation causing acquired hemophilia A, while simultaneously triggering compensatory platelet hyperaggregation in vivo","E":"IgM anticardiolipin antibodies cross-react with von Willebrand factor, prolonging PTT by displacing vWF from the phospholipid surface while stimulating endothelial vWF release and platelet adhesion in vessels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The antiphospholipid syndrome paradox arises from different phospholipid environments in vitro vs in vivo. In vitro PTT assays use exogenous phospholipid reagents; antiphospholipid antibodies compete with clotting factors for these surfaces, slowing clot formation and prolonging PTT. In vivo, these antibodies bind beta2-glycoprotein I and other proteins (protein C, protein S, annexin V, prothrombin), impairing natural anticoagulant pathways and directly activating endothelial cells (upregulating tissue factor and adhesion molecules) and platelets, producing a prothrombotic state. Factor XII is not the relevant target. True systemic anticoagulation does not selectively prevent venous but allow arterial thrombosis. Factor VIII immune complex formation is acquired hemophilia, a different disorder. vWF is not the antiphospholipid target.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antiphospholipid syndrome = thrombosis + pregnancy loss + positive lupus anticoagulant or anticardiolipin antibodies. Prolonged PTT in lupus is from lupus anticoagulant, NOT a bleeding risk. COMLEX: APS is the leading cause of hypercoagulability in young SLE women.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which serologic finding, when persistently detected on repeat testing in an SLE patient with recurrent miscarriages, is most specific for diagnosing secondary antiphospholipid syndrome?","choices":{"A":"High-titer anti-dsDNA with simultaneously reduced C3 and C4, confirming active SLE-driven immune complex production that secondarily creates a hypercoagulable state via complement-mediated endothelial activation","B":"Lupus anticoagulant detected by clotting-based assays or medium-to-high titer IgG or IgM anticardiolipin antibodies, confirmed as persistent on repeat testing weeks apart","C":"ANA titer of 1:640 or higher with homogeneous pattern on HEp-2 immunofluorescence in the context of recurrent first-trimester miscarriages and DVT history","D":"Anti-Smith antibody detected on two consecutive visits with concurrent thrombocytopenia below 100,000 per microliter confirming SLE-driven platelet consumption","E":"False-positive VDRL confirmed by a subsequent positive fluorescent treponemal antibody absorption test in a young female with DVT and pregnancy loss history"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Antiphospholipid syndrome is defined serologically by persistent positivity on repeat testing for: lupus anticoagulant (clotting-based assay), medium-to-high titer IgG or IgM anticardiolipin antibodies, or anti-beta2-glycoprotein I antibodies. The persistence requirement excludes transient positivity from infections or medications. Anti-dsDNA with hypocomplementemia defines SLE flare activity but not APS specifically. ANA titer characterizes SLE, not APS. Anti-Sm is specific for SLE but does not diagnose APS. A positive FTA-ABS would confirm true syphilis and rule out the false-positive seen in APS patients (APS patients have positive VDRL but negative FTA-ABS).","boardPrep":"Step 1: APS criteria require: clinical event (thrombosis or pregnancy morbidity) + persistent laboratory positivity on repeat testing. The three lab criteria are lupus anticoagulant, anticardiolipin IgG or IgM, or anti-beta2-GPI. COMLEX: Recurrent spontaneous miscarriage in a young woman should prompt APS workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient with SLE and antiphospholipid antibodies presents with focal cerebral ischemia and no cardiac source of embolism on workup. Which mechanism most directly accounts for this complication?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies impair the natural anticoagulants protein C, protein S, and annexin V and induce endothelial tissue factor, promoting arterial thrombi that occlude cerebral vessels","B":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies cross-react with neuronal NMDA receptors and cause direct excitotoxic neuronal death in cortical neurons, producing ischemic-appearing lesions without true vascular occlusion","C":"Type III immune complex deposition in cerebral arteriolar walls activates complement-driven fibrinoid necrosis, destroying the vessel wall and producing ischemia via structural vessel disintegration","D":"Noninflammatory intimal proliferation driven by immune complex endothelial injury gradually narrows cerebral small vessel lumens over years, producing watershed zone infarctions without acute thrombosis","E":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis vegetations on the mitral or aortic valve shed sterile fibrin-platelet microemboli that travel to cerebral arterioles and produce occlusive embolic strokes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Arterial thrombosis causing focal cerebral ischemia in SLE with antiphospholipid antibodies is most directly explained by antiphospholipid syndrome: these antibodies bind beta2-GPI and interfere with the function of anticoagulant proteins (protein C, S, annexin V), while also directly activating endothelial cells to express tissue factor and adhesion molecules and activating platelets, producing arterial thromboembolism. Anti-NMDA receptor antibodies may contribute to neuropsychiatric manifestations via direct neurotoxicity rather than ischemia. Type III immune complex fibrinoid necrosis is SLE vasculitis, not the APS thrombosis mechanism. Noninflammatory intimal proliferation is a morphologic SLE finding but not the primary explanation here given the APS context. Libman-Sacks can embolize but the specific APS antibody profile points to thrombotic APS as the mechanism.","boardPrep":"Step 1: The SLE-APS triad for focal neurologic deficits: young female + antiphospholipid antibodies + arterial thrombosis without cardiac source. COMLEX: APS causes ocular ischemia (amaurosis fugax), cerebral ischemia, and DVT-PE in the same patient.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 3 — Antiphospholipid antibodies including anticardiolipin, anti-beta2-GPI, and lupus anticoagulant define secondary antiphospholipid syndrome and produce a paradoxical hypercoagulable state despite prolonging PTT in vitro."},{"id":"sdl42_q13","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female meets 6 ACR criteria for SLE including nonerosive synovitis. Which feature of her joint disease most definitively distinguishes SLE arthritis from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"The presence of anti-CCP antibodies at low titer in her serum, which confirms early seropositive rheumatoid arthritis coexisting with SLE as a recognized overlap connective tissue syndrome","B":"The absence of rheumatoid factor in her serum, which confirms that no B-cell autoimmune activation is occurring in the joint synovium and therefore excludes rheumatoid pathology","C":"The nonerosive synovitis of SLE: despite persistent inflammation and effusions, SLE arthritis characteristically does not erode cartilage or subchondral bone, unlike the destructive pannus of RA","D":"The symmetric small joint distribution involving wrists and MCPs, which is pathognomonic for SLE arthritis and does not occur in rheumatoid arthritis which characteristically spares the MCPs","E":"The presence of anti-dsDNA antibodies that cross-react with cartilage surface proteoglycans and produce inflammation without the destructive enzymatic cascade activated by RF-containing immune complexes in RA"},"correct":"C","explanation":"SLE arthritis is specifically defined as nonerosive synovitis in the ACR classification criteria. Unlike RA, where synovial pannus invades and erodes cartilage and bone over time, SLE arthritis produces tenderness, swelling, and effusions without radiographic erosions. This nonerosive characteristic is clinically and radiographically distinguishable. Anti-CCP antibodies are highly specific for RA and are rarely present in SLE. Rheumatoid factor can be positive in SLE (course SDL 44), so its absence does not definitively exclude RA overlap. Symmetric small joint involvement occurs in both RA and SLE. Anti-dsDNA antibodies are not known to cross-react with cartilage proteoglycans.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE arthritis = nonerosive; RA = erosive pannus. On board radiographs, SLE joints look normal or show subluxation (Jaccoud arthropathy) without erosions. COMLEX: SLE joint disease causes Jaccoud arthropathy due to ligamentous laxity, not cartilage destruction.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q14","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of laboratory findings is most characteristic of an active SLE flare with renal involvement?","choices":{"A":"Elevated ESR, elevated CRP, elevated serum C3 and C4, and high-titer RF confirming systemic inflammation with intact complement and an additional rheumatoid disease component","B":"Positive ANA with fine speckled pattern, normal complement levels, normal CBC, and trace proteinuria confirming low-grade serologically active SLE without clinical end-organ manifestations","C":"High-titer anti-Sm antibody, normal renal function, normal urinalysis, and only lymphopenia on CBC confirming serologic disease activity without current end-organ involvement","D":"High-titer anti-dsDNA antibodies, low C3 and C4, hematuria with RBC casts, and thrombocytopenia, confirming active lupus nephritis with immune complex-driven complement consumption","E":"Positive lupus anticoagulant, prolonged PTT, elevated serum creatinine, and anti-centromere antibody positivity confirming antiphospholipid nephropathy rather than immune complex glomerulonephritis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Active lupus nephritis produces a classic constellation: rising anti-dsDNA titer (most reliable flare activity marker), hypocomplementemia with low C3 and C4 from complement consumption, active urinary sediment with hematuria and RBC casts confirming glomerulonephritis, and thrombocytopenia from concurrent Type II autoantibody-mediated platelet destruction. CRP is characteristically low in SLE flares (unlike bacterial infection where CRP rises); elevated CRP in SLE should prompt workup for infection. Fine speckled ANA with normal complement is nonspecific. Anti-Sm is specific for SLE but does not correlate with disease activity. Anti-centromere is associated with limited systemic sclerosis, not SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE flare markers = rising anti-dsDNA + falling complement (C3, C4). CRP is usually low or normal in SLE flares; high CRP suggests superimposed infection. COMLEX: Monitor SLE with serial anti-dsDNA, C3, C4, and urinalysis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q15","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis in SLE is best distinguished from infective endocarditis and rheumatic heart disease by which morphologic characteristic?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are confined exclusively to the lines of closure of valve leaflets, identical in distribution to rheumatic vegetations but distinguished by their larger size and irregular surface texture","B":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis produces large friable vegetations on a single valve that shed emboli to the systemic circulation more frequently than infective endocarditis due to their loose fibrin-platelet composition","C":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are detectable only on transesophageal echocardiography and are composed of calcified fibrin that distinguishes them from the non-calcified plaques of rheumatic endocarditis","D":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis involves exclusively right-sided heart valves reflecting immune complex deposition preferentially in low-pressure chambers and is the only form of endocarditis affecting the tricuspid valve","E":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are small (1 to 3 mm) nonbacterial warty deposits that can occur on either surface of any valve leaflet, unlike the larger infective vegetations and closure-line rheumatic deposits"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is characterized by 1 to 3 mm warty nonbacterial deposits that can appear on either surface (atrial or ventricular) of the leaflets of any heart valve. This distinguishes it from infective endocarditis (much larger, typically single-surface vegetations, with signs of systemic infection) and from rheumatic endocarditis (small beads limited to the lines of closure of valve leaflets). Libman-Sacks is not confined to closure lines. Vegetations are not consistently large and friable. They are visible on standard echocardiography and are not calcified. They can affect any valve, not only right-sided valves.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Libman-Sacks = nonbacterial verrucous endocarditis = small vegetations on either leaflet surface of any valve = associated with SLE and antiphospholipid syndrome. COMLEX: Negative blood cultures + valvular vegetations in SLE patient = Libman-Sacks, not infective endocarditis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — The broad clinical spectrum of SLE including hypocomplementemia during flares, cytopenias on CBC, and multi-organ manifestations reflects the systemic impact of immune complex deposition and autoantibody production."},{"id":"sdl42_q16","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 22-year-old woman presents with 4 months of fatigue, low-grade fevers, wrist and knee swelling, and a facial rash that worsens with sun exposure. Her mother has Hashimoto thyroiditis. Labs: ANA 1:1280 homogeneous, anti-dsDNA positive, C3 low, lymphopenia 950 cells/mm3, serum IFN-alpha markedly elevated. The high IFN-alpha most likely reflects activation of which upstream pathway?","choices":{"A":"CD8+ cytotoxic T cells lysing self-reactive plasma cells and releasing granule contents that secondarily stimulate plasmacytoid DCs to produce IFN-alpha in a cytotoxicity-triggered innate response","B":"IgE-mediated mast cell degranulation at dermoepidermal junctions releasing vasoactive mediators that secondarily activate plasmacytoid dendritic cells to produce type I interferons","C":"TLR stimulation of plasmacytoid dendritic cells by self nucleic acids in apoptotic debris and immune complexes, driving IFN-alpha production that amplifies B-cell autoantibody responses","D":"NK cell recognition of MHC class I-deficient autoreactive B cells in lymph nodes that releases IFN-gamma cross-activating plasmacytoid DCs to switch from IFN-gamma to IFN-alpha production","E":"Complement-mediated opsonization of nuclear debris by C1q that directs apoptotic material to plasmacytoid DCs, which then produce IFN-alpha in a strictly complement-dependent manner"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The type I IFN signature in SLE is primarily driven by plasmacytoid DCs (pDCs) bearing TLR7 and TLR9. When nuclear DNA and RNA in immune complexes or apoptotic debris engage these TLRs, pDCs produce large amounts of IFN-alpha. This IFN-alpha further activates B cells, promotes autoreactive T-cell responses, and induces apoptosis in other cells, creating a self-amplifying loop that correlates with SLE disease activity. CD8+ T cells are not the initiating trigger for pDC IFN-alpha production. IgE-mast cell degranulation is Type I allergy. NK cell IFN-gamma does not cross-activate pDCs to produce IFN-alpha. C1q deficiency promotes SLE but C1q-dependent opsonization is not the trigger for IFN-alpha production.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Type I IFN signature is a biomarker of SLE activity. TLR7 recognizes single-stranded RNA; TLR9 recognizes unmethylated CpG DNA — both can be engaged by self nucleic acids in SLE. COMLEX: Belimumab targets BAFF/BLyS; anifrolumab targets IFNAR (type I IFN receptor) for refractory SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q17","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old woman with SLE has worsening symptoms after 6 months off medications. She works as a lifeguard and notes UV-triggered flares. Her twin sister was recently diagnosed with SLE. Genetic testing reveals HLA-DQ alleles associated with anti-Sm production and a heterozygous C4 null allele. Which statement best characterizes the role of the C4 deficiency in her SLE susceptibility?","choices":{"A":"C4 deficiency prevents activation of the classical complement pathway, resulting in unopposed alternative pathway activation that drives chronic low-grade vessel inflammation mimicking type III vasculitis","B":"C4 deficiency causes impaired opsonization of encapsulated bacteria, leading to recurrent pyogenic infections whose molecular mimics trigger anti-dsDNA production via cross-reactive epitopes with nuclear antigens","C":"C4 deficiency reduces total serum immunoglobulin production because complement-dependent B-cell co-receptor signaling through CD21-CR2 is impaired in peripheral lymph nodes during germinal center reactions","D":"C4 deficiency increases TLR7 and TLR9 surface expression on autoreactive B cells by impairing intracellular trafficking of complement-receptor signaling complexes, lowering the threshold for nuclear antigen-driven activation","E":"C4 deficiency impairs clearance of immune complexes and apoptotic debris via complement receptors CR1 and CR2, allowing nuclear autoantigens to persist and sustain autoreactive B-cell activation and anti-dsDNA production"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Inherited C4 deficiency (particularly the C4A null allele) promotes SLE via two related mechanisms: impaired clearance of circulating immune complexes through complement receptors (CR1, CR2), allowing deposits to accumulate in tissues, and impaired phagocytic clearance of apoptotic cells (normally requiring C1q and downstream complement activation to opsonize apoptotic debris). The resulting accumulation of nuclear autoantigen sustains B-cell autoreactivity and anti-dsDNA production. C4 deficiency does not preferentially activate the alternative pathway. Recurrent bacterial infection from isolated C4 deficiency is mild because the alternative and lectin pathways remain intact. C4 does not regulate total immunoglobulin synthesis. TLR expression is not regulated by C4-dependent complement receptor signaling.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Early complement deficiencies (C1q, C4, C2) predispose to SLE by impairing immune complex and apoptotic debris clearance. C4A null allele is the most common genetic complement risk factor for SLE. COMLEX: Recurrent Neisseria infections point to terminal complement deficiency (C5-C9), not C4 deficiency.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q18","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Understand the genetic, immunologic, and environmental contributions to the predisposition and possible causes of systemic lupus erythematosus.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old male with drug-induced lupus from procainamide has anti-histone antibodies but no anti-dsDNA or anti-Sm. Symptoms resolve 6 weeks after stopping the drug. Compared to idiopathic SLE, drug-induced lupus is least likely to cause which feature, and why?","choices":{"A":"Arthritis and arthralgias are unlikely in drug-induced lupus because procainamide-modified histones selectively activate T cells with a skin-homing phenotype rather than joint-specific synovial T cells","B":"Photosensitivity is absent in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone antibodies do not undergo class switching to IgE, which is required for UV-triggered mast cell degranulation in dermal vasculature","C":"Thrombocytopenia is rare in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone antibodies cannot cross-react with platelet surface glycoproteins in the manner that anti-dsDNA antibodies opsonize platelets","D":"Severe nephritis and CNS disease are uncommon in drug-induced lupus because anti-histone immune complexes are less nephritogenic than anti-dsDNA complexes and resolve when the drug is withdrawn","E":"Hypocomplementemia does not occur in drug-induced lupus because histone-reactive immune complexes are too large to pass through glomerular fenestrations and therefore do not activate classical pathway complement"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Drug-induced lupus (procainamide, hydralazine, D-penicillamine) is characterized by anti-histone antibodies and typically lacks the highly pathogenic anti-dsDNA antibodies of idiopathic SLE. Anti-histone complexes are less complement-fixing and less persistently produced; when the causative drug is withdrawn, antigen disappears and immune complex formation resolves, explaining the mild and self-limited course without severe nephritis or CNS disease. Arthritis is common in drug-induced lupus. Photosensitivity can occur through complement-independent mechanisms. Thrombocytopenia is not specifically prevented by anti-histone antibody specificity. Hypocomplementemia can occur in drug-induced lupus, though it is milder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Drug-induced lupus mnemonic = HIPP (Hydralazine, Isoniazid, Procainamide, Phenytoin) = anti-histone antibodies = no nephritis = resolves with drug discontinuation. COMLEX: First step in drug-induced lupus management is discontinuing the offending drug.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl42_q19","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old woman with SLE has edema, hypertension, creatinine 2.1 mg/dL, urine protein 3.8 g/24 hours, RBC casts, C3 11 mg/dL (normal 88-201), C4 4 mg/dL (normal 16-47), and anti-dsDNA 1:640. Biopsy shows diffuse proliferative GN with subendothelial deposits on EM. Which IF finding is most consistent with this diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Granular deposits of IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q (full-house pattern) along the capillary loops and mesangium, consistent with immune complex deposition in class IV lupus nephritis","B":"Linear IgG deposits along the glomerular basement membrane with no C3 or IgM, consistent with antibodies directed against the NC1 domain of type IV collagen in anti-GBM disease","C":"Negative immunofluorescence with segmental fibrinoid necrosis and crescents on light microscopy, consistent with pauci-immune glomerulonephritis from ANCA-associated vasculitis","D":"Coarse granular IgG and C3 deposits limited to the subepithelial surface of the outer GBM with no mesangial deposits, consistent with membranous nephropathy from anti-PLA2R autoantibodies","E":"Mesangial IgA deposits with C3 and rare IgG in a mesangial-predominant pattern without capillary loop deposits, consistent with IgA nephropathy from mucosal antigen deposition"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Lupus nephritis class III or IV shows the full-house immunofluorescence pattern: IgG, IgM, IgA, C3, and C1q all positive in the mesangium and along capillary loops in a granular pattern. This full-house positivity is essentially pathognomonic for lupus nephritis and reflects the broad autoantibody repertoire in SLE targeting multiple nuclear antigens. Linear IgG is the pattern of anti-GBM disease. Pauci-immune negative IF defines ANCA-associated GN. Subepithelial granular IgG with anti-PLA2R characterizes idiopathic membranous nephropathy. Mesangial IgA with C3 is IgA nephropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Full-house IF (IgG+IgM+IgA+C3+C1q) is pathognomonic for lupus nephritis. Class IV (diffuse proliferative) = most severe, most common symptomatic class, wire-loop lesions on LM, subendothelial deposits on EM. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis is the most common cause of death in young women with SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q20","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old woman with class IV lupus nephritis treated for 8 months shows normalizing C3 and C4 and falling anti-dsDNA titer from 1:640 to 1:40. Which mechanism best explains why complement levels serve as a surrogate marker of lupus nephritis activity?","choices":{"A":"Active lupus nephritis exclusively activates the alternative complement pathway, depleting circulating C3 while leaving C4 normal because C4 is only consumed by the classical and lectin pathways","B":"The lectin pathway is activated in lupus nephritis via mannose-binding lectin binding to glomerular glycoproteins, consuming C4 specifically while leaving C3 relatively spared compared to classical pathway activation","C":"High levels of anti-dsDNA antibodies block the C3 convertase of the classical pathway by competing for C3b binding sites, reducing C3 levels via a competitive exclusion mechanism independent of actual complex deposition","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies in SLE directly inhibit C1q from binding immune complexes in the glomerulus, consuming C4 before C3 and leaving C3 disproportionately low relative to C4 during active nephritis","E":"DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes activate the classical complement pathway via C1q, consuming C4 and then C3, so hypocomplementemia during flares reflects consumption exceeding hepatic complement synthesis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Lupus nephritis complement consumption follows the classical pathway: DNA-anti-DNA complexes are recognized by C1q, which cleaves C4 and C2 to form C3 convertase, then cleaves C3. During active disease, immune complex formation and classical pathway activation exceed hepatic complement synthesis, producing hypocomplementemia with low C3 and C4. As treatment reduces immune complex burden, complement levels recover. This is why serial C3 and C4 levels serve as activity markers alongside anti-dsDNA titer. The alternative pathway does not explain C4 depletion. The lectin pathway is not the primary driver of lupus nephritis complement consumption. Anti-dsDNA does not block C3 convertase. Antiphospholipid antibodies do not inhibit C1q.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Classical pathway sequence = C1q-C1r-C1s cleaves C4 and C2 then C3. Lupus nephritis consumes both C4 and C3 (classical). Membranoproliferative GN type II consumes C3 but not C4 (alternative pathway C3 nephritic factor). COMLEX: Rising C3 and C4 = effective immunosuppressive therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q21","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the type of hypersensitivity responsible for lupus renal disease.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old SLE patient's biopsy shows wire-loop thickening of glomerular capillary loops on light microscopy, full-house IF staining, and massive subendothelial immune complex deposits on EM. Which of the following best explains the wire-loop lesion?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies binding endothelial cell phospholipid membranes cause endothelial swelling that compresses the capillary lumen and creates a wire-loop appearance on silver staining","B":"Fibrinoid necrosis of capillary loops driven by macrophage-mediated Type IV granulomatous inflammation deposits collagen along the GBM, producing the thickened wire-loop appearance on PAS staining","C":"Massive subendothelial immune complex deposits between the endothelial cell and the GBM expand the capillary wall into a thickened rigid loop, the classic wire-loop lesion of Class IV lupus nephritis","D":"Mesangial cell hyperplasia and matrix expansion extending into capillary loops creates a double-contour pattern on silver staining that is commonly misidentified as the wire-loop lesion in clinical practice","E":"Complement membrane attack complex (C5b-9) insertion into the GBM causes focal splitting of the lamina densa and creates a wire-loop appearance on periodic acid-Schiff staining in severe Class IV disease"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The wire-loop lesion is the classic light microscopy finding in Class IV (diffuse proliferative) lupus nephritis. It results from massive subendothelial immune complex deposits composed of DNA-anti-DNA complexes and complement that accumulate between the endothelial cell and the glomerular basement membrane. These large deposits push the capillary wall outward, creating the characteristic thick rigid loop appearance on PAS or silver staining. It is not produced by antiphospholipid antibodies on endothelial surfaces, granulomatous fibrinoid necrosis, mesangial expansion (which causes double-contour in MPGN), or membrane attack complex insertion into the GBM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Wire-loop lesion = Class IV lupus nephritis = massive subendothelial deposits = EM shows electron-dense subendothelial deposits. Contrast with Class V (membranous lupus) which shows subepithelial deposits and spike-and-dome pattern. COMLEX: Class IV has worst prognosis and requires aggressive treatment with mycophenolate mofetil or cyclophosphamide.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q22","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 27-year-old female with SLE has hemoglobin 7.8 g/dL, elevated reticulocyte count of 4.2%, positive direct Coombs for IgG, indirect bilirubin 3.1 mg/dL, and undetectable haptoglobin. Her peripheral smear shows spherocytes but no schistocytes. Which best explains the absence of schistocytes?","choices":{"A":"The SLE autoantibodies target red cell ABO surface antigens rather than structural membrane proteins, causing osmotic lysis in the plasma without mechanical fragmentation or passage through splenic cords","B":"The hemolysis is predominantly extravascular: splenic macrophages partially engulf antibody-coated RBCs and remodel them into spherocytes without fragmenting them, unlike microangiopathic shear in damaged vessels","C":"The positive direct Coombs reflects cold agglutinins of the IgM class that agglutinate RBCs at peripheral temperatures but dissociate at body temperature without causing membrane fragmentation","D":"Complement activation via membrane attack complex inserts into RBC membranes and lyses them osmotically in the systemic circulation, which produces spherocytic ghosts rather than fragmented schistocyte forms","E":"The elevated reticulocyte count reflects vigorous compensatory erythropoiesis that maintains RBC deformability, preventing fragmentation because reticulocytes are more flexible than mature RBCs when traversing the microvasculature"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Warm AIHA produces extravascular hemolysis: IgG autoantibodies coat RBCs, which are then partially engulfed by splenic macrophages via Fc receptors. During partial phagocytosis, pieces of the RBC membrane are removed, reducing surface area relative to volume and converting the biconcave disc to a spherocyte. Since this process occurs in the spleen rather than in vessels with mechanical shear forces (fibrin strands in TTP or HUS, prosthetic valve turbulence), there is no fragmentation and no schistocytes. ABO antigens are not the primary targets of warm AIHA. Cold agglutinins are IgM, not IgG, and produce a different Coombs pattern. Direct MAC lysis in the circulation is not the mechanism of warm IgG AIHA. Reticulocyte flexibility does not prevent fragmentation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Schistocytes = microangiopathic hemolytic anemia (TTP, HUS, DIC, valve hemolysis). Spherocytes without schistocytes = immune (warm AIHA) or hereditary spherocytosis. Distinguish by Coombs test. COMLEX: In SLE, look for both warm AIHA (Type II) and thrombocytopenia (Type II) simultaneously.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q23","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old female with new SLE has a platelet count of 48,000 per microliter, normal PT and PTT, and bone marrow biopsy showing megakaryocyte hyperplasia. Anti-platelet IgG is detected. High-dose corticosteroids improve her count to 112,000 within 3 weeks. Which mechanism best explains the corticosteroid response?","choices":{"A":"Corticosteroids directly stimulate thrombopoietin synthesis in the liver, increasing platelet production by megakaryocytes in the bone marrow to compensate for ongoing peripheral immune-mediated destruction","B":"Corticosteroids block TLR7 and TLR9 signaling on B cells, suppressing the nuclear antigen-driven co-stimulatory pathway that specifically produces anti-platelet glycoprotein autoantibodies in SLE","C":"Corticosteroids activate splenic regulatory T cells to produce IL-10, which directly inhibits autoreactive B-cell clones producing antiplatelet antibodies via STAT3-dependent suppression","D":"Corticosteroids downregulate Fc receptor expression on splenic macrophages, reducing phagocytic destruction of antibody-coated platelets, while broadly suppressing B-cell autoantibody production","E":"Corticosteroids stabilize platelet phospholipid membranes by intercalating into the bilayer, preventing complement-dependent MAC formation and osmotic platelet lysis by antiplatelet antibodies"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Corticosteroids improve immune thrombocytopenia via two complementary mechanisms: reducing Fc receptor expression on splenic macrophages (decreasing recognition and phagocytosis of antibody-coated platelets) and broadly suppressing B-cell activity and autoantibody production over time. Megakaryocyte hyperplasia confirms that platelet production is intact and peripheral destruction is the mechanism, making the splenic Fc receptor effect the most immediate therapeutic target. Corticosteroids do not stimulate thrombopoietin. TLR blockade is not the primary steroid mechanism. Treg-IL-10 activation may contribute secondarily but is not the primary splenic mechanism explaining rapid response. Corticosteroids do not intercalate into platelet membranes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Corticosteroids work in ITP and SLE thrombocytopenia by reducing splenic Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis. For refractory cases: IVIG (blocks Fc receptors acutely), splenectomy, rituximab, or thrombopoietin receptor agonists. COMLEX: Megakaryocyte hyperplasia on bone marrow biopsy = increased production trying to compensate for peripheral destruction.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q24","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the type of hypersensitivity responsible for blood cell destruction in SLE (RBC, WBC, Platelets).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 30-year-old female with SLE has WBC 2,800 per microliter with 750 lymphocytes per microliter. Anti-lymphocyte antibodies are detected. Her physician explains this is the same hypersensitivity mechanism as her prior autoimmune hemolytic anemia. Which mechanism best accounts for the lymphopenia?","choices":{"A":"Type III immune complex deposition in bone marrow sinusoids suppresses hematopoietic stem cell proliferation, reducing lymphocyte output and producing lymphopenia without peripheral destruction of mature lymphocytes","B":"Type IV delayed hypersensitivity: sensitized CD8+ T cells recognize autologous lymphocytes expressing aberrant MHC class II presentation of self peptides and destroy them via perforin-granzyme cytotoxic pathways","C":"Type II antibody-mediated cytotoxicity: anti-lymphocyte IgG autoantibodies opsonize circulating lymphocytes for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytosis in the spleen and liver, as in autoimmune hemolytic anemia","D":"Type I hypersensitivity: IgE anti-lymphocyte antibodies bind Fc epsilon receptors on lymphocytes and trigger complement-dependent osmotic lysis of the antibody-bound cells in peripheral blood","E":"Direct retroviral cytopathic effect by an endogenous retroviral element activated by SLE immune dysregulation that preferentially infects and kills T cells in the peripheral blood without antibody involvement"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-lymphocyte antibodies in SLE are IgG autoantibodies that opsonize circulating T and B lymphocytes for Fc receptor-mediated phagocytic destruction in the spleen and liver — the exact same Type II antibody-mediated mechanism as autoimmune hemolytic anemia, applied to a different cellular target. The ACR criteria include leukopenia (less than 4,000 per mm3) and lymphopenia (less than 1,500 per mm3) as hematologic manifestations of SLE. Type III immune complex bone marrow suppression does not explain peripheral lymphocyte destruction. Type IV CD8+ cytotoxicity requires antigen-specific recognition, not the nonspecific anti-lymphocyte antibody mechanism. IgE is not the isotype of SLE autoantibodies. Endogenous retroviral elements are proposed SLE triggers but are not the mechanism of ongoing lymphopenia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE hematologic criteria include anemia (warm AIHA), thrombocytopenia, leukopenia, and lymphopenia, all via Type II antibody-mediated mechanisms. COMLEX: SLE patients are immunocompromised from both the disease itself (lymphopenia, complement depletion) and from immunosuppressive treatment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl42_q25","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 32-year-old female with SLE has three prior miscarriages, two DVTs, and amaurosis fugax. PTT is 68 seconds, PT is normal. Anticardiolipin IgG is positive on two occasions 14 weeks apart. Her rheumatologist orders anti-beta2-GPI testing. Why is anti-beta2-GPI the most pathogenically relevant antibody target in this syndrome?","choices":{"A":"Beta2-GPI is a lipid transfer protein that delivers phospholipids to platelet membranes; its inhibition by autoantibodies directly destabilizes platelet lipid bilayers and causes thrombocytopenia as the primary APS manifestation","B":"Beta2-GPI is a cofactor for thrombomodulin on endothelial cells; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies block thrombomodulin function, preventing thrombin-mediated protein C activation and creating acquired activated protein C resistance","C":"Beta2-GPI is the dominant complement-fixing antigen in SLE; anti-beta2-GPI immune complexes deposit in vessel walls and activate the classical complement pathway, mimicking Type III vasculitis to cause arterial stenosis","D":"Beta2-GPI binds phospholipid membranes and normally promotes anticoagulation; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies interfere with this anticoagulant function, inhibit annexin V, and directly activate endothelial cells and platelets through Fc and apoER2 receptors to create a prothrombotic state","E":"Beta2-GPI is expressed on trophoblast surfaces during early pregnancy; anti-beta2-GPI directly activates the trophoblast intrinsic apoptosis pathway via caspase-9, causing placental insufficiency entirely independent of uteroplacental thrombosis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Beta2-glycoprotein I (apolipoprotein H) is a plasma protein that binds phospholipid membranes and has anticoagulant properties, including displacing prothrombin from phospholipid surfaces and supporting annexin V anticoagulant shield formation. Anti-beta2-GPI antibodies interfere with these anticoagulant functions and also directly activate endothelial cells (via TLR4 and apoER2 receptors) to upregulate tissue factor and adhesion molecules, and activate platelets, producing a net prothrombotic state. This explains the PTT prolongation in vitro (antibodies compete with clotting factors for phospholipid surfaces) combined with thrombosis in vivo. Beta2-GPI is not a lipid transfer protein. It does not function through thrombomodulin. It is not the primary complement-fixing antigen. Trophoblast apoptosis may occur but uteroplacental thrombosis is the primary mechanism of pregnancy loss.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Beta2-GPI = the true antigenic target in APS. Anticardiolipin antibodies bind cardiolipin-beta2-GPI complexes. Anti-beta2-GPI is the most specific test for true APS. COMLEX: APS = most common acquired cause of hypercoagulability; treat with anticoagulation (warfarin for thrombotic APS, heparin during pregnancy).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q26","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old female with SLE and secondary APS is 10 weeks pregnant with three prior first-trimester losses. She is started on low-molecular-weight heparin. Which best explains why heparin is preferred over warfarin during early pregnancy?","choices":{"A":"Heparin enhances anti-beta2-GPI antibody clearance through the reticuloendothelial system, reducing antiphospholipid antibody titers more effectively than warfarin during the critical first-trimester implantation period","B":"Warfarin crosses the placenta and is teratogenic in the first trimester, causing fetal warfarin syndrome (nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses), whereas heparin is too large to cross the placenta and is safe for the fetus","C":"Warfarin is ineffective in antiphospholipid syndrome because it inhibits protein C and protein S, which are already functionally blocked by antiphospholipid antibodies, making the combined anticoagulant effect paradoxically procoagulant","D":"Heparin has direct anti-complement activity at the C3 level that neutralizes complement-mediated trophoblast destruction independent of its anticoagulant mechanism, providing protection that warfarin cannot replicate","E":"Heparin activates tissue plasminogen activator at the uteroplacental interface, dissolving fibrin microthrombi in spiral arteries before they progress to complete vessel occlusion and placental infarction"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Heparin is the anticoagulant of choice during pregnancy, especially the first trimester, because it is a large, negatively charged molecule that does not cross the placental barrier and is therefore not teratogenic. Warfarin crosses the placenta freely and in the first trimester can inhibit vitamin K-dependent gamma-carboxylation of proteins involved in bone and cartilage development, causing fetal warfarin syndrome: nasal hypoplasia, stippled epiphyses (chondrodysplasia punctata), and intrauterine growth restriction. In APS pregnancy, LMWH with low-dose aspirin is continued throughout gestation. Heparin does not reduce antiphospholipid antibody titers. Warfarin inhibiting protein C and S is a transient concern at initiation but is not the reason for preferring heparin in pregnancy. Anti-complement effects of heparin are not the primary clinical rationale. tPA activation is not the mechanism of heparin action.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LMWH is safe in pregnancy (does not cross placenta); warfarin is teratogenic in the first trimester. For APS in pregnancy: LMWH + low-dose aspirin throughout pregnancy. COMLEX: Recurrent pregnancy loss workup includes antiphospholipid antibody panel as first-line serologic testing.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q27","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Recognize the antibodies and clinical manifestations in lupus patients with hypercoagulable outcomes (DVT, arterial thrombosis, recurrent spontaneous miscarriages, focal cerebral or ocular ischemia).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old female with SLE has a positive lupus anticoagulant screen and a reactive VDRL. A confirmatory FTA-ABS test returns negative. She has no history of thrombosis or STIs. Which best explains the false-positive VDRL in this patient?","choices":{"A":"The VDRL detects IgG against treponemal lipoprotein; anti-beta2-GPI antibodies bind the lipoprotein carrier in the VDRL antigen, producing a false positive that is confirmed as specific by the FTA-ABS cross-reactivity","B":"The lupus anticoagulant cross-reacts with recombinant Treponema pallidum proteins used in both the VDRL and FTA-ABS assays, making both tests reactive in all patients with confirmed antiphospholipid syndrome","C":"Antiphospholipid antibodies in SLE cross-react with cardiolipin in the VDRL antigen, giving a false-positive nontreponemal result, while the FTA-ABS stays negative because SLE antibodies do not bind treponemal antigens","D":"Anti-dsDNA antibodies in SLE cross-react with VDRL cardiolipin because both antigens share a negatively charged phosphate backbone, producing false-positive VDRL but negative FTA-ABS confirming no actual syphilis","E":"The prolonged PTT from lupus anticoagulant physically interferes with the flocculation reaction in the VDRL assay, producing a false-positive result that normalizes when serum is diluted to correct for the PTT abnormality"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The VDRL and RPR use a cardiolipin-cholesterol-lecithin antigen to detect reagin antibodies as a screen for syphilis. In SLE and APS, antiphospholipid antibodies (particularly anticardiolipin antibodies) cross-react with the cardiolipin in the VDRL antigen, producing a false-positive nontreponemal test. The FTA-ABS uses actual Treponema pallidum organisms as antigen; SLE and APS antibodies do not bind these treponemal antigens, so the FTA-ABS is negative, confirming the VDRL positivity is false (syphilis is excluded). A negative FTA-ABS with positive VDRL is the classic APS serologic profile. Lupus anticoagulant does not cross-react with treponemal proteins in FTA-ABS. Anti-beta2-GPI binds phospholipid complexes, not treponemal lipoproteins. Anti-dsDNA cross-reactivity with VDRL via phosphate backbone is not the mechanism. PTT does not interfere with flocculation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APS/SLE false-positive VDRL: positive nontreponemal (VDRL/RPR) + negative treponemal (FTA-ABS/TPPA) = false positive due to anticardiolipin antibodies. True syphilis: both positive. COMLEX: False-positive VDRL is an ACR classification criterion for SLE.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 3 — Autoantibodies in Rheumatic Disease Associated with Specific Conditions"},{"id":"sdl42_q28","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 26-year-old African American female presents with fatigue, alopecia, oral ulcers, malar rash, bilateral pleuritis, confusion, WBC 2,400, hemoglobin 9.2 g/dL, platelets 88,000, proteinuria, hematuria with casts, and ANA 1:2560. She meets multiple ACR classification criteria. Which best characterizes why SLE presents so heterogeneously across patients?","choices":{"A":"SLE exclusively involves organs that express double-stranded DNA on their cell surfaces, and the breadth of involvement reflects the ubiquity of dsDNA as a plasma membrane antigen across all body systems","B":"SLE follows a predictable sequential pattern of organ involvement proceeding from skin to joints to kidney to CNS, with new systems affected only after prior systems have responded to initial treatment","C":"SLE causes multisystem involvement primarily because anti-Sm antibodies bind RNA splicing machinery in all actively transcribing cells, globally inhibiting protein synthesis in tissues with the highest mRNA turnover","D":"SLE organ involvement is limited to structures expressing Ro/SS-A and La/SS-B antigens, which explains why the same organs affected in Sjogren syndrome are affected in SLE with autoantibody overlap","E":"SLE causes multisystem disease because immune complex deposition, autoantibodies against diverse cellular targets, and complement activation can injure nearly any organ, varying by autoantibody profile"},"correct":"E","explanation":"SLE is the prototypic multisystem autoimmune disease because its pathogenic mechanisms — immune complex deposition in small vessels (Type III), autoantibody binding to diverse cell surface and intracellular antigens (Type II), and complement activation — can affect virtually any organ or tissue. The specific organs involved in a given patient depend on their autoantibody repertoire (anti-dsDNA correlates with nephritis, antiphospholipid antibodies with thrombosis, anti-Ro with neonatal lupus), genetic background (HLA alleles, complement gene polymorphisms), and environmental exposures (UV light). This inherent mechanistic and genetic heterogeneity makes SLE both the great imitator and highly variable in presentation. dsDNA is not expressed on cell surfaces. Organ involvement does not follow a fixed sequence. Anti-Sm does not inhibit global protein synthesis. Ro/SS-A and La/SS-B positivity is not required for organ involvement in SLE.","boardPrep":"Step 1: SLE = great imitator; must be in the differential for any young woman with multisystem disease. 95-100% ANA positive (sensitive but not specific); anti-dsDNA and anti-Sm are specific for SLE. COMLEX: SLE is more prevalent and severe in African American, Hispanic, and Asian women than in European ancestry women.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl42_q29","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old SLE patient on hydroxychloroquine has her anti-dsDNA titer rise from 1:40 to 1:640 and C3 fall from 97 to 52 mg/dL over 3 months, but remains asymptomatic with normal urinalysis. Two months later she returns with proteinuria, edema, and hematuria. Which trend best predicted the renal flare?","choices":{"A":"The rise in total ANA titer from 1:320 to 1:640 directly reflects the degree of complement activation and accurately predicts the magnitude of glomerular immune complex deposition during flares","B":"The reduction in serum C3 alone is the most sensitive single serologic predictor of SLE nephritis because complement depletion always precedes glomerular injury by exactly 8 to 12 weeks in Type III hypersensitivity","C":"Rising anti-dsDNA titer with falling C3 and C4 reflects increasing immune complex formation and classical pathway activation, and is a validated serologic predictor of impending lupus nephritis flare","D":"The normalization of hemoglobin and platelet count over the same period indicates that Type II autoantibody activity is decreasing, signaling a shift to predominant Type III renal disease activity in SLE","E":"The rise in ESR with simultaneously normal CRP over the same 3-month period is characteristic of SLE flare and specifically predicts renal involvement when ESR exceeds 60 mm per hour"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The combination of rising anti-dsDNA titer and falling complement levels (both C3 and C4) is the validated serologic predictor of impending lupus nephritis flare. Rising anti-dsDNA reflects increasing autoantibody production; falling complement reflects consumption of the classical pathway by DNA-anti-DNA immune complexes. Together they indicate increasing immune complex burden before overt clinical renal disease develops, providing a window for treatment intensification. ANA titer does not directly reflect complement activation or flare severity. C3 alone is less specific than the combination with rising anti-dsDNA. Changes in hemoglobin and platelets (Type II manifestations) do not specifically predict Type III renal flare. Elevated ESR with normal CRP is a general SLE pattern but does not specifically predict nephritis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Monitor SLE activity with serial anti-dsDNA + C3 + C4 + urinalysis. Rising anti-dsDNA + falling complement = treat before overt nephritis. CRP is useful for distinguishing SLE flare (low CRP) from superimposed infection (high CRP). COMLEX: Hydroxychloroquine reduces flare frequency and decreases risk of progression to nephritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl42_q30","objective":5,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 5 — Understand the broad variety of clinical and laboratory manifestations which may be found in patients with SLE.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 33-year-old female with SLE has echocardiographic vegetations on both the atrial and ventricular surfaces of the mitral valve with negative blood cultures after 5 days. Libman-Sacks endocarditis is diagnosed. Compared to infective endocarditis, Libman-Sacks carries which specific risk related to SLE pathogenesis?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks vegetations are more highly embologenic than infective endocarditis vegetations because they contain more lipid-rich foam cells and no bacterial matrix to hold the vegetation cohesively to the valve surface","B":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis invariably progresses to severe valvular stenosis requiring surgical replacement within 5 years because immune complex deposits in the vegetation undergo progressive dystrophic calcification and leaflet fusion","C":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis causes accelerated coronary atherosclerosis specifically by shedding immune complex microemboli into coronary ostia that initiate endothelial injury and lipid deposition in the coronary artery intima","D":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is associated with secondary antiphospholipid syndrome, so its presence in an SLE patient marks a coexisting hypercoagulable risk from antiphospholipid antibodies that can cause systemic thromboembolism including stroke and retinal ischemia","E":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis causes persistent sterile febrile illness indistinguishable from infective endocarditis, and the prolonged antibiotic courses given before the correct diagnosis suppress normal flora and worsen SLE immune dysregulation"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis is nonbacterial verrucous endocarditis strongly associated with antiphospholipid syndrome in SLE. The vegetations can embolize to the brain, retina, kidney, and extremities, and their presence in an SLE patient should prompt antiphospholipid antibody testing, as they represent a clinical manifestation of secondary APS. The association with APS is the SLE-specific risk that distinguishes Libman-Sacks from other nonbacterial endocarditis. Libman-Sacks vegetations are not proven more embologenic than infective endocarditis. Most cases do not progress to severe valvular stenosis requiring surgery. Accelerated coronary atherosclerosis in SLE is multifactorial (corticosteroids, immune complex endothelial damage, traditional risk factors) and is not primarily caused by Libman-Sacks microemboli. Antibiotics are not given for Libman-Sacks endocarditis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Libman-Sacks = SLE + APS association. Small vegetations on either valve surface + negative cultures + SLE history. COMLEX: Libman-Sacks vegetations are sterile and do not require antibiotics; treat the underlying APS and SLE. Echocardiogram surveillance is recommended in APS patients.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"}],"43":[{"id":"sdl43_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly pairs an inflammatory myopathy with its distinguishing skin involvement and characteristic muscle biopsy pattern?","choices":{"A":"Polymyositis — heliotrope rash and Gottron papules over the knuckles with perifascicular atrophy on biopsy caused by CD8+ T cells invading the perimysium and complement-fixing the capillary bed","B":"Dermatomyositis — pathognomonic heliotrope rash and Gottron papules with perifascicular atrophy on biopsy, driven by CD4+ T cell perimysial inflammation and complement-mediated vasculopathy of intramuscular capillaries","C":"Inclusion body myositis — mechanic's hands and calcinosis cutis with rimmed vacuoles and amyloid deposits on biopsy in elderly patients with symmetric proximal leg and distal arm weakness","D":"Antisynthetase syndrome — heliotrope rash with anti-Jo-1 antibodies driving perimysial endomysial CD8+ T cell infiltrates and perifascicular atrophy in patients with concomitant interstitial lung disease","E":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy — Gottron papules with prominent myofiber necrosis and anti-SRP antibodies specifically in patients with prior statin exposure and normal skin on non-knuckle surfaces"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Dermatomyositis (DM) is the only inflammatory myopathy with pathognomonic skin findings: the heliotrope rash (lilac discoloration of the upper eyelids) and Gottron papules (erythematous papules over the extensor surfaces of MCP and IP joints). The muscle biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy resulting from CD4+ T cell-driven perimysial inflammation and complement (C5b-9)-mediated capillary dropout, which preferentially damages myofibers at the edge of fascicles. Polymyositis has no skin involvement and shows CD8+ endomysial infiltrates. Inclusion body myositis has no skin findings and shows rimmed vacuoles. Antisynthetase syndrome features mechanic's hands, not heliotrope rash, and has a different biopsy pattern. IMNM has no skin involvement and shows pauci-inflammatory necrosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Dermatomyositis = heliotrope rash + Gottron papules + perifascicular atrophy. The only inflammatory myopathy with skin changes. COMLEX: Both heliotrope rash and Gottron papules are pathognomonic for DM — know both on sight.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old female presents with 4 months of symmetric proximal weakness, no skin changes, elevated CK, and muscle biopsy showing CD8+ T cells invading endomysial connective tissue with diffuse necrotic and regenerating myofibers scattered throughout fascicles. Which inflammatory myopathy best fits this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Dermatomyositis presenting without skin manifestations, in which the CD8+ T cell endomysial pattern represents a variant with normal IFN signaling and absent perifascicular atrophy due to preserved capillary density","B":"Inclusion body myositis, characterized by asymmetric distal-predominant weakness with rimmed vacuoles, CD8+ T cell invasion of individual myofibers, and amyloid deposits in patients over 50 years","C":"Polymyositis (now often reclassified as antisynthetase syndrome or overlap myositis), characterized by CD8+ endomysial T cell infiltrates, scattered myofiber necrosis and regeneration, and no skin manifestations","D":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, characterized by a pauci-inflammatory biopsy with predominantly necrotic and regenerating fibers without significant T cell infiltrate and positive anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies","E":"Juvenile dermatomyositis, characterized by symmetric proximal weakness with calcinosis cutis, GI vasculitis, and perifascicular atrophy in a pediatric patient under age 18 with anti-NXP-2 antibodies"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Polymyositis (and its modern reclassifications including antisynthetase syndrome) presents with symmetric proximal weakness without skin involvement. The biopsy shows CD8+ cytotoxic T cells invading the endomysium (the connective tissue surrounding individual myofibers) with diffuse myofiber necrosis and regeneration scattered throughout fascicles — unlike dermatomyositis which preferentially damages the perifascicular region. The absence of skin findings and the CD8+ endomysial pattern distinguish it from DM. Inclusion body myositis causes asymmetric weakness and shows rimmed vacuoles. IMNM has minimal inflammatory infiltrate on biopsy. Juvenile DM presents with skin findings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Polymyositis = CD8+ endomysial infiltrates + diffuse random necrosis + no skin. DM = CD4+ perimysial + perifascicular atrophy + skin. IBM = CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles + asymmetric distal predominant. COMLEX: Most patients formerly called polymyositis are now reclassified into antisynthetase syndrome, IMNM, or IBM.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which features best distinguish inclusion body myositis from dermatomyositis and polymyositis in terms of patient demographics, pattern of weakness, and biopsy findings?","choices":{"A":"Inclusion body myositis affects patients over 50 (most common myopathy after 65), causes asymmetric weakness of the quadriceps and finger flexors, and shows rimmed vacuoles, amyloid, and CD8+ T cell invasion, unlike symmetric DM and PM","B":"Inclusion body myositis affects children and young adults, causes symmetric proximal weakness with rapid progression over weeks, and shows perifascicular atrophy with CD4+ T cell perimysial infiltrates and C5b-9 capillary deposits","C":"Inclusion body myositis is the only inflammatory myopathy that responds consistently to corticosteroids, with dramatic improvement in CK and muscle strength within 6 weeks of treatment, distinguishing it from steroid-resistant dermatomyositis","D":"Inclusion body myositis causes symmetric proximal limb weakness indistinguishable from polymyositis but is distinguished solely by calcinosis cutis and GI vasculitis in older patients and the absence of any positive autoimmune antibodies","E":"Inclusion body myositis only affects bulbar muscles producing dysphagia and dysarthria in isolation, without any limb muscle weakness, distinguishing it clearly from the limb-predominant weakness of dermatomyositis and polymyositis"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sporadic inclusion body myositis (sIBM) is the most common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 65 years and is unique in its pattern: asymmetric weakness predominantly in the quadriceps (causing falls) and distal upper extremity muscles, especially the finger flexors (causing difficulty with grip and pinch). Dysphagia is also common. Muscle biopsy shows CD8+ T cells invading individual myofibers, rimmed vacuoles (containing lysosomal aggregates and neurodegenerative proteins like beta-amyloid and TDP-43), and amyloid deposits. Importantly, sIBM typically responds poorly to corticosteroids and immunosuppressive drugs, unlike dermatomyositis and polymyositis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBM = age >50 + asymmetric quadriceps + finger flexors weak + rimmed vacuoles + steroid-resistant. DM and PM = symmetric proximal + steroid-responsive. COMLEX: IBM is the most common inflammatory myopathy in patients over 65 and is the one that does NOT respond to steroids.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 3 — Dermatomyositis (CD4+ perimysial vasculopathy, perifascicular atrophy, skin involvement), polymyositis/antisynthetase (CD8+ endomysial T cells, no skin), and inclusion body myositis (CD8+ + rimmed vacuoles, asymmetric distal-proximal) represent distinct T-cell-mediated inflammatory myopathies."},{"id":"sdl43_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which combination of extra-muscular manifestations defines the multi-system morbidity of dermatomyositis and mandates specific surveillance in all adult patients at diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Polyarthritis, uveitis, and hepatosplenomegaly driven by granulomatous perimysial inflammation, occurring in over 50% of dermatomyositis patients regardless of antibody subtype and mandating liver biopsy at diagnosis","B":"Glomerulonephritis, CNS vasculitis, and Libman-Sacks endocarditis from immune complex deposition in dermatomyositis, occurring in a pattern indistinguishable from secondary antiphospholipid syndrome in SLE","C":"Peripheral neuropathy, mononeuritis multiplex, and autonomic dysfunction from anti-Jo-1-directed complement-mediated Schwann cell destruction in dermatomyositis patients with antisynthetase overlap","D":"Interstitial lung disease, myocarditis, dysphagia from esophageal muscle involvement, and an increased risk of occult malignancy (especially with anti-TIF1-γ after age 38) requiring cancer screening","E":"Renal amyloidosis, splenomegaly, and adrenal insufficiency from chronic IL-6 and SAA-driven secondary amyloidosis in patients with longstanding dermatomyositis who have not received adequate immunosuppression"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dermatomyositis has important multi-system manifestations. The increased risk of occult malignancy (breast, lung, GI, ovarian, pancreatic, bladder), highest with anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies after age 38, mandates comprehensive cancer screening in all adult patients at diagnosis. ILD can occur in any DM subtype and is particularly aggressive with anti-MDA5 antibodies. Myocarditis causes arrhythmias and cardiomyopathy. Dysphagia results from pharyngeal and esophageal muscle involvement in approximately one-third of patients. Granulomatous inflammation is not a feature of DM. Lupus-type renal and CNS manifestations are not characteristic of DM. Peripheral neuropathy and amyloidosis are not primary multi-system features of DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: All adult DM patients need cancer screening — especially breast, ovarian, lung, GI. Anti-TIF1-gamma = highest cancer risk over age 38. COMLEX: Dermatomyositis can be a paraneoplastic syndrome; skin and muscle improve when the cancer is treated.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which antibody-clinical phenotype pairing in dermatomyositis is CORRECT?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Mi-2 antibodies are associated with rapidly progressive ILD and minimal muscle disease, with the poorest prognosis of all dermatomyositis subtypes regardless of age at diagnosis or geographic ancestry","B":"Anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies are associated with prominent skin involvement, dysphagia, and a significant association with occult malignancy in dermatomyositis patients over 38 years of age","C":"Anti-NXP-2 antibodies are associated with the highest malignancy risk among all DM antibody subtypes and produce the most severe myositis with prominent high CK and perifascicular necrosis on biopsy","D":"Anti-MDA5 antibodies are associated with high CK, severe myositis, periungual telangiectasias, and a uniformly favorable prognosis with first-line corticosteroid treatment in all patient populations","E":"Anti-SRP antibodies are associated with dermatomyositis-specific skin changes including calcinosis cutis, GI vasculitis, and childhood-onset disease with excellent treatment response to hydroxychloroquine alone"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-TIF1-gamma (transcriptional intermediary factor 1 gamma) antibodies define a dermatomyositis subtype with prominent skin involvement, dysphagia, and importantly a significant association with underlying malignancy in patients over 38 years — the highest cancer-associated DM subtype. Anti-Mi-2 is associated with severe myositis and high CK, but not primarily ILD or minimal muscle disease. Anti-NXP-2 is associated with calcinosis cutis and muscle involvement, with moderate cancer risk. Anti-MDA5 is associated with clinically amyopathic DM, rapidly progressive ILD, and poor prognosis — not favorable outcomes. Anti-SRP is an IMNM antibody, not a DM-specific antibody.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM antibody pearls — anti-TIF1-gamma = cancer risk (age >38); anti-MDA5 = ILD, little muscle; anti-Mi-2 = classic DM, high CK; anti-NXP-2 = calcinosis in juvenile DM. COMLEX: Anti-SRP and anti-HMGCR are IMNM antibodies, not DM-specific.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 14-year-old male with dermatomyositis develops abdominal pain, bloody stools, and bowel wall thickening on imaging. Which mechanism best explains this gastrointestinal complication unique to juvenile dermatomyositis?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Jo-1-mediated smooth muscle autoimmunity causing progressive intestinal hypomotility, bacterial overgrowth, and malabsorption in pediatric dermatomyositis patients with the antisynthetase overlap phenotype","B":"Anti-NXP-2-mediated calcinosis cutis extending into the bowel wall submucosa, causing mechanical obstruction of the intestinal lumen and mucosal erosion with secondary infection in juvenile dermatomyositis","C":"Immune complex deposition in mesenteric arterioles triggering Type III hypersensitivity vasculitis of the bowel wall in juvenile dermatomyositis, identical to the renal vasculitis pattern seen in childhood-onset lupus nephritis","D":"CD8+ T cell invasion of gastrointestinal mucosal enterocytes producing an IBD-like pattern of transmural inflammation in response to anti-Mi-2 antibody cross-reactivity with intestinal nucleosome proteins","E":"Vasculitis of the gastrointestinal blood supply from CD4+ T cell- and complement-mediated vascular injury, causing ischemic symptoms from abdominal pain and GI bleeding to bowel perforation in juvenile dermatomyositis"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Juvenile dermatomyositis (JDM) is the only form of inflammatory myopathy that characteristically involves GI vasculitis. The same vascular mechanism driving perifascicular muscle atrophy (CD4+ T cells and complement-mediated capillary damage) extends to the GI vasculature, causing ischemic injury to the bowel wall. This can manifest as abdominal pain, GI bleeding, or, in severe cases, bowel perforation. This is a distinguishing feature of JDM compared to adult DM. Anti-Jo-1 smooth muscle targeting is not the mechanism. Calcinosis does not obstruct the bowel lumen. Type III immune complex GI vasculitis characterizes HSP (IgA vasculitis), not DM. CD8+ enterocyte invasion is IBM and polymyositis pattern, not DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Juvenile DM features beyond adult DM: calcinosis cutis (especially anti-NXP-2), GI vasculitis with bowel perforation risk. COMLEX: All DM patients should have esophageal evaluation for dysphagia; juvenile DM additionally needs GI vasculitis monitoring.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Type I IFN signaling and CD4+ T-cell cytokine release in dermatomyositis drive multi-system morbidity including ILD, myocarditis, dysphagia, and a 20-30% malignancy risk requiring cancer surveillance in all adult patients."},{"id":"sdl43_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which medication class is most commonly associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, and what best distinguishes IMNM from direct drug myotoxicity?","choices":{"A":"Statins; IMNM is distinguished by weakness that persists or worsens after the drug is stopped, anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies, necrosis with minimal T cells on biopsy, and need for immunosuppression","B":"Fibrates are most associated with IMNM; fibrate-induced IMNM is distinguished by elevated lipoprotein lipase activity, systemic hypertriglyceridemia, and massive rhabdomyolysis that resolves completely within days of drug discontinuation without immunosuppressive treatment","C":"Hydroxychloroquine is most associated with IMNM, driving immune-mediated muscle damage by activating plasmacytoid dendritic cells via TLR7 and TLR9 to produce IFN-alpha that directly attacks myofibers expressing HMG-CoA reductase","D":"Corticosteroids are most associated with IMNM; high-dose corticosteroids paradoxically trigger anti-HMGCR antibody production via TSH receptor-mediated HMG-CoA suppression in skeletal muscle","E":"Antiretrovirals such as zidovudine are most associated with IMNM via mitochondrial DNA polymerase gamma inhibition, distinguished from direct toxicity by the presence of anti-SRP antibodies and concurrent ILD requiring additional immunosuppression"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Statins are the medication class most associated with IMNM. Direct statin myotoxicity resolves with drug discontinuation, while IMNM is an antibody-driven disease that persists or worsens after stopping the statin, requires immunosuppressive therapy, and shows positive anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies. Biopsy in IMNM shows prominent necrotic and regenerating myofibers with a paucity of inflammatory infiltrate — distinct from the robust CD8+ infiltrate in polymyositis. Note: anti-HMGCR IMNM also occurs in statin-naive patients, indicating that the antibody can arise independently of statin exposure. Fibrates, hydroxychloroquine, steroids, and zidovudine are not the primary statin-associated IMNM class.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Statins cause both direct myotoxicity (resolves with discontinuation, no antibody) and IMNM (anti-HMGCR antibody, persists after stopping, requires immunosuppression). Key distinction on boards. COMLEX: Risk factors for direct statin toxicity: age >65, renal failure, hepatic failure, hypothyroidism, high-dose statin.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"In IMNM associated with anti-HMGCR antibodies and prior statin use, which of the following best describes the antibody-driven mechanism of myofiber damage?","choices":{"A":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies form circulating immune complexes that deposit in the endomysium, activate the classical complement pathway, and trigger MAC-mediated osmotic lysis of myofibers in a Type III pattern analogous to lupus nephritis","B":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies bind HMG-CoA reductase expressed on myofiber surfaces (upregulated by statin exposure), activate complement, and recruit macrophages, producing necrotic and regenerating myofibers with few inflammatory cells on biopsy","C":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies block intracellular cholesterol synthesis in myofibers, causing sarcolemmal membrane instability from cholesterol depletion that leads to spontaneous myofiber rupture and the characteristic rimmed vacuoles on modified Gomori trichrome stain","D":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies cross-react with cardiac troponin I, causing both skeletal myopathy and severe myocarditis that specifically distinguishes anti-HMGCR IMNM from anti-SRP IMNM which has exclusively skeletal muscle involvement","E":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies activate Toll-like receptors on satellite cells, permanently suppressing myofiber regeneration and causing progressive proximal weakness despite statin discontinuation and high-dose corticosteroid therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"In IMNM, statins upregulate HMG-CoA reductase (HMGCR) expression on the surface of myofibers as a compensatory response to enzymatic blockade. Anti-HMGCR autoantibodies then bind these surface-expressed HMGCR molecules and mediate myofiber destruction through complement activation and recruitment of NK cells and macrophages — primarily a Type II antibody-mediated process rather than T cell-mediated Type IV. This explains the characteristic biopsy finding of prominent necrotic and regenerating myofibers with a paucity of T cell infiltrate (pauci-inflammatory necrosis), which contrasts sharply with polymyositis and dermatomyositis. Anti-HMGCR does not form deposited immune complexes like lupus nephritis. Cholesterol depletion does not cause rimmed vacuoles. Cross-reactivity with troponin I is not the established mechanism. Satellite cell TLR activation is not the pathway.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IMNM biopsy = prominent necrosis + minimal inflammation. Statin increases HMGCR on myofiber surface → anti-HMGCR binds → complement + macrophage mediated necrosis. Contrast with DM (CD4+ perimysial) and PM (CD8+ endomysial). COMLEX: Anti-HMGCR and anti-SRP are the two IMNM antibodies.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"How does anti-SRP antibody-associated IMNM clinically differ from anti-HMGCR antibody-associated IMNM?","choices":{"A":"Anti-SRP patients more often have pathognomonic skin changes including Gottron papules and heliotrope rash, while anti-HMGCR patients have no skin involvement and are definitively associated with prior statin exposure in 100% of cases","B":"Anti-SRP patients have exclusively childhood-onset IMNM with calcinosis cutis and GI vasculitis, while anti-HMGCR patients have adult-onset IMNM occurring only in statin-naive patients without any relevant drug history","C":"Anti-SRP disease is typically more severe, with more frequent dysphagia and cardiac involvement (arrhythmias, cardiomyopathy), while anti-HMGCR patients may or may not have statin exposure and usually respond better to treatment","D":"Anti-SRP patients achieve complete remission with statin discontinuation alone without immunosuppressive therapy, while anti-HMGCR patients require indefinite high-dose corticosteroids plus cyclophosphamide for disease control","E":"Anti-SRP patients present with lower CK levels and a more indolent clinical course than anti-HMGCR patients, who characteristically present with acute rhabdomyolysis and oliguric renal failure requiring emergent plasmapheresis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anti-SRP (signal recognition particle) IMNM is generally more severe than anti-HMGCR IMNM. Anti-SRP patients more frequently develop dysphagia from pharyngeal muscle involvement and cardiac involvement including arrhythmias and myocarditis, contributing to greater overall morbidity. Anti-SRP IMNM also tends to present with higher CK levels and more rapid weakness progression. Anti-HMGCR IMNM can occur with or without prior statin exposure and generally has a milder course with better treatment response. Neither subtype has dermatomyositis-specific skin findings. Anti-SRP is not exclusive to children. Statin discontinuation alone is insufficient for either subtype (both require immunosuppression). Anti-SRP patients do not have lower CK levels than anti-HMGCR.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-SRP = more severe, dysphagia, cardiac involvement. Anti-HMGCR = statin-associated (but not always), less severe. Both require immunosuppression. COMLEX: Both IMNM subtypes show pauci-inflammatory necrosis on biopsy — the low T cell infiltrate distinguishes IMNM from polymyositis.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 3 — Statins (HMG-CoA reductase inhibitors) are the medication class most associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, driven by anti-HMGCR or anti-SRP antibodies causing myofiber necrosis that persists after drug discontinuation and requires immunosuppressive therapy."},{"id":"sdl43_q10","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 45-year-old female with proximal weakness, muscle cramping, slowed reflexes, weight gain, and cold intolerance has CK of 1,800 U/L and TSH of 45 mIU/L. Which muscle biopsy finding is most consistent with hypothyroid myopathy?","choices":{"A":"Myofiber atrophy, increased subsarcolemmal nuclei abnormally positioned internally, glycogen aggregates, and occasional mucopolysaccharide deposition in connective tissue, reflecting metabolic changes from thyroid hormone deficiency affecting muscle energy metabolism","B":"Perifascicular atrophy with CD4+ T cell perimysial infiltrates and C5b-9 complement deposition in capillaries, consistent with an autoimmune mechanism triggered by hypothyroidism-induced upregulation of complement proteins in skeletal muscle","C":"Rimmed vacuoles with CD8+ T cell endomysial invasion and amyloid deposits on Congo red stain, indicating that hypothyroidism accelerates the protein aggregation pathways of sporadic inclusion body myositis in genetically susceptible older patients","D":"Prominent pauci-inflammatory myofiber necrosis with positive anti-HMGCR antibodies, indicating that hypothyroidism promotes IMNM by upregulating HMG-CoA reductase expression on myofiber surfaces in the absence of statin exposure","E":"Nemaline rods on modified Gomori trichrome staining representing alpha-actinin Z-band disruption from hypothyroid-mediated sarcomeric reorganization of type I oxidative fibers in chronically hypothyroid skeletal muscle"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Hypothyroid myopathy results from thyroid hormone deficiency impairing muscle metabolism. The histologic changes include myofiber atrophy, abnormally positioned (internal) nuclei, glycogen aggregates from impaired glycogenolysis, and occasional mucopolysaccharide (glycosaminoglycan) deposition in connective tissue. These metabolic changes are reversible with thyroid hormone replacement. There is no autoimmune inflammatory infiltrate (distinguishing it from DM, PM), no rimmed vacuoles (distinguishing it from IBM), no pauci-inflammatory anti-HMGCR necrosis (distinguishing it from IMNM), and no nemaline rods (a congenital myopathy finding).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroid myopathy = elevated CK + slowed deep tendon reflexes + cramping + proximal weakness. Reverts with levothyroxine. Hypothyroidism also increases statin myotoxicity risk. COMLEX: Check TSH in any patient with unexplained proximal weakness and elevated CK before pursuing inflammatory myopathy workup.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q11","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"How does thyrotoxic myopathy differ from hypothyroid myopathy in clinical presentation, CK levels, and reflex examination?","choices":{"A":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as asymmetric distal limb weakness with elevated CK and hyperreflexia, while hypothyroid myopathy causes symmetric proximal weakness with normal CK and mildly slowed but otherwise normal deep tendon reflexes","B":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as a rapidly progressive bulbar palsy with markedly elevated CK and absent deep tendon reflexes, while hypothyroid myopathy causes predominantly respiratory muscle weakness with normal CK and preserved reflexes throughout the course","C":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as episodic paralysis triggered by carbohydrate intake with dramatically elevated CK between episodes, while hypothyroid myopathy causes persistent proximal weakness with normal CK and hyperreflexia from thyroid hormone-mediated sodium channel upregulation","D":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as an acute inflammatory myositis with elevated anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies and positive ANA at high titer, while hypothyroid myopathy presents with anti-HMGCR antibodies and pauci-inflammatory necrosis on biopsy","E":"Thyrotoxic myopathy presents as proximal muscle weakness with normal or low CK and hyperreflexia (brisk reflexes) in the context of hyperthyroidism signs, while hypothyroid myopathy causes proximal weakness with markedly elevated CK, slowed movement and reflexes, and muscle cramping"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Thyrotoxic myopathy most commonly presents as subacute or chronic proximal muscle weakness in the setting of hyperthyroidism (tachycardia, heat intolerance, weight loss, exophthalmos). CK is characteristically normal or low because the hypermetabolic state accelerates muscle protein breakdown without causing necrosis as the primary mechanism. Reflexes are brisk. Ophthalmoplegia (exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia) with eyelid swelling and diplopia may also occur. Hypothyroid myopathy presents with elevated CK (sometimes markedly), slowed movement, slow relaxation of deep tendon reflexes (the classic \"hung-up\" reflex), and cramping from reduced myosin ATPase activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyrotoxic = proximal weakness + normal/low CK + hyperreflexia + hyperthyroid signs. Hypothyroid = proximal weakness + elevated CK + slowed \"hung-up\" reflexes + cramping. Both improve with treating the underlying thyroid disorder. COMLEX: Hung-up reflexes = hypothyroidism hallmark; brisk reflexes in muscle weakness = consider hyperthyroidism or upper motor neuron disorder.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q12","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":1,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"Which endocrine condition is listed as a specific risk factor for direct statin myotoxicity (as opposed to statin-induced IMNM), and what best explains this increased risk?","choices":{"A":"Hyperthyroidism, because elevated thyroid hormone increases CYP3A4 enzyme activity that metabolizes lipophilic statins more rapidly, producing toxic hydroxylated statin intermediates that accumulate preferentially in skeletal muscle cells","B":"Hypothyroidism, a recognized risk factor for direct statin myotoxicity, because hypothyroid muscle already has impaired energy metabolism and myosin ATPase activity (a metabolic myopathy with raised CK), so a statin adds a second insult","C":"Type 2 diabetes mellitus, because hyperglycemia upregulates HMG-CoA reductase expression on myofiber surfaces via AGE-receptor signaling, creating metabolic vulnerability to statin-induced mitochondrial coenzyme Q10 depletion","D":"Primary hyperparathyroidism, because elevated PTH directly disrupts myofiber membrane calcium homeostasis and increases susceptibility to statin-mediated mitochondrial complex I inhibition via calpain-dependent proteolysis","E":"Adrenal insufficiency (Addison disease), because cortisol deficiency impairs satellite cell recovery from statin-mediated mitochondrial damage by blocking glucocorticoid receptor-mediated upregulation of mitochondrial biogenesis genes in regenerating myofibers"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Hypothyroidism is listed with high statin dose, age over 65, renal failure, and hepatic failure as a risk factor for direct statin myotoxicity. Hypothyroidism independently causes a metabolic myopathy (impaired oxidative metabolism and myosin ATPase activity, often with a raised CK), so muscle that is already energetically compromised is more vulnerable to the added mitochondrial insult of a statin, and the combination raises the risk of rhabdomyolysis. Hyperthyroidism is not a listed risk factor, and the CYP3A4 mechanism offered for it is not the basis of statin myotoxicity risk. Type 2 diabetes, hyperparathyroidism, and adrenal insufficiency are not established statin myotoxicity risk factors.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Statin myotoxicity risk factors = high statin dose + age >65 + renal failure + hepatic failure + HYPOTHYROIDISM. Check TSH before attributing weakness to statin alone. COMLEX: A hypothyroid patient started on a statin who develops weakness should have the statin held and thyroid function optimized.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 2 — Thyroid dysfunction causes myopathy detectable on CK and EMG: hypothyroid myopathy produces elevated CK, slowed reflexes, glycogen aggregates, and cramping; thyrotoxic myopathy produces proximal weakness with normal or low CK and brisk reflexes in the setting of hypermetabolism."},{"id":"sdl43_q13","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old woman has 3 months of difficulty climbing stairs and combing her hair. She has violaceous discoloration of the upper eyelids, scaly violaceous papules over the MCP and PIP joints, and facial erythema across the cheeks that extends into the nasolabial folds. CK is 2,900 U/L. Which feature best distinguishes her facial rash from the malar rash of SLE?","choices":{"A":"Its photodistribution on sun-exposed facial skin, a pattern the malar rash of SLE does not show","B":"Its spread across both cheeks, a distribution that the malar rash of SLE does not take","C":"Its association with painless oral ulcers, a mucosal finding that does not occur in SLE","D":"Its extension down into the nasolabial folds, which the malar rash of SLE characteristically spares","E":"Its occurrence with joint pain, a combination that the malar rash of SLE does not show"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Dermatomyositis can produce a malar rash that resembles lupus, but unlike the malar rash of SLE it involves the nasolabial folds. Here the facial erythema extends into the folds and is accompanied by the heliotrope rash and Gottron papules, the pathognomonic DM findings, together with proximal weakness and a raised CK. Photosensitivity does not separate the two: FA describes photodistributed facial erythema in DM and lists photosensitivity among SLE features. Erythema across the cheeks is exactly where the lupus malar rash lies. Painless oral ulcers and nonerosive arthritis are both features of SLE, so neither can mark a rash as dermatomyositis rather than lupus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM skin = heliotrope rash (violaceous eyelids), Gottron papules (extensor MCP/IP), shawl-and-face rash, mechanic's hands; the DM malar rash involves the nasolabial folds, the SLE malar rash spares them. COMLEX: Heliotrope rash and Gottron papules are pathognomonic for DM, and adult DM requires a search for occult cancer.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q14","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 42-year-old female presents with proximal weakness, mechanic's hands (hyperkeratotic fissuring on the radial aspects of fingers), arthritis, and progressive dyspnea. HRCT shows bilateral ground-glass opacities. CK is 1,800 U/L. Anti-Jo-1 antibodies are positive. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy and perifascicular necrosis. Which disease does this constellation represent, and what extra-muscular feature most determines prognosis?","choices":{"A":"Antisynthetase syndrome (Jo-1 syndrome): the triad of myositis with proximal weakness, interstitial lung disease, and arthritis plus mechanic's hands defines it, and ILD is the extramuscular complication that most strongly determines prognosis","B":"Dermatomyositis with anti-MDA5 antibodies presenting as clinically amyopathic dermatomyositis, where ILD is less rapidly progressive than classic anti-MDA5 DM and mechanic's hands represent a DM-specific skin manifestation distinct from Gottron papules","C":"Polymyositis with antisynthetase overlap, in which the ILD reflects aspiration pneumonitis from esophageal muscle weakness rather than primary autoimmune lung injury driven by anti-Jo-1 antibody-mediated tRNA synthetase damage in pneumocytes","D":"Systemic sclerosis with overlap myositis, in which anti-Jo-1 antibodies cross-react with pulmonary fibroblast tRNA synthetase, driving progressive ILD independently of the myositis component through direct fibroblast activation","E":"Sjogren syndrome with overlap inflammatory myopathy and anti-Ro antibody-mediated lung disease, where mechanic's hands represent a secondary sicca-related fissuring keratoderma from decreased sweat gland secretion"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Antisynthetase syndrome is defined by antibodies against aminoacyl tRNA synthetases, most commonly anti-Jo-1 (anti-histidyl tRNA synthetase). The full syndrome includes myositis, interstitial lung disease, arthritis, mechanic's hands (hyperkeratotic fissuring on radial fingers and ulnar thumb), Raynaud phenomenon, and fever. ILD is the major determinant of morbidity and mortality in antisynthetase syndrome. Biopsy may show perifascicular atrophy similar to DM or random necrosis. Anti-MDA5 DM presents without mechanic's hands as a defining feature. Aspiration pneumonitis is not the mechanism of ILD in antisynthetase syndrome. Systemic sclerosis has a distinct antibody profile (anti-Scl-70, anti-centromere). Sjogren lacks mechanic's hands.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Antisynthetase syndrome = anti-Jo-1 + myositis + ILD + arthritis + mechanic's hands. ILD determines prognosis. COMLEX: Anti-Jo-1 is the most common antisynthetase antibody; others include anti-PL-7, anti-PL-12, anti-EJ.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q15","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate the types of inflammatory myopathy with or without skin involvement.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 7-year-old female has proximal weakness, heliotrope rash, Gottron papules, and calcium deposits over her elbows and buttocks. Biopsy shows perifascicular atrophy and C5b-9 in capillaries. Compared to adult dermatomyositis, which features specifically characterize juvenile dermatomyositis?","choices":{"A":"Juvenile DM has a lower risk of malignancy than adult DM but a significantly higher risk of ILD, making pulmonary function testing and lung surveillance the priority over cancer screening in all pediatric dermatomyositis patients","B":"Juvenile DM is enriched for anti-NXP-2 antibodies, more often develops calcinosis cutis (dystrophic calcium in superficial soft tissues), and can cause GI vasculitis with pain, bleeding, or perforation, less characteristic of adult DM","C":"Juvenile DM always responds completely to corticosteroids as monotherapy without needing steroid-sparing agents, making its long-term prognosis uniformly favorable compared to the more treatment-refractory adult dermatomyositis subtype","D":"Juvenile DM produces perimysial rather than perifascicular atrophy on biopsy and lacks C5b-9 capillary deposition, histopathologically distinguishing it from adult DM in which complement deposition is the defining mechanistic feature","E":"Juvenile DM primarily affects boys over age 10 with anti-TIF1-gamma antibodies and carries the same malignancy risk as adult DM over 38, requiring identical cancer screening from age 10 onward regardless of sex or antibody status"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Juvenile DM differs from adult DM in several important ways. First, malignancy is uncommon in juvenile DM (unlike adult DM, which carries an increased risk of occult cancer). Second, juvenile DM is enriched for anti-NXP-2 antibodies, which are associated with calcinosis cutis — dystrophic calcium deposits in superficial soft tissues including skin, subcutaneous tissue, and muscle fasciae. Third, juvenile DM can cause GI vasculitis (the same complement-mediated capillary injury that damages muscle and skin extends to GI vasculature), ranging from abdominal pain and bleeding to bowel perforation — a potentially life-threatening complication rarely seen in adult DM. The biopsy findings (perifascicular atrophy, C5b-9 deposition) are similar to adult DM.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Juvenile DM hallmarks = calcinosis cutis + GI vasculitis (perforation risk) + anti-NXP-2 enrichment + low malignancy risk. COMLEX: All DM features occur on a spectrum — JDM adds the calcinosis and vasculitis dimensions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 3 — Hypersensitivity Type Classification and Autoimmune Disease Pathogenesis"},{"id":"sdl43_q16","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 59-year-old man with dermatomyositis develops choking and coughing when he drinks liquids, nasal regurgitation, and a right lower lobe aspiration pneumonia. Which mechanism best explains his swallowing problem?","choices":{"A":"Fibrous replacement of the smooth muscle of the lower esophagus with incompetence of the lower esophageal sphincter","B":"Loss of inhibitory neurons in the myenteric plexus, so that the lower esophageal sphincter fails to relax on swallowing","C":"Extrinsic compression of the pharynx by dystrophic calcinosis deposits that form in the deep soft tissues of the neck","D":"Destruction of the glossopharyngeal and vagus nerves by anti-Jo-1 antibodies directed against Schwann cell myelin","E":"Inflammatory weakness of the oropharyngeal and esophageal muscles, a complication in about one-third of patients"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Dysphagia in dermatomyositis results from involvement of the oropharyngeal and esophageal muscles and occurs in about one-third of patients; weak swallowing muscles let liquids enter the airway and nose, causing choking, nasal regurgitation, and aspiration pneumonia. Fibrous replacement of the esophageal muscularis with sphincter dysfunction and reflux is the esophageal lesion of systemic sclerosis. Failure of the lower esophageal sphincter to relax from degeneration of inhibitory myenteric neurons is achalasia, which produces a dilated esophagus rather than oropharyngeal choking with nasal regurgitation. Calcinosis in DM forms in superficial soft tissues, mainly in juvenile and anti-NXP-2 disease, and does not compress the pharynx. Anti-Jo-1 is directed against histidyl-tRNA synthetase and defines antisynthetase syndrome; it is not an antibody against cranial nerve myelin.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM dysphagia (about one-third of patients) = weakness of oropharyngeal and esophageal muscle → aspiration; SSc dysphagia = fibrosis of esophageal smooth muscle with reflux; achalasia = failed LES relaxation from myenteric neuron loss. COMLEX: Ask every DM patient about swallowing, since aspiration adds pulmonary morbidity alongside ILD.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q17","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old female with dermatomyositis develops progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough 6 months after her myopathy diagnosis. HRCT shows bilateral basilar ground-glass opacities. PFTs show a restrictive pattern with reduced DLCO. Anti-MDA5 antibodies are detected. Which statement best characterizes the ILD in this clinical context?","choices":{"A":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is caused solely by aspiration pneumonitis from pharyngeal and esophageal muscle weakness, making swallowing rehabilitation and head-of-bed elevation the primary interventions rather than immunosuppression","B":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is driven by anti-Jo-1 antibodies that invariably coexist with anti-MDA5 in the antisynthetase overlap, producing the same clinical and radiographic ILD phenotype as anti-Jo-1-positive antisynthetase syndrome","C":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM is reversible with corticosteroid monotherapy within 4 to 6 weeks because MDA5 is an intracellular target not accessible to circulating antibodies once membrane integrity is restored by immunosuppression","D":"ILD associated with anti-MDA5 antibodies can follow a rapidly progressive course with a poor prognosis in a subset of patients, even though their skeletal muscle involvement is often mild or absent (clinically amyopathic DM)","E":"The ILD in anti-MDA5 DM develops exclusively in patients who smoke and is produced by IFN-alpha-mediated fibroblast activation independent of the T cell-mediated vasculopathy that causes perifascicular atrophy in DM muscle"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-MDA5 (melanoma differentiation-associated protein 5) is an IFN-induced cytosolic viral RNA sensor. Anti-MDA5 DM is characterized by clinically amyopathic or mild muscle disease combined with a high risk of rapidly progressive ILD (RP-ILD), which can be life-threatening. Some anti-MDA5 patients have a more indolent ILD course with skin manifestations and joint involvement, while others develop fulminant RP-ILD with poor prognosis even with aggressive immunosuppression. This paradox — minimal myositis but severe pulmonary involvement — is a clinical pearl. The ILD is not from aspiration. Anti-Jo-1 and anti-MDA5 are distinct antibodies defining different DM subtypes. Corticosteroid monotherapy is often insufficient for anti-MDA5 ILD. Smoking is not required.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-MDA5 = amyopathic DM + rapidly progressive ILD = worst prognosis subset of DM. Remember the inverse: least muscle disease = most lung disease risk. COMLEX: Anti-MDA5 DM patients need pulmonologist co-management from diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q18","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the multi-system involvement causing significant morbidity which can occur in dermatomyositis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 62-year-old male with dermatomyositis develops exertional dyspnea and resting tachycardia. ECG shows PR prolongation and nonspecific ST changes. Echo reveals LVEF of 40% with global hypokinesis. Troponin-I is elevated. Which mechanism best explains the cardiac complication?","choices":{"A":"Libman-Sacks endocarditis from antiphospholipid antibody-mediated fibrin-platelet valve vegetation formation that subsequently embolizes to the coronary arteries causing myocardial infarction and diffuse global hypokinesis in DM patients","B":"Pericarditis from immune complex deposition in the pericardium identical to SLE serositis, in which anti-dsDNA antibodies that coexist with DM cross-react with double-stranded DNA in pericardial mesothelial cells","C":"Myocarditis from CD4+ T cell and IFN-driven inflammatory injury to cardiac myocytes, analogous to the perimysial vasculopathy and IFN signaling that damage skeletal muscle in dermatomyositis, producing arrhythmias and cardiomyopathy","D":"Coronary artery aneurysm formation from complement-mediated vasculitis of the epicardial coronary vessels, identical in mechanism to the coronary artery dilation seen in pediatric Kawasaki disease inflammatory vasculitis","E":"Pulmonary arterial hypertension from anti-MDA5-driven pulmonary endothelial Raynaud-like vasospasm producing right heart strain that reduces LVEF through interventricular septal flattening and paradoxical motion on echo"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Myocarditis is a recognized extra-muscular manifestation of dermatomyositis, occurring through the same pathogenic mechanisms that damage skeletal muscle: CD4+ T cell-mediated and IFN-driven inflammatory injury to myocytes, causing global myocardial dysfunction. This produces cardiomyopathy (reduced LVEF, global hypokinesis), arrhythmias (conduction block, PR prolongation), and elevated troponin. The clinical picture is that of inflammatory cardiomyopathy, not coronary artery disease. Libman-Sacks endocarditis and anti-dsDNA are SLE findings. Coronary aneurysms are Kawasaki disease. PAH with right heart strain would cause RV dilation and right-axis deviation, not global LV hypokinesis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: DM multi-system = ILD + myocarditis + malignancy + dysphagia. Myocarditis in DM produces the same inflammatory cardiomyopathy phenotype as viral myocarditis. COMLEX: Screen DM patients with ECG and echo at baseline; elevated troponin in DM may reflect myocarditis, not coronary disease.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl43_q19","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Statins are the drugs most often linked to immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy. Which other drug class can also trigger an immune-mediated myopathy, rather than injuring muscle by a direct toxic or metabolic mechanism?","choices":{"A":"Zidovudine and related antiretrovirals, which damage muscle mitochondria to produce an acquired mitochondrial myopathy","B":"Hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine, which impair lysosomal function and cause vacuolation mainly in type I fibers","C":"Glucocorticoids, which act catabolically on muscle to cause selective atrophy of type II fibers and proximal weakness","D":"Fibrates, which directly damage individual myofibers and can cause necrosis severe enough to produce rhabdomyolysis","E":"Immune checkpoint inhibitors, which block inhibitory signals on T cells and can release an immune attack on muscle"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Statins and immune checkpoint inhibitors are the medications named as triggers of an immune-mediated myopathy. Checkpoint inhibitors are antibodies against PD-1, PD-L1, or CTLA-4, molecules that normally restrain T-cell activation, so blocking them can release immune responses against self tissues such as muscle. The other drugs injure muscle without an immune attack. Zidovudine damages mitochondria and produces an acquired mitochondrial myopathy. Hydroxychloroquine and chloroquine interfere with lysosomal function, causing a vacuolar myopathy that mainly affects type I fibers. Glucocorticoids are catabolic and cause selective type II fiber atrophy. Fibrates, like statins and high-dose alcohol, can directly damage and necrose individual myofibers and, when severe, cause rhabdomyolysis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Immune-mediated drug myopathy = statins (anti-HMGCR IMNM) and immune checkpoint inhibitors. Direct or metabolic = fibrates, alcohol, statin toxicity (necrosis); zidovudine (mitochondrial); hydroxychloroquine (lysosomal, type I vacuoles); glucocorticoids (type II atrophy). COMLEX: New proximal weakness with a raised CK in a patient on a PD-1, PD-L1, or CTLA-4 inhibitor should raise concern for an immune-mediated myopathy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q20","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 67-year-old man taking simvastatin 40 mg daily is prescribed clarithromycin for pneumonia. Five days later he has diffuse muscle pain, weakness, and dark urine, and his CK is 22,000 U/L. The statin is stopped; over 3 weeks his CK normalizes and his strength returns. Anti-HMGCR antibodies are negative. Which mechanism best explains his myopathy?","choices":{"A":"Clarithromycin acted as a hapten that triggered anti-HMGCR antibodies, causing an immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy","B":"Clarithromycin induced CYP3A4 and lowered simvastatin levels, so the muscle injury came from the pneumonia itself","C":"Simvastatin upregulated HMG-CoA reductase on myofibers, which recruited CD8+ T cells into the endomysium","D":"Clarithromycin inhibited CYP3A4, raising simvastatin levels and causing dose-related direct injury to myofibers","E":"Clarithromycin blocked ryanodine receptors in muscle, causing a calcium release like that of malignant hyperthermia"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Simvastatin, lovastatin, and atorvastatin are cleared mainly by CYP3A4, and macrolide antibiotics such as clarithromycin inhibit or compete for that enzyme, so statin levels rise and myopathy becomes more likely. The risk of direct statin toxicity increases with the statin dose, and affected patients typically improve once the drug is stopped, as happened here; if the drug is continued, rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria and renal injury can follow. Anti-HMGCR immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy would be antibody positive and would persist after the statin is stopped, requiring immunosuppression. Clarithromycin inhibits CYP3A4; inducers such as rifampin are the drugs that lower statin levels. Upregulated HMGCR is the antibody target in IMNM, whose biopsy shows necrosis with few inflammatory cells, not a CD8+ endomysial infiltrate. Ryanodine receptor–mediated calcium release is the mechanism of malignant hyperthermia, a toxicity of inhaled anesthetics and succinylcholine, not of macrolides.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CYP3A4-cleared statins (simvastatin, lovastatin, atorvastatin) + CYP3A4 inhibitors (macrolides, azole antifungals, cyclosporine, some HIV protease inhibitors, grapefruit juice) → ↑statin levels → myopathy/rhabdomyolysis; pravastatin or rosuvastatin is preferred with such drugs. COMLEX: Direct statin myotoxicity resolves after stopping the drug; weakness that persists or worsens suggests anti-HMGCR IMNM.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q21","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Define the medication most often associated with immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy as well as the antibody-driven mechanism of damage.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 60-year-old female with anti-HMGCR IMNM is told she must restart a statin for secondary prevention after a recent MI. Which best explains why anti-HMGCR IMNM can develop in statin-naive patients, complicating the simple causal assumption?","choices":{"A":"Statin-naive patients develop anti-HMGCR IMNM because statins contaminate livestock feed at sub-therapeutic concentrations, exposing individuals to cumulative statin doses that prime autoreactive B cells for HMGCR-targeted responses over decades","B":"Statin-naive patients with anti-HMGCR IMNM have a genetic HMGCR variant that serves as a neoantigen, triggering anti-HMGCR antibody production via molecular mimicry with microbial cell wall sterol biosynthesis enzymes","C":"HMG-CoA reductase expression is upregulated in regenerating muscle fibers regardless of statin exposure, so any muscle injury that triggers regeneration can expose HMGCR antigen and induce anti-HMGCR antibodies in susceptible people","D":"Anti-HMGCR antibodies in statin-naive patients arise from cross-reactivity between HMGCR and complement protein C1q, triggered by early complement deficiency that unmasks HMGCR as a neoplastic self-antigen in regenerating muscle","E":"Statin-naive anti-HMGCR IMNM is always a paraneoplastic process, arising exclusively when tumor cells upregulate HMG-CoA reductase for cholesterol biosynthesis and generate immune responses that cross-react with normal myofiber HMGCR expression"},"correct":"C","explanation":"The occurrence of anti-HMGCR IMNM in statin-naive patients reveals that HMGCR is expressed physiologically in regenerating muscle fibers (as part of the cholesterol synthesis required for membrane assembly during regeneration), independent of pharmacologic HMG-CoA inhibition. Any trigger of muscle injury and regeneration — including other causes of myopathy, mechanical injury, or viral infection — can upregulate HMGCR in regenerating myofibers, potentially presenting this antigen to the immune system and triggering anti-HMGCR antibody production in genetically susceptible individuals. This also explains why re-exposing a patient with anti-HMGCR IMNM to a statin is dangerous — it amplifies HMGCR expression on myofibers. Food contamination is not evidence-based. A genetic HMGCR neoantigen has not been identified. C1q cross-reactivity is speculative. Not all statin-naive cases are paraneoplastic.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-HMGCR IMNM can occur without statin exposure because regenerating myofibers normally upregulate HMGCR. This distinguishes IMNM as a true autoimmune disease, not purely drug-induced. COMLEX: Do not restart statins in confirmed anti-HMGCR IMNM patients; alternative lipid-lowering agents (ezetimibe, PCSK9 inhibitors) are preferred.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 3 — Most Common Adverse Effects by Class of Treatment"},{"id":"sdl43_q22","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 54-year-old woman reports fatigue, cold intolerance, weight gain, aching proximal muscles, and nighttime tingling of her thumb, index, and middle fingers. Tapping her biceps with a reflex hammer raises a small, transient mound of muscle at the point struck. She takes no medications. CK is 1,300 U/L and TSH is 38 mIU/L. Which best accounts for her muscle and hand findings?","choices":{"A":"Hypothyroidism, causing a myopathy with raised CK and myoedema plus compression of the median nerve at the wrist","B":"Thyrotoxicosis, causing a catabolic myopathy with myoedema plus a neuropathy that resembles Guillain-Barré syndrome","C":"Dermatomyositis, causing perifascicular atrophy with raised CK plus vasculitic infarction of the median nerve","D":"Polymyositis, causing CD8+ endomysial myositis with raised CK plus mononeuritis multiplex of the median nerve","E":"Immune-mediated necrotizing myopathy, causing pauci-inflammatory necrosis plus antibody injury to the median nerve"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The musculoskeletal effects of hypothyroidism include hypothyroid myopathy (proximal weakness with raised CK), carpal tunnel syndrome, and myoedema, a small lump that rises on the surface of a muscle when it is struck with a hammer. Hypothyroidism also causes muscle cramping or aching with slowed movement and reflexes, and it can produce compression mononeuropathies such as carpal tunnel syndrome. Her cold intolerance, weight gain, and TSH of 38 confirm hypothyroidism. Thyrotoxic myopathy causes proximal weakness with a normal CK, and the rare Guillain-Barré-like neuropathy occurs with hyperthyroidism, not with a high TSH. Dermatomyositis and polymyositis are inflammatory myopathies; she has no rash, and neither explains myoedema or her thyroid findings. IMNM is a necrotizing myopathy linked to anti-SRP or anti-HMGCR antibodies; it does not cause myoedema or a median neuropathy.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Hypothyroid muscle = proximal weakness + ↑CK + cramps or aching + slowed reflexes + myoedema; hypothyroid nerve = carpal tunnel (compression) or distal sensory axonal polyneuropathy. Thyrotoxic myopathy = proximal weakness with normal CK. COMLEX: Check TSH in a patient with proximal weakness, a raised CK, and carpal tunnel symptoms.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl43_q23","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 36-year-old woman has 6 weeks of difficulty climbing stairs and lifting her arms overhead. She has not noticed palpitations, tremor, or weight loss. CK is 70 U/L. TSH is undetectable and free T4 is elevated. A month later she develops eyelid swelling, conjunctival edema, and double vision. Which statement about her myopathy is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Proximal weakness from thyroid excess appears only after years of overt symptoms such as tremor and weight loss","B":"Her normal CK excludes a myopathy and shows that her weakness must arise at the neuromuscular junction","C":"Her eye findings point to a second disease, because thyroid-related myopathy never involves the orbital tissues","D":"Thyrotoxic myopathy can precede other hyperthyroid signs, keeps CK normal, and may come with exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia","E":"Her weakness reflects a necrotizing myopathy that must be confirmed by muscle biopsy before thyroid treatment"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Thyrotoxic myopathy most commonly presents as acute or chronic proximal weakness that may precede other signs of hyperthyroidism, and affected patients may also develop exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia, with swelling of the eyelids, conjunctival edema, and diplopia. FA characterizes thyrotoxic myopathy as proximal weakness with a normal CK, in contrast to the raised CK of hypothyroid myopathy. Weakness therefore need not wait for tremor or weight loss. A normal CK does not exclude a myopathy; it is the expected finding in thyrotoxic myopathy. The eyelid swelling, conjunctival edema, and diplopia belong to the same thyroid disorder, not to a second disease. A normal CK gives no evidence of a necrotizing myopathy, so there is nothing for a biopsy to confirm before the hyperthyroidism is addressed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Thyrotoxic myopathy = proximal weakness, normal CK, may precede other hyperthyroid signs, ± exophthalmic ophthalmoplegia (eyelid swelling, conjunctival edema, diplopia). Hypothyroid myopathy = ↑CK, cramps, slowed reflexes. COMLEX: Unexplained proximal weakness with a normal CK should prompt thyroid testing even before classic hyperthyroid symptoms appear.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"},{"id":"sdl43_q24","objective":4,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 4 — Describe the myopathies associated with thyroid dysfunction.","batch":2,"isHighYield":false,"stem":"A 58-year-old man has 4 months of proximal weakness, muscle aching, and cramps. CK is 2,200 U/L. His ankle reflexes show a normal contraction followed by slow relaxation. He has no rash or dysphagia, takes no statin, and his ANA is negative. His physician plans a muscle biopsy for a suspected inflammatory myopathy. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":{"A":"Start high-dose prednisone empirically, because waiting for a diagnosis risks a permanent loss of strength","B":"Proceed directly to muscle biopsy, because a CK above 2,000 U/L can only come from an inflammatory myopathy","C":"Measure TSH, because hypothyroid myopathy causes proximal weakness, cramps, raised CK, and slowed reflexes","D":"Order anti-HMGCR antibodies and begin IVIG, because statin-naive IMNM is the leading cause of this picture","E":"Perform repetitive nerve stimulation, because cramps with slow reflexes indicate a neuromuscular junction disorder"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hypothyroidism causes a myopathy with proximal weakness and raised CK, together with cramping or aching of muscles and slowed movement and reflexes, so it can mimic an inflammatory myopathy. Slow reflex relaxation and cramps in a patient without rash, dysphagia, or statin exposure make TSH the appropriate first test before biopsy or immunosuppression. Empiric prednisone would give an immunosuppressant before the cause is known, and glucocorticoids themselves cause type II fiber atrophy and proximal weakness. A high CK is not specific for inflammatory myopathy: hypothyroid myopathy, direct statin or fibrate toxicity, and IMNM all raise it. Anti-HMGCR antibodies can occur in statin-naive patients, but jumping to antibody testing and IVIG skips the metabolic cause that his slowed reflexes and cramps suggest. Cramps and slow reflex relaxation are muscle findings of hypothyroidism, not signs of a neuromuscular junction disorder.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Myopathies with ↑CK: inflammatory (DM, antisynthetase syndrome, IMNM), toxic (statins, fibrates, alcohol), and metabolic (hypothyroidism). Slowed reflexes + cramps + ↑CK → check TSH. COMLEX: Exclude hypothyroidism before muscle biopsy or immunosuppression in a patient with proximal weakness and a raised CK.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 44 Obj 2 — Commonly Used Laboratory Tests to Aid Evaluation of Rheumatic Disease"}],"44":[{"id":"sdl44_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements correctly defines the sensitivity of a diagnostic test?","choices":{"A":"The proportion of patients with disease who test positive, representing the true positive rate of the test","B":"The proportion of patients without disease who test negative, representing the true negative rate of the test","C":"The probability that a positive result correctly identifies a patient who truly has the disease in question","D":"The ratio of true positives to the sum of true positives and false positives for a given diagnostic test","E":"The overall proportion of correct results from a test, including both true positives and true negatives combined"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitivity measures how well a test detects disease: it equals true positives divided by all patients with the disease. The answer describing true negatives over all without disease defines specificity. Positive predictive value is true positives over all positive results. Accuracy is total correct results over all tested. Sensitivity is critical for ruling out disease; a highly sensitive test rarely misses cases.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sensitivity = TP / TP + FN; a sensitive test rules OUT disease when negative (SnNout). COMLEX: High-sensitivity tests are preferred when missing a diagnosis causes serious harm.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A screening test for rheumatoid arthritis has a sensitivity of 60% and a specificity of 80% in a studied population. Which of the following values most closely represents the positive likelihood ratio of this test?","choices":{"A":"0.5, consistent with a test that performs no better than random chance in this patient population setting","B":"8.0, consistent with good diagnostic performance and a meaningful shift in the probability of disease","C":"12.0, consistent with excellent diagnostic performance and a large definitive shift in the post-test probability","D":"3.0, consistent with fair to poor diagnostic performance by standard likelihood ratio interpretation guidelines","E":"0.2, consistent with the negative likelihood ratio value, which reflects performance when the test result is negative"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The positive likelihood ratio equals sensitivity divided by one minus specificity. With 60% sensitivity and 80% specificity, LR+ = 0.60 / (1 - 0.80) = 0.60 / 0.20 = 3.0. A LR+ below 5 indicates fair to poor performance. LR+ of 5 to 10 indicates good performance, and LR+ of 10 or greater indicates excellent performance. The 0.2 choice is not this test's negative likelihood ratio either: LR− = (1 − 0.60) / 0.80 = 0.50.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LR+ = Sensitivity / (1 - Specificity). LR+ greater than 10 = excellent; 5 to 10 = good; below 5 = fair to poor. COMLEX: These formulas appear on COMLEX-1 in biostatistics clinical vignettes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements about likelihood ratios correctly characterizes a test with excellent diagnostic performance?","choices":{"A":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 5 and the negative likelihood ratio is 0.1 to 0.2, indicating good performance overall","B":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 3 and the negative likelihood ratio is 0.2 to 0.5, indicating fair to poor performance","C":"The positive likelihood ratio is at least 10 and the negative likelihood ratio is less than 0.1, indicating excellent performance","D":"The positive likelihood ratio equals 1.0, indicating that a positive result produces no change in the pretest probability","E":"The positive likelihood ratio is greater than 2 and the negative likelihood ratio is below 0.5, indicating moderate performance"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Excellent test performance is defined by a positive LR of 10 or greater combined with a negative LR below 0.1. A positive LR of 5 to 10 and negative LR of 0.1 to 0.2 represents good performance. Values below these thresholds represent fair to poor performance. A LR+ of 1.0 means the test result does not change the pre-test probability at all, making it diagnostically worthless.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Memorize LR thresholds: LR+ more than 10 and LR minus less than 0.1 is excellent. COMLEX: Likelihood ratios are independent of disease prevalence, unlike predictive values.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 29 → Obj 3 — Sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio govern the interpretation of all rheumatologic laboratory tests; pretest probability and disease prevalence determine whether a positive result meaningfully shifts post-test odds in clinical decision making."},{"id":"sdl44_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the difference in response kinetics between erythrocyte sedimentation rate and C-reactive protein during inflammatory rheumatic disease?","choices":{"A":"CRP is the primary marker of bone marrow function and is superior to ESR for monitoring drug-induced cytopenias during DMARD therapy","B":"ESR is preferred over CRP for detecting infection in patients receiving IL-6 receptor antagonists because it is unaffected by cytokine pathway inhibition","C":"ESR is produced directly by hepatocytes in response to IL-6, whereas CRP is determined by red blood cell aggregation caused by plasma proteins","D":"CRP values are physiologically higher in women than in men and increase with age and during pregnancy, while ESR remains constant across groups","E":"ESR changes slowly in response to both increases and decreases in inflammation, whereas CRP rises and falls more rapidly as disease activity fluctuates"},"correct":"E","explanation":"CRP is directly synthesized by hepatocytes under IL-6 stimulation and peaks and troughs rapidly with disease activity changes. ESR reflects erythrocyte aggregation driven by fibrinogen and immunoglobulins and is much slower to change. It is ESR that is physiologically higher in women, older adults, and pregnant patients. CRP may stay normal despite infection in patients on IL-6 receptor blockers such as tocilizumab; ESR, which depends on IL-6-driven fibrinogen, is not a safe substitute, so clinicians rely on clinical examination and imaging in that setting. CRP is a liver-made acute-phase protein that tracks inflammation, not bone marrow function, so it cannot monitor DMARD cytopenias.","boardPrep":"Step 1: CRP is the better real-time inflammatory marker; ESR lags. Both are elevated in PMR and GCA. COMLEX: Marked ESR elevation is a classic PMR finding; the ESR is below 40 mm/h in only a minority (5 to 20 percent) of patients (course SDL 39).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with a 15-year history of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents with recurrent bacterial infections. CBC shows a white blood cell count of 2,100 per microliter with an absolute neutrophil count of 800. Physical examination reveals firm splenomegaly without lymphadenopathy. Which of the following is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":{"A":"Drug-induced bone marrow suppression from disease-modifying antirheumatic drug therapy, causing global cytopenias requiring immediate dose reduction","B":"Systemic lupus erythematosus, which can cause neutropenia and splenomegaly through anti-neutrophil antibodies and immune complex deposition","C":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis, which characteristically produces marked eosinophilia rather than neutropenia in affected patients","D":"Large granular lymphocyte syndrome unrelated to rheumatoid arthritis, presenting with isolated neutropenia and splenomegaly without autoantibodies","E":"Felty syndrome, defined by the triad of seropositive rheumatoid arthritis, neutropenia, and splenomegaly, often with recurrent bacterial infections"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Felty syndrome is the classic triad of long-standing seropositive RA, neutropenia, and splenomegaly. Neutropenia in RA in the absence of splenomegaly raises concern for drug toxicity. Neutropenia plus eosinophilia in another clinical context suggests Churg-Strauss. Large granular lymphocyte syndrome can mimic Felty but lacks the RA background. Drug toxicity typically causes pancytopenia rather than isolated neutropenia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Felty syndrome = seropositive RA plus neutropenia plus splenomegaly; neutropenia predisposes to recurrent skin and pulmonary infections. COMLEX: Associated with high RF titers and HLA-DR4.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A patient receiving tocilizumab for rheumatoid arthritis develops a temperature of 38.9 degrees Celsius, tachycardia, and purulent sputum, but the CRP level returns at 0.4 mg per liter. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this discrepancy?","choices":{"A":"Tocilizumab depletes circulating B lymphocytes, reducing immunoglobulin production and thereby blunting all inflammatory marker production","B":"Tocilizumab directly neutralizes tumor necrosis factor-alpha, the primary cytokine responsible for CRP induction during bacterial infection","C":"Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor on hepatocytes, suppressing CRP synthesis even in the presence of active bacterial infection","D":"CRP is physiologically lower in all patients on long-term immunosuppression because chronic therapy suppresses baseline hepatic acute-phase responses","E":"Tocilizumab increases IL-10 production, which selectively counteracts IL-6 at the receptor level without affecting ESR or serum ferritin levels"},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-6 is the primary driver of hepatic CRP synthesis. Tocilizumab blocks the IL-6 receptor, preventing CRP production regardless of the infectious stimulus. This creates a dangerous clinical gap where CRP cannot be relied upon to detect infection, and clinicians must depend on clinical examination, procalcitonin, and imaging. Tocilizumab does not deplete B cells or block TNF-alpha; rituximab depletes B cells and adalimumab blocks TNF-alpha.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-6 drives CRP; tocilizumab blocks this. CRP may be normal during infection in patients on IL-6 inhibitors. COMLEX: A common RA management clinical question involving monitoring for infectious complications on biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 37 → Obj 2 — IL-6 is the primary hepatic stimulus for CRP synthesis and fibrinogen production, linking the pro-inflammatory cytokine milieu of RA and other rheumatic diseases to the acute-phase reactants used to monitor disease activity and drug toxicity."},{"id":"sdl44_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following rheumatic diseases has the highest reported sensitivity for antinuclear antibody detection by indirect immunofluorescence on HEp-2 cells?","choices":{"A":"Mixed connective tissue disease, with antinuclear antibody positivity approaching 100% because high-titer anti-U1 RNP is a diagnostic prerequisite","B":"Systemic sclerosis, with antinuclear antibody positivity of 60 to 80% and frequent anticentromere or anti-Scl-70 specificity","C":"Drug-induced lupus erythematosus, with antinuclear antibody positivity of approximately 90% and predominantly anti-histone antibody specificity","D":"Sjögren syndrome, with antinuclear antibody positivity of 40 to 70% and predominantly anti-Ro and anti-La antibody specificity on testing","E":"Pauciarticular juvenile chronic arthritis, with antinuclear antibody positivity of approximately 70% in the pauciarticular subtype specifically"},"correct":"A","explanation":"According to published reference data, mixed connective tissue disease has a 100% sensitivity for ANA because high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies, a prerequisite for diagnosis, invariably produce a positive ANA result. Systemic sclerosis is 60 to 80%, drug-induced lupus approximately 90%, and pauciarticular JCA approximately 70%. Sjögren syndrome has the lowest sensitivity among these at 40 to 70%. Knowing the hierarchy of ANA sensitivity by disease is high-yield for USMLE question stems.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ANA sensitivity hierarchy: MCTD 100% then SLE 95-100% then drug-induced lupus 90%. ANA is a screening tool, not diagnostic. COMLEX: A positive ANA requires reflex testing for anti-dsDNA, anti-Sm, anti-Ro, anti-La.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following statements about anti-Smith antibodies best describes their diagnostic utility in systemic lupus erythematosus?","choices":{"A":"Anti-Sm antibodies are the most sensitive serologic marker for SLE, detectable in over 90% of patients during active disease flares and remission","B":"Anti-Sm antibodies are highly specific for SLE but have low sensitivity of 10 to 40%, so a negative result does not exclude the diagnosis","C":"Anti-Sm antibodies are the defining serologic prerequisite for diagnosing mixed connective tissue disease when present at high titers","D":"Anti-Sm antibodies are associated with subacute cutaneous lupus, photosensitivity, and congenital heart block through transplacental transfer","E":"Anti-Sm antibodies produce a centromere staining pattern on HEp-2 cells and are specifically associated with limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Anti-Sm antibodies target the spliceosome and produce a coarse speckled ANA pattern. They are found almost exclusively in SLE, making them highly specific, but they are present in only 10 to 40% of SLE patients, limiting their sensitivity. The prerequisite antibody for MCTD is anti-U1 RNP, not anti-Sm. Anti-Ro and anti-La are associated with subacute cutaneous lupus and neonatal lupus. Anticentromere antibodies are specific to limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Sm = very specific for SLE, low sensitivity; positive result is strong evidence for SLE but a negative does not exclude it. COMLEX: Included in EULAR/ACR SLE classification criteria as a domain item.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following clinical phenotypes is most strongly associated with anti-Scl-70 antibodies in patients with systemic sclerosis?","choices":{"A":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis with isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension and anticentromere antibody co-positivity in the same patient","B":"Mixed connective tissue disease with overlapping features of systemic sclerosis and lupus driven by high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies","C":"Primary Sjögren syndrome with predominant glandular involvement, dry eyes, dry mouth, and anti-Ro and anti-La antibodies as driving markers","D":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with interstitial lung disease involving pulmonary parenchymal fibrosis and restrictive physiology","E":"Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis presenting with asthma, eosinophilia, and perinuclear ANCA as the primary serologic finding"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Anti-Scl-70 targets topoisomerase I and is associated with diffuse cutaneous SSc and pulmonary parenchymal interstitial lung disease. By contrast, anticentromere antibodies are associated with limited SSc and pulmonary arterial hypertension. These two antibodies rarely occur in the same patient. Anti-Scl-70 and anticentromere represent distinct clinical endotypes of SSc. Limited SSc is defined as skin involvement distal to the elbows and knees, while diffuse SSc extends proximal to these landmarks. Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis is an MPO-ANCA (p-ANCA) vasculitis with asthma and eosinophilia, not a scleroderma phenotype.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anti-Scl-70 equals diffuse SSc plus ILD; anticentromere equals limited SSc plus PAH. These two antibodies rarely co-occur in the same patient. COMLEX: Limited SSc features CREST syndrome: Calcinosis, Raynaud, Esophageal dysmotility, Sclerodactyly, Telangiectasias.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Autoantibodies arise from failed central and peripheral self-tolerance; their specificity for individual diseases reflects unique antigen presentations in each condition, making antibody-disease correlation a core rheumatologic diagnostic skill."},{"id":"sdl44_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old woman with fatigue and diffuse arthralgias for four months is referred to rheumatology after a positive antinuclear antibody test at a titer of 1:80. The prevalence of SLE in the general population is approximately 1 in 2,000, and 1 in 20 healthy individuals test positive for ANA at this titer. She has no rash, no serositis, and no renal symptoms. Which of the following best explains why this positive ANA result has a low positive predictive value in this clinical scenario?","choices":{"A":"ANA testing by HEp-2 immunofluorescence is poorly standardized across laboratory platforms, making all positive results unreliable and clinically uninterpretable","B":"Low disease prevalence means that even a highly sensitive test will generate substantially more false positives than true positives in an unselected population","C":"A titer of 1:80 falls below the diagnostic threshold of 1:320 required for antinuclear antibodies to carry any clinical significance in autoimmune evaluation","D":"Positive predictive value is artificially elevated when tests are ordered in referral populations, so this result actually represents a stronger diagnostic signal than it appears","E":"Antinuclear antibody sensitivity for SLE is below 50%, making it an inappropriate first-line screening test and causing frequent false-positive results"},"correct":"B","explanation":"PPV depends on both test performance and disease prevalence. When SLE has a prevalence of 1 in 2,000 but 1 in 20 healthy individuals test ANA positive, the vast majority of positive results will be false positives. This is a fundamental Bayesian principle: the lower the pretest probability, the lower the PPV even for a highly sensitive test. ANA sensitivity for SLE is 95 to 100%, so it is a valid screening tool, but its low specificity in general populations dilutes PPV significantly.","boardPrep":"Step 1: PPV decreases when prevalence falls, even if sensitivity and specificity are constant. ANA is a screening test, not diagnostic. COMLEX: A positive ANA in a patient with low pretest probability for SLE requires reflex testing and clinical correlation, not immediate diagnosis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 44-year-old man with inflammatory low back pain, anterior uveitis, and restricted lumbar flexion is tested for HLA-B27. The test has a sensitivity of 90% for ankylosing spondylitis and a specificity of 94% in non-diseased controls in this population. Which of the following correctly calculates the positive likelihood ratio for this test?","choices":{"A":"LR+ equals 0.90 multiplied by 0.94, yielding a value of 0.85, which represents the combined probability of correct classification across all results","B":"LR+ equals 1 minus 0.90 divided by 0.94, yielding a value of 0.11, which actually represents the negative likelihood ratio for this test","C":"LR+ equals 0.94 divided by 1 minus 0.90, yielding a value of 9.4, which overestimates performance by inverting sensitivity and specificity","D":"LR+ equals 0.90 plus 0.94 divided by 2, yielding 0.92, which represents the arithmetic mean of sensitivity and specificity rather than LR+","E":"LR+ equals 0.90 divided by 1 minus 0.94, yielding a value of 15.0, which indicates excellent diagnostic performance by standard thresholds"},"correct":"E","explanation":"LR+ = sensitivity / (1 - specificity) = 0.90 / (1 - 0.94) = 0.90 / 0.06 = 15.0, indicating excellent performance. LR minus = (1 - sensitivity) / specificity = 0.10 / 0.94 = 0.11, indicating excellent negative performance as well. Multiplying sensitivity by specificity is not a valid formula for any standard diagnostic test metric. An LR+ of 15 means that a positive HLA-B27 result shifts the odds of ankylosing spondylitis 15-fold, making it clinically very meaningful in the appropriate setting.","boardPrep":"Step 1: LR+ formula and interpretation are directly tested. HLA-B27 is positive in 90% of ankylosing spondylitis patients and 6% of the general population, making it a useful but not definitive test. COMLEX: HLA-B27 is associated with the seronegative spondyloarthropathies, which are a COMLEX Clinical Presentation category.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Explain the concept of sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratio in the use of laboratory testing for rheumatic diseases.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A rheumatology fellow applies an anti-dsDNA assay with 95% specificity and 70% sensitivity to two different populations: a high-risk SLE referral clinic where SLE prevalence is 20%, and a general primary care population where SLE prevalence is 0.1%. Which of the following best predicts how test performance will differ between these two settings?","choices":{"A":"The positive predictive value will be substantially higher in the SLE referral clinic, even though sensitivity and specificity remain identical across both populations","B":"The sensitivity and specificity of the anti-dsDNA assay will increase in the higher-prevalence SLE clinic because more true positives are present to anchor both values","C":"The positive predictive value will be identical in both populations because it is calculated from the same sensitivity and specificity in both settings","D":"The likelihood ratio will increase in the SLE referral clinic, making it the most appropriate statistic for comparing test utility across populations","E":"The negative predictive value will be substantially lower in the primary care population because fewer patients without disease are available for calculation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Sensitivity and specificity are intrinsic properties of the test and do not change with prevalence. However, PPV increases as prevalence increases because there are proportionally more true positives relative to false positives. In a population with 20% SLE prevalence, a positive anti-dsDNA result is highly likely to represent true disease. In a 0.1% prevalence population, most positive results are false positives. Likelihood ratios are also prevalence-independent and would remain constant across both settings.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sensitivity and specificity are stable across populations; PPV and NPV change with prevalence. This is one of the most tested biostatistics concepts on USMLE Step 1. COMLEX: Applied to rheumatologic testing in the context of appropriate test ordering and clinical reasoning.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 29 Obj 3 — Diagnostic Laboratory Testing for Joint Effusions"},{"id":"sdl44_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with known SLE presents with facial edema, hematuria, and 3+ proteinuria. Laboratory evaluation reveals anti-dsDNA titer of 1:640, C3 of 45 mg per dL, C4 of 5 mg per dL, and creatinine of 1.9 mg per dL, consistent with active lupus nephritis. With successful immunosuppressive treatment of the acute flare, which of the following laboratory changes would most reliably indicate improvement?","choices":{"A":"Rising rheumatoid factor titers with normalization of complement levels, indicating immune reconstitution independent of SLE disease activity","B":"Falling ESR with persistently elevated CRP, reflecting resolution of the acute-phase response despite ongoing nephritis at the cellular level","C":"Normalization of ANA titer from 1:640 to negative, since ANA levels track directly and predictably with SLE disease activity during nephritis flares","D":"Rising C3 and C4 with falling anti-dsDNA titers, reflecting decreased immune complex deposition and reduced complement pathway consumption","E":"Rising anti-Sm titers alongside decreasing anti-dsDNA, indicating a shift toward a less nephrotoxic autoantibody specificity as nephritis resolves"},"correct":"D","explanation":"In active lupus nephritis, DNA-anti-dsDNA immune complexes deposit in the glomeruli, activate complement, and consume C3 and C4. Successful treatment reduces immune complex formation, allowing complement levels to normalize and anti-dsDNA titers to fall. ANA titers do not reliably track with disease activity; ANA can remain positive even in remission. Anti-Sm titers do not correlate with lupus nephritis activity. RF is not a marker of SLE disease activity.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Active lupus nephritis = elevated anti-dsDNA plus low C3 and C4 plus active urinary sediment. Normalization of complement with falling anti-dsDNA tracks treatment response. COMLEX: Lupus nephritis is a Type III hypersensitivity injury from immune complex deposition in glomeruli.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with seropositive rheumatoid arthritis is started on methotrexate 15 mg weekly along with folic acid supplementation. He returns for follow-up after 8 weeks of therapy. Which of the following laboratory tests is most essential to monitor at regular intervals throughout this treatment course?","choices":{"A":"Serum uric acid levels and synovial fluid crystal analysis, to detect methotrexate-induced hyperuricemia and drug-related crystal arthropathy","B":"Complete blood count and hepatic aminotransferases, with methotrexate stopped if AST or ALT rises above three times the upper limit of normal","C":"Complement C3 and C4 levels alongside anti-dsDNA titers, to exclude the development of methotrexate-induced lupus erythematosus during therapy","D":"Serum ferritin and S100 protein levels as markers of macrophage activation syndrome, which can be triggered by methotrexate in susceptible patients","E":"Antinuclear antibody and anti-histone antibody testing, to detect methotrexate-induced autoimmunity and drug-induced lupus in this patient"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Methotrexate requires monitoring for hepatotoxicity, bone marrow suppression, and renal function because impaired renal clearance increases methotrexate accumulation and toxicity. AST and ALT should be monitored, and the drug should be stopped if levels exceed three times the upper limit of normal. CBC monitors for macrocytosis and cytopenias from folate antagonism. Methotrexate does not cause crystal arthropathy or complement depletion. Drug-induced lupus from methotrexate is extremely rare and anti-histone testing is not standard monitoring.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Methotrexate monitoring: CBC for cytopenias plus liver function tests for hepatotoxicity plus creatinine for renal clearance. Folic acid supplementation reduces mucosal and hematologic toxicity with only a modest (10–18%) loss of efficacy. COMLEX: Methotrexate toxicity is worsened by NSAIDs, alcohol, and renal impairment.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Understand the commonly used laboratory tests to aid in evaluation of patients with rheumatic disease (CBC, CMP, ESR, CRP).","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 61-year-old woman with long-standing seropositive rheumatoid arthritis presents for routine follow-up. CBC shows hemoglobin of 10.1 g per dL with a mean corpuscular volume of 83 fL. Iron studies show low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, and markedly elevated ferritin. Which of the following mechanisms best explains this pattern?","choices":{"A":"NSAID-induced gastrointestinal bleeding causes true iron deficiency anemia, which presents with low serum iron, low ferritin, and elevated total iron-binding capacity","B":"Methotrexate-induced folate deficiency causes macrocytic anemia with megaloblastic changes on peripheral smear and elevated mean corpuscular volume","C":"Chronic inflammation-driven hepcidin excess impairs macrophage iron release and reduces intestinal iron absorption, producing functional iron-restricted erythropoiesis","D":"Antierythrocyte antibodies in rheumatoid arthritis cause hemolytic anemia with low haptoglobin, elevated indirect bilirubin, and positive direct Coombs test","E":"Renal amyloidosis from long-standing RA reduces erythropoietin production, causing normocytic anemia with low reticulocyte count and normal iron stores"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Anemia of chronic inflammation involves IL-6-driven hepcidin production by the liver. Hepcidin binds and degrades ferroportin, trapping iron inside macrophages and enterocytes and reducing circulating iron available for erythropoiesis. This produces a normocytic or mildly microcytic anemia with low serum iron, low transferrin saturation, but elevated ferritin because iron is sequestered, not depleted. True iron deficiency anemia would show low ferritin and elevated TIBC. Folate deficiency from methotrexate produces macrocytic anemia.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anemia of chronic disease features low serum iron plus low TIBC plus elevated ferritin, distinguishing it from iron deficiency where ferritin is low and TIBC is high. COMLEX: Hepcidin is the master iron regulator; chronic inflammation elevates it via IL-6.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 37 Obj 2 — Pro-inflammatory Cytokines Involved in Rheumatoid Inflammation"},{"id":"sdl44_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old woman with a 10-year history of Raynaud phenomenon, skin tightening limited to the hands and face, and telangiectasias is found to have anticentromere antibodies on ANA reflex testing. Which of the following disease associations and complication risks is most consistent with this antibody profile?","choices":{"A":"Limited cutaneous systemic sclerosis with elevated risk of isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension rather than pulmonary parenchymal fibrosis","B":"Diffuse cutaneous systemic sclerosis with severe interstitial lung disease and likely co-positivity of anti-Scl-70 antibodies in the same patient","C":"Primary Sjögren syndrome with predominant glandular lymphocytic infiltration, dry eyes, dry mouth, and anti-Ro and anti-La as the primary antibodies","D":"Mixed connective tissue disease with overlapping features of sclerosis, lupus, and myositis driven by high-titer anti-U1 RNP antibodies on ELISA","E":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis with upper respiratory destruction, saddle-nose deformity, and proteinase-3 ANCA as the primary serologic marker"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Anticentromere antibodies are strongly associated with limited cutaneous SSc, characterized by skin involvement distal to elbows and knees, Raynaud phenomenon, telangiectasias, and a high risk of isolated pulmonary arterial hypertension. In contrast, anti-Scl-70 antibodies target topoisomerase I and are associated with diffuse SSc and interstitial pulmonary fibrosis. These two antibodies rarely occur in the same patient and represent distinct SSc endotypes with different organ complication profiles. Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is a PR3-ANCA (c-ANCA) vasculitis, unrelated to anticentromere antibodies.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Anticentromere = limited SSc plus PAH risk; anti-Scl-70 = diffuse SSc plus ILD risk. These two antibody types rarely overlap. COMLEX: Limited SSc features the CREST syndrome constellation and a better prognosis than diffuse SSc.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl44_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman presents with her third first-trimester pregnancy loss. Coagulation studies show a prolonged activated partial thromboplastin time that does not correct on a 1:1 mixing study with normal plasma. Anticardiolipin IgG antibodies are markedly elevated and have been confirmed on repeat testing 12 weeks apart. Which of the following best explains the paradox of a prolonged clotting assay with a clinically hypercoagulable phenotype?","choices":{"A":"Antiphospholipid antibodies directly activate intrinsic coagulation cascade factors, causing both laboratory clotting factor consumption and clinical arterial thrombosis","B":"Factor VIII deficiency co-existing with antiphospholipid syndrome causes a prolonged aPTT that corrects on mixing study and produces bleeding rather than thrombosis","C":"Lupus anticoagulant blocks phospholipid-dependent coagulation complexes in vitro, prolonging clotting assays, while promoting thrombin generation and clotting in vivo","D":"Antiphospholipid antibodies activate complement on platelet surfaces, simultaneously causing thrombocytopenia and prolonged clotting times through platelet depletion","E":"Lupus anticoagulant blocks thrombomodulin on endothelial cells, reducing protein C activation and causing both laboratory assay prolongation and in vivo thrombosis"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Lupus anticoagulant antibodies bind to phospholipid-protein complexes in the test tube, blocking the assembly of the prothrombinase complex and prolonging phospholipid-dependent clotting assays such as the aPTT. However, in vivo these antibodies promote a prothrombotic state by interfering with anticoagulant mechanisms. The failure to correct on a 1:1 mixing study distinguishes lupus anticoagulant from factor deficiencies, which would correct. Antiphospholipid syndrome diagnoses require confirmed positive testing on repeat occasions.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Lupus anticoagulant paradox: prolonged aPTT in vitro plus thrombosis in vivo. Does not correct on mixing study. COMLEX: Antiphospholipid syndrome triad: thrombosis plus pregnancy loss plus positive antiphospholipid antibodies on two occasions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl44_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Describe the autoantibodies in rheumatic disease as they associate with specific conditions. Understand the antibodies which are specific, non-specific, and those that are sensitive for diagnosis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old man presents with saddle-nose deformity, chronic sinusitis with bloody nasal discharge, hemoptysis, and hematuria with red blood cell casts on urinalysis. ANCA testing shows a coarse granular fluorescence in the cytoplasm of ethanol-fixed neutrophils, which is confirmed by ELISA targeting a specific serine protease. Which of the following combinations of ANCA pattern and target antigen is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against myeloperoxidase, characteristic of microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis","B":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against proteinase-3, seen in a subset of granulomatosis with polyangiitis patients when formalin-fixed cells are used","C":"Cytoplasmic ANCA pattern directed against myeloperoxidase, associated with drug-induced vasculitis and atypical ANCA-associated disease presentations","D":"Perinuclear ANCA pattern directed against lactoferrin, a nonspecific finding associated with inflammatory bowel disease rather than systemic vasculitis","E":"Cytoplasmic ANCA pattern directed against proteinase-3, characteristic of granulomatosis with polyangiitis affecting upper airways, lungs, and kidneys"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Granulomatosis with polyangiitis is characterized by c-ANCA targeting proteinase-3. The cytoplasmic fluorescence pattern results from the diffuse distribution of PR3 in neutrophil cytoplasm. Microscopic polyangiitis and eosinophilic GPA are associated with p-ANCA targeting myeloperoxidase. The saddle-nose deformity, upper and lower respiratory tract involvement, and glomerulonephritis with RBC casts are the classic clinical triad of GPA. ANCA testing guidelines now recommend direct ELISA for MPO and PR3 as the primary method rather than IIF screening.","boardPrep":"Step 1: c-ANCA plus anti-PR3 equals GPA; p-ANCA plus anti-MPO equals MPA or eGPA. GPA classically involves upper respiratory tract, lungs, and kidneys. COMLEX: ANCA-associated vasculitides cause pauci-immune glomerulonephritis with few immune deposits on immunofluorescence.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"}],"45":[{"id":"sdl45_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following agents is correctly classified as a conventional synthetic DMARD?","choices":{"A":"Adalimumab, a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant DNA technology that neutralizes both soluble and membrane-bound TNF-alpha","B":"Methotrexate, a chemically synthesized small-molecule anti-metabolite administered orally or subcutaneously that inhibits folate-dependent enzymes","C":"Abatacept, a soluble CTLA-4 fusion protein produced by recombinant DNA technology that blocks CD28-mediated costimulation of T lymphocytes","D":"Tocilizumab, a humanized monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant technology that blocks both soluble and membrane-bound IL-6 receptors","E":"Rituximab, a chimeric anti-CD20 monoclonal antibody produced by recombinant technology that depletes mature B cells through multiple cytotoxic mechanisms"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Conventional synthetic DMARDs are chemically synthesized small molecules, including methotrexate, leflunomide, hydroxychloroquine, sulfasalazine, azathioprine, mycophenolate mofetil, and cyclophosphamide. Biological DMARDs are large proteins produced by recombinant DNA technology and include TNF inhibitors, IL-6 receptor antagonists, abatacept, rituximab, and belimumab. JAK inhibitors are targeted synthetic DMARDs, a distinct subclass of small-molecule agents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: csDMARDs = small molecules; bDMARDs = recombinant proteins. Methotrexate is the anchor csDMARD for RA. COMLEX: Methotrexate is the first-line csDMARD for RA; triple therapy (methotrexate plus hydroxychloroquine plus sulfasalazine) is a combination option.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the fundamental distinction between biological DMARDs and conventional synthetic DMARDs?","choices":{"A":"Biological DMARDs are proteins produced by recombinant technology that target immune mediators, whereas conventional synthetic DMARDs are chemically synthesized small molecules","B":"Biological DMARDs are exclusively given intravenously and always require infusion center administration, while conventional synthetic DMARDs are always taken as daily oral tablets","C":"Biological DMARDs have shorter half-lives than conventional synthetic DMARDs and require more frequent daily dosing to maintain adequate therapeutic levels","D":"Biological DMARDs act broadly across all inflammatory pathways without selectivity, while conventional synthetic DMARDs each target only a single cytokine or receptor","E":"Conventional synthetic DMARDs require mandatory tuberculosis screening before initiation, while biological DMARDs carry no risk of infectious reactivation"},"correct":"A","explanation":"The defining distinction is method of production and molecular size. Biological DMARDs are recombinant proteins targeting specific immune molecules such as TNF-alpha, IL-6 receptors, CD20, CD28, or BLyS. Conventional synthetic DMARDs are small chemically synthesized molecules with broader metabolic mechanisms. Latent TB screening is required before biologics (and JAK inhibitors), which can reactivate infection. Many biologics are available subcutaneously as well as intravenously.","boardPrep":"Step 1: bDMARDs target specific immune proteins; csDMARDs are synthetic small molecules. Screen for latent TB and viral hepatitis before starting a biologic or JAK inhibitor. COMLEX: Biosimilar bDMARDs have equivalent efficacy and toxicity to original biologics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Tofacitinib is approved for rheumatoid arthritis and was initially marketed alongside biological agents. It is correctly classified as which type of DMARD?","choices":{"A":"A conventional synthetic DMARD equivalent in classification to methotrexate and leflunomide, because it is not produced by recombinant DNA technology","B":"A biosimilar DMARD approved as a lower-cost alternative to abatacept, sharing its mechanism of T cell costimulation blockade at reduced production cost","C":"A targeted synthetic DMARD, because it is a chemically synthesized small molecule that selectively inhibits JAK kinases rather than a recombinant protein","D":"A biological original DMARD produced by recombinant DNA technology, because it was the first agent approved specifically targeting the JAK-STAT pathway","E":"A hybrid DMARD occupying a distinct regulatory class between conventional synthetic and biological agents defined by its intracellular enzymatic mechanism"},"correct":"C","explanation":"JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, baricitinib, upadacitinib) are targeted synthetic DMARDs because they are small molecules synthesized chemically, not recombinant proteins. Despite targeting specific intracellular kinases, they are chemically rather than biologically derived. This places them in their own tsDMARD category distinct from both csDMARDs and bDMARDs. They are not biosimilars, which are approved copies of existing biologic reference products.","boardPrep":"Step 1: tsDMARDs = small-molecule targeted agents; JAK inhibitors are the main tsDMARD class in rheumatology. COMLEX: JAK inhibitors carry FDA black box warnings for VTE, MACE, and malignancy not shared with all other DMARD classes.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 28 → Obj 4 — T and B cell activation mechanisms underpin the targets of biological DMARDs: abatacept blocks CD28 costimulation, rituximab depletes CD20-positive B cells, and belimumab blocks BLyS-dependent B cell survival, while conventional synthetic DMARDs use small-molecule chemistry to broadly suppress proliferating immune cells."},{"id":"sdl45_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best describes the primary mechanism of action of low-dose methotrexate in the treatment of rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of dihydrofolate reductase as the primary mechanism at rheumatic doses, leading to reduced tetrahydrofolate and impaired nucleotide synthesis","B":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, selectively blocking guanosine synthesis and preferentially suppressing T and B lymphocyte proliferation","C":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, reducing pyrimidine synthesis and arresting proliferating lymphocytes in the G1 phase of the cell cycle","D":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase leading to extracellular AMP accumulation, suppressing neutrophil, macrophage, dendritic cell, and lymphocyte function","E":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase, preventing NFAT nuclear translocation and selectively reducing IL-2, IL-3, IL-4, and TNF-alpha gene transcription"},"correct":"D","explanation":"At low rheumatic doses, methotrexate primarily inhibits AICAR transformylase and thymidylate synthetase, leading to extracellular adenosine accumulation. Adenosine is broadly anti-inflammatory, suppressing neutrophil chemotaxis and macrophage, dendritic cell, and lymphocyte function. The dihydrofolate reductase mechanism is relatively minor at these doses and is more relevant to oncologic uses. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase is inhibited by leflunomide; IMPDH is inhibited by mycophenolate; calcineurin is inhibited by cyclosporine and tacrolimus.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX at low rheumatic doses works via AICAR/adenosine accumulation, not primarily DHFR. Folic acid supplementation reduces toxicity with minimal efficacy loss. COMLEX: MTX is contraindicated in pregnancy; use teratogenicity counseling.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism by which mycophenolate mofetil exerts its immunosuppressive effects?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase, leading to extracellular AMP accumulation that broadly suppresses neutrophil chemotaxis and macrophage activation","B":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase via the active metabolite A77 1726, blocking pyrimidine synthesis and arresting lymphocytes in G1 phase","C":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, blocking guanosine nucleotide synthesis and selectively suppressing T and B lymphocyte proliferation","D":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase activity, preventing NFAT nuclear translocation and reducing transcription of IL-2 and other T cell survival cytokines","E":"Blockade of CD28-mediated T cell costimulation by competitively binding CD80 and CD86 on antigen-presenting cells to prevent full T cell activation"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Mycophenolate mofetil is a prodrug converted to mycophenolic acid, which inhibits IMPDH. This selectively blocks de novo guanosine synthesis, which T and B lymphocytes depend on because they lack the salvage pathway used by other cells. Downstream, MMF also inhibits leukocyte adhesion by reducing E-selectin, P-selectin, and ICAM expression. AICAR transformylase is the methotrexate target. Dihydroorotate dehydrogenase is the leflunomide target. Calcineurin is blocked by cyclosporine and tacrolimus. CD28 blockade is abatacept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MMF = IMPDH inhibitor; selectively suppresses lymphocytes. Used for lupus nephritis and SSc ILD. COMLEX: MMF is contraindicated in pregnancy; switch to azathioprine for pregnant SLE patients.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the mechanism of action of leflunomide in rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Inhibition of CD20 on B cell surfaces, triggering complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity to deplete B lymphocytes","B":"Inhibition of AICAR transformylase leading to extracellular AMP accumulation that broadly suppresses innate and adaptive immune cell proliferation and function","C":"Inhibition of inosine monophosphate dehydrogenase, depleting guanosine nucleotides and preferentially blocking T and B lymphocyte de novo nucleotide synthesis","D":"Inhibition of calcineurin phosphatase, blocking NFAT nuclear translocation and selectively reducing transcription of IL-2 and pro-inflammatory cytokine genes","E":"Inhibition of dihydroorotate dehydrogenase via its active metabolite A77 1726, blocking pyrimidine de novo synthesis and arresting activated lymphocytes in G1 phase"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Leflunomide is a prodrug rapidly converted to its active metabolite A77 1726 in the intestines and plasma. A77 1726 inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase, the rate-limiting enzyme in pyrimidine synthesis, arresting proliferating cells including lymphocytes in G1. It also decreases TNF-alpha-dependent NF-kB activation. Leflunomide has approximately equal efficacy to methotrexate in RA. The other mechanisms described belong to methotrexate, mycophenolate, calcineurin inhibitors, and rituximab respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Leflunomide inhibits dihydroorotate dehydrogenase and equals MTX efficacy in RA. Active metabolite A77 1726 has a half-life of 19 days. COMLEX: Teratogenic; washout with cholestyramine required if pregnancy is planned.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 2 — Cytokine functions define the molecular rationale for biologic DMARD targets: TNF-alpha drives synovial inflammation in RA, IL-6 stimulates acute-phase proteins and osteoclastogenesis, IL-17 promotes neutrophil recruitment in spondyloarthritis, and IL-23 sustains Th17 differentiation in psoriatic disease."},{"id":"sdl45_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following adverse effects is covered by the FDA black box warning that applies to all three approved JAK inhibitors used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Reactivation of latent tuberculosis as the primary concern, requiring monthly chest radiograph monitoring and isoniazid prophylaxis in all patients","B":"Increased risk of serious infections, venous thromboembolism, major adverse cardiovascular events, and malignancy based on postmarketing safety data","C":"Hepatitis B reactivation causing fulminant hepatic failure, requiring serologic screening and prophylactic antiviral therapy before treatment initiation","D":"Progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy from JC virus reactivation, particularly in patients with long-term use and low CD4 lymphocyte counts","E":"Irreversible retinopathy causing central scotoma and color vision loss, requiring annual ophthalmologic examination with spectral-domain OCT imaging"},"correct":"B","explanation":"All JAK inhibitors carry an FDA black box warning for serious infections, VTE including DVT and PE, MACE, and malignancy. This originated from a large postmarketing study of tofacitinib that showed dose-dependent cardiovascular and thrombotic events. Herpes zoster reactivation is a notable infection risk for JAK inhibitors. TB screening is required before initiation, but TB reactivation is not the primary black box concern as it is for TNF inhibitors. Hepatitis B reactivation is the signature risk of rituximab. PML is associated with natalizumab. Retinopathy is the signature toxicity of hydroxychloroquine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: JAK inhibitor black box = VTE plus MACE plus malignancy plus serious infections. Herpes zoster risk is distinctively elevated versus other DMARD classes. COMLEX: JAK inhibitors require lipid monitoring due to dose-dependent LDL elevation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following correctly describes the most serious long-term adverse effect of hydroxychloroquine used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Nephrotoxicity with progressive glomerular filtration rate decline, requiring regular serum creatinine monitoring and dose adjustment in renal impairment","B":"Hepatotoxicity with elevation of aminotransferases above three times normal, requiring dose reduction and consideration of drug discontinuation","C":"Retinopathy causing irreversible parafoveal photoreceptor damage, occurring in less than 1% of patients at recommended doses within the first five years of use","D":"Bone marrow suppression causing neutropenia, thrombocytopenia, and macrocytic anemia requiring complete blood count monitoring at each clinical visit","E":"Pulmonary toxicity causing progressive interstitial pneumonitis, presenting with dyspnea, dry cough, and bilateral ground-glass infiltrates on chest CT"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Hydroxychloroquine retinopathy is dose-dependent. At recommended doses (5 to 6 mg per kg per day or less), retinopathy risk is below 1% within 5 years and below 2% within 10 years, increasing thereafter. Retinopathy is irreversible, so a baseline eye examination is followed by annual screening (automated visual fields and spectral-domain OCT) after 5 years of use. HCQ does not cause nephrotoxicity, hepatotoxicity at standard doses, or pulmonary toxicity. Bone marrow suppression is associated with methotrexate and azathioprine.","boardPrep":"Step 1: HCQ retinopathy is dose-dependent and irreversible; baseline eye exam, then annual screening with visual fields and OCT after 5 years. Safer in pregnancy than most other DMARDs. COMLEX: HCQ also improves lipid profiles and reduces cardiovascular risk in SLE and RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which of the following best summarizes the major class adverse effects shared by all five approved TNF-alpha inhibitors used in rheumatic diseases?","choices":{"A":"Venous thromboembolism, major adverse cardiovascular events, dose-dependent hyperlipidemia, and herpes zoster reactivation as the primary shared class risks","B":"Hepatitis B reactivation without risk of tuberculosis reactivation, progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy, and infusion-related hypersensitivity reactions","C":"Retinopathy causing parafoveal visual loss, drug-induced lupus with anti-histone antibodies, and reversible male infertility during active therapy","D":"Reactivation of latent tuberculosis and deep fungal infections, worsening or new-onset congestive heart failure, demyelinating disease, and lupus-like syndrome","E":"Injection-site reactions in up to 40% of treated patients, depression and suicidality, and gastrointestinal perforations in patients with prior diverticulitis"},"correct":"D","explanation":"TNF-alpha inhibitors share a class adverse effect profile including serious infections with particular risk of latent TB reactivation and deep fungal infections, worsening of CHF at moderate to severe grades, new or worsening demyelinating disease, possible lymphoma risk, headache, rash, and drug-induced lupus-like syndrome. VTE and MACE are the signature black box concern for JAK inhibitors, not TNF inhibitors. Hepatitis B reactivation without TB risk is characteristic of rituximab. Retinopathy is associated with hydroxychloroquine. Injection site reactions in 40% and depression are associated with IL-1 inhibitors and belimumab respectively.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF inhibitor class risks: TB reactivation plus fungal infections plus CHF worsening plus demyelination plus lupus-like syndrome. Screen all patients for latent TB before initiation. COMLEX: Contraindicated in moderate to severe CHF, active demyelinating disease, and active serious infection.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 1 — Adverse effects of DMARDs directly reflect disrupted immune tolerance mechanisms: TNF inhibitors reactivate latent TB by impairing granuloma maintenance, rituximab reactivates hepatitis B by depleting B cell immunity, and JAK inhibitors increase herpes zoster risk by impairing cytokine-dependent viral surveillance."},{"id":"sdl45_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old woman with recently diagnosed seropositive rheumatoid arthritis has active synovitis in multiple small joints, morning stiffness lasting 90 minutes, elevated CRP, and elevated anti-CCP titer. She has no prior DMARD exposure. Which of the following represents the most appropriate first-line disease-modifying treatment for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Methotrexate, the preferred first-line csDMARD for RA with efficacy in reducing synovitis, inhibiting joint erosion, and enabling biologic escalation","B":"Adalimumab, a TNF-alpha inhibitor biologic that is the most appropriate initial choice in newly diagnosed seropositive RA before any csDMARD trial","C":"Abatacept, a T cell costimulation blocker indicated as first-line monotherapy in seropositive RA due to its superior mechanism in ACPA-positive patients","D":"Rituximab, a B cell depleting biologic recommended as initial therapy in seropositive RA due to the pathogenic role of anti-CCP antibodies in this disease","E":"Tocilizumab, an IL-6 receptor antagonist preferred over methotrexate as initial therapy due to its rapid and superior normalization of inflammatory markers"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Methotrexate is the anchor csDMARD for RA and is the preferred first-line agent per ACR guidelines. It can be used as monotherapy or as the backbone for combination therapy with other csDMARDs or biologics. Biologic DMARDs such as TNF inhibitors, abatacept, rituximab, and IL-6 inhibitors are indicated for patients with inadequate response to methotrexate, not as first-line therapy. Triple csDMARD therapy with methotrexate, hydroxychloroquine, and sulfasalazine can match biologic efficacy in some patients.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX is the first-line csDMARD for RA. Biologics are added or substituted when csDMARD response is inadequate. COMLEX: 2015 ACR guidelines strongly recommend combination traditional DMARDs or addition of biologics for moderate-to-high disease activity.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms Involved in Activating T Cells and B Cells"},{"id":"sdl45_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 58-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has been stable on the original adalimumab product. Her insurer switches her to a biosimilar adalimumab. How is the new product classified among disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs?","choices":{"A":"A conventional synthetic DMARD, because biosimilars are made by chemical synthesis like methotrexate","B":"A targeted synthetic DMARD, because it is a copy that blocks a single cytokine like tofacitinib","C":"A biosimilar biologic DMARD, a subgroup of bDMARDs separate from the biological originator product","D":"A generic small-molecule DMARD, because biosimilars are identical copies of the reference drug","E":"A biological originator DMARD, because every adalimumab product keeps the original classification"},"correct":"C","explanation":"Biologic DMARDs are large-molecule agents, usually proteins made by recombinant DNA technology, and they are divided into biological originator (legacy) products, boDMARDs, and biosimilar DMARDs, bsDMARDs; a biosimilar adalimumab is a bsDMARD. Conventional synthetic DMARDs such as methotrexate are chemically synthesized small molecules. Tofacitinib is a small-molecule targeted synthetic DMARD that inhibits JAKs, not a copy of a protein. A biosimilar is a protein, not a small-molecule generic. The originator label belongs to the original product. Sources: course SDL 45 reading (Katzung Ch 36, Disease-modifying antirheumatic drugs, introduction).","boardPrep":"Step 1: csDMARDs = small molecules (methotrexate, leflunomide, sulfasalazine, hydroxychloroquine); tsDMARD = tofacitinib; bDMARDs = proteins such as abatacept, rituximab, tocilizumab, and TNF-alpha blockers (Katzung Ch 36). COMLEX: DMARD effects may take 2 weeks to 6 months to become clinically evident (Katzung Ch 36).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — classes and mechanisms of biologic DMARDs"},{"id":"sdl45_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Differentiate between the conventional synthetic DMARDs from the biological DMARDs.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with SLE and persistent active disease on hydroxychloroquine and mycophenolate mofetil is started on belimumab after two major flares in the past year. Which of the following correctly identifies belimumab's target and mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Belimumab binds the IL-6 receptor on B cells, blocking STAT3 activation and suppressing B cell differentiation into antibody-secreting plasma cells","B":"Belimumab is a CTLA-4 fusion protein that blocks CD28 costimulation on autoreactive T helper cells, preventing their activation by self-antigen-loaded APCs","C":"Belimumab blocks the type I interferon receptor on plasmacytoid dendritic cells, reducing IFN-alpha-driven upregulation of toll-like receptor pathways","D":"Belimumab is an anti-CD20 antibody that selectively depletes mature circulating B cells while preserving naive and transitional B cell populations","E":"Belimumab is a fully human IgG1 antibody that inhibits soluble B-lymphocyte stimulator, reducing B cell survival, maturation, and autoantibody production"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Belimumab is a fully human IgG1 monoclonal antibody that selectively inhibits soluble B-lymphocyte stimulator protein, also called BLyS or BAFF. BLyS promotes B cell survival, differentiation, and class switching. By blocking BLyS, belimumab reduces B cell counts, lowers autoantibody titers, and decreases complement consumption in SLE. It is approved for SLE and active lupus nephritis in patients on background standard-of-care therapy. It should not be combined with other biologics or cyclophosphamide.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Belimumab targets BLyS/BAFF, a B cell survival factor; approved for SLE. Depression and suicidality are notable adverse effects requiring monitoring. COMLEX: Belimumab is approved for patients aged 5 and older with active SLE on standard-of-care therapy.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 28 Obj 4 — Mechanisms Involved in Activating T Cells and B Cells"},{"id":"sdl45_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 52-year-old man with granulomatosis with polyangiitis refractory to cyclophosphamide is started on rituximab combined with glucocorticoids. His rheumatologist explains how this agent works. Which of the following correctly describes rituximab's mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab is a fully human antibody that inhibits B-lymphocyte stimulator protein, blocking B cell survival signals and reducing autoantibody production in vasculitis","B":"Rituximab is a chimeric anti-CD20 antibody that depletes mature B cells through complement-dependent cytotoxicity and antibody-dependent cellular cytotoxicity","C":"Rituximab inhibits IMPDH in activated B cells, depleting guanosine nucleotides and selectively blocking B cell clonal expansion without depleting resting B cells","D":"Rituximab is a bispecific antibody targeting both CD20 on B cells and CD3 on T cells, enabling T cell-mediated killing of autoreactive B lymphocytes","E":"Rituximab binds the IL-6 receptor on activated B cells, preventing downstream STAT3 signaling and blocking B cell differentiation into antibody-secreting plasma cells"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rituximab is a chimeric monoclonal antibody targeting CD20, a transmembrane phosphoprotein expressed on mature B cells but absent on stem cells and plasma cells. Binding triggers rapid depletion of peripheral and synovial B cells through complement-dependent cytotoxicity, ADCC via NK cells, and direct apoptosis. Depletion of synovial tissue B cells correlates with clinical response in RA. RTX is approved for RA with methotrexate and for GPA and MPA with glucocorticoids. Anti-BLyS is belimumab; IMPDH is MMF; bispecific CD20/CD3 antibodies are a separate oncology agent.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rituximab = anti-CD20 = B cell depletion via complement plus ADCC plus apoptosis. CD20 is absent on plasma cells so autoantibody production may not fully normalize. COMLEX: Indicated for RA after TNF inhibitor failure and for GPA and MPA in combination with glucocorticoids.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines and Their Roles in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl45_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 46-year-old man with psoriatic arthritis and extensive plaque psoriasis has an inadequate response to methotrexate. His rheumatologist starts ustekinumab, a fully human IgG monoclonal antibody given by subcutaneous injection. Which of the following describes its mechanism of action?","choices":{"A":"It binds IL-17A and prevents it from engaging the IL-17A receptor, blocking the main effector cytokine of Th17 cells","B":"It binds the p19 subunit found in IL-23 but not IL-12, blocking IL-23 receptor signaling while leaving IL-12 intact","C":"It acts as a recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist, blocking the effects of IL-1 alpha and IL-1 beta at the IL-1 receptor","D":"It binds the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, blocking receptor binding and the formation of Th1 and Th17 cells","E":"It inhibits phosphodiesterase 4, raising intracellular cAMP and lowering the production of IL-12, IL-23 and TNF-alpha"},"correct":"D","explanation":"Ustekinumab is a fully human IgG monoclonal antagonist of IL-12 and IL-23 that acts at their shared p40 subunit. By preventing p40 from binding IL-12R-beta-1 on CD4+ T cells and NK cells, it suppresses the formation of proinflammatory Th1 and Th17 cells. It is indicated for psoriatic arthritis, alone or with methotrexate, and for plaque psoriasis, ulcerative colitis and Crohn disease. Binding of IL-17A describes secukinumab (and ixekizumab). Selective binding of the IL-23 p19 subunit describes guselkumab and risankizumab, which spare IL-12. A recombinant IL-1 receptor antagonist is anakinra. Phosphodiesterase 4 inhibition with a rise in cAMP is the mechanism of apremilast, an oral small molecule rather than an antibody.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Psoriasis and PsA biologics: ustekinumab = anti-p40 (IL-12 and IL-23); guselkumab, risankizumab, tildrakizumab = anti-IL-23 (p19); secukinumab, ixekizumab = anti-IL-17A; brodalumab = anti-IL-17 receptor. COMLEX: Ustekinumab also treats Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis, a useful choice when psoriatic disease and IBD coexist.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 46 Obj 2 — Proinflammatory mediators in spondyloarthritis, including IL-23"},{"id":"sdl45_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Describe the classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional synthetic DMARDs: TNF-alpha inhibitors, IL-6 inhibitors, Janus Kinase inhibitors, T cell activation inhibitors, Interferon blockers, PDE4 inhibitors, B cell inhibitors, IL-23 inhibitors, IL-17 inhibitors, calcineurin inhibitors, antimalarials, IL-1 inhibitors, mycophenolate mofetil, anti-metabolites.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 47-year-old man with moderate psoriatic arthritis and psoriatic skin plaques develops prominent diarrhea and nausea shortly after starting a new oral small-molecule agent. His medication increases intracellular cAMP levels in immune cells and inhibits production of TNF-alpha, IL-8, IL-12, IL-17, and IL-23. Which of the following agents and mechanism is most consistent with this presentation?","choices":{"A":"Baricitinib, a JAK1 and JAK2 inhibitor whose most prominent adverse effects include infection risk, VTE, and dose-dependent hyperlipidemia rather than early GI symptoms","B":"Leflunomide, a dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibitor that can cause diarrhea but which does not work through cAMP elevation or PDE4 inhibition","C":"Tofacitinib, a JAK1 and JAK3 inhibitor whose primary adverse effects include infection risk, VTE, and MACE rather than prominent nausea and diarrhea","D":"Upadacitinib, a selective JAK1 inhibitor whose most prominent adverse effects include serious infections, herpes zoster, and elevated creatine kinase levels","E":"Apremilast, a PDE4 inhibitor that prevents cAMP degradation in immune cells, reducing transcription of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines through PKA activation"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Apremilast is an oral PDE4 inhibitor that increases intracellular cAMP by blocking its degradation. Elevated cAMP activates protein kinase A, which reduces transcription of multiple pro-inflammatory cytokines including TNF-alpha, IL-8, IL-12, IL-17, IL-23, IFN-gamma, and others. The most common adverse effects are GI: diarrhea (predominant), nausea, upper abdominal pain, vomiting, and upper respiratory infections. GI symptoms typically improve over the first month. It is approved for psoriatic arthritis, plaque psoriasis, and oral ulcers in Behcet disease.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Apremilast = PDE4 inhibitor = elevated cAMP = reduced cytokine production. GI side effects are the most common. COMLEX: Apremilast is a small oral molecule approved for psoriatic arthritis without requiring TB screening, unlike biologics.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 2 — Functions of Cytokines and Their Roles in Regulating Immune Responses"},{"id":"sdl45_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 48-year-old woman with RA refractory to methotrexate and two TNF inhibitors is being considered for rituximab. Her rheumatologist reviews the unique infectious risk profile of rituximab compared to TNF-alpha inhibitors. Which of the following best describes this distinction?","choices":{"A":"Rituximab carries the same risk of latent tuberculosis reactivation as TNF inhibitors, requiring identical screening, prophylaxis, and ongoing chest radiograph monitoring","B":"Rituximab is associated with hepatitis B reactivation but, unlike TNF inhibitors, has not been associated with reactivation of latent tuberculosis or increased lymphoma risk","C":"Rituximab causes progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy from JC virus reactivation as its primary infectious complication, replacing the TB risk seen with TNF inhibitors","D":"Rituximab is associated with herpes zoster reactivation as its most distinctive infectious risk, analogous to the pattern seen with JAK inhibitor use in RA","E":"Rituximab causes deep endemic fungal infections and histoplasmosis as its primary risk, replacing the bacterial infection pattern typical of TNF inhibitor therapy"},"correct":"B","explanation":"Rituximab is uniquely associated with hepatitis B reactivation because it depletes CD20-positive B cells including memory B cells that harbor latent HBV. Hepatitis B surface antigen and core antibody screening is required before initiation, and prophylactic antiviral therapy should be given. In contrast to TNF inhibitors, rituximab has not been associated with reactivation of latent TB or de novo lymphoma. PML from JC virus has been reported but is extremely rare. Herpes zoster is the signature infectious risk of JAK inhibitors, not rituximab.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Rituximab = hepatitis B reactivation risk, not TB. TNF inhibitors = TB reactivation risk. Both require screening before initiation. COMLEX: Screen for HBsAg and anti-HBc before rituximab; treat positive patients with prophylactic antivirals.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl45_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 45-year-old woman with RA on methotrexate 20 mg weekly develops painful oral ulcers, mild hair thinning, and fatigue over three weeks. CBC shows hemoglobin of 10.6 g per dL with elevated mean corpuscular volume. Which of the following best explains the mechanism underlying these toxicities?","choices":{"A":"Methotrexate inhibits folate-dependent enzymes, impairing nucleotide synthesis and reducing proliferation of mucosal epithelial, hematopoietic, and follicular cells","B":"Methotrexate accumulates in hepatocytes and inhibits mitochondrial aldehyde dehydrogenase, causing direct hepatocyte necrosis and secondary hematopoietic suppression","C":"Methotrexate blocks pyrimidine synthesis through dihydroorotate dehydrogenase inhibition, causing mucosal cell atrophy and megaloblastic changes in bone marrow","D":"Methotrexate inhibits calcineurin in mucosal epithelial cells, reducing IL-2 production and causing immune-mediated apoptosis of rapidly regenerating surface cells","E":"Methotrexate depletes guanosine nucleotides through IMPDH inhibition, blocking DNA synthesis specifically in lymphocytes, follicular cells, and enterocytes"},"correct":"A","explanation":"Methotrexate inhibits thymidylate synthetase and AICAR transformylase, ultimately reducing folate-dependent nucleotide synthesis. Rapidly dividing cells including mucosal epithelium, hematopoietic precursors, and hair follicles are most vulnerable, producing stomatitis, macrocytic anemia, leukopenia, and alopecia. Supplemental folic acid reduces these toxicities by providing substrate that bypasses the inhibited enzymes, with a modest 10 to 18% reduction in efficacy. Leucovorin is used for acute toxicity rescue. MTX is contraindicated in pregnancy because of these antiproliferative effects.","boardPrep":"Step 1: MTX toxicities reflect antifolate effects on dividing cells: stomatitis, alopecia, macrocytic anemia, leukopenia. Folic acid supplementation reduces but does not eliminate toxicity. COMLEX: Leucovorin rescues acute MTX toxicity; folic acid is daily prophylaxis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"},{"id":"sdl45_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the most common adverse effects by class of treatment.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and peripheral joint involvement is started on sulfasalazine. He and his partner are planning to conceive within the next year. Which of the following correctly describes the most important reproductive consideration for this patient?","choices":{"A":"Sulfasalazine is teratogenic in both sexes and must be discontinued at least six months before any conception attempt by either the male or female partner","B":"Sulfasalazine irreversibly reduces testosterone production by inhibiting Leydig cell steroidogenesis, causing permanent hypogonadism after prolonged use","C":"Sulfasalazine impairs female fertility primarily by inducing anovulation and luteal phase dysfunction, requiring hormonal supplementation throughout therapy","D":"Sulfasalazine is the safest available DMARD in pregnancy for both sexes with no reproductive effects, making it the preferred agent in patients planning to conceive","E":"Sulfasalazine causes reversible male infertility through impaired sperm motility and morphology that resolves after the drug is discontinued"},"correct":"E","explanation":"Sulfasalazine causes reversible oligospermia and impaired sperm motility and morphology in men, thought to be due to the sulfapyridine moiety. This effect resolves within months of discontinuation. Sulfasalazine does not affect female fertility and is relatively safe in pregnancy. It does not cause irreversible hypogonadism or testosterone suppression. This reversibility is clinically important because the patient can discontinue the drug when actively attempting conception and resume therapy afterward if needed.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Sulfasalazine causes reversible male infertility; safe in female patients and pregnancy. Does not cause irreversible testosterone suppression. COMLEX: Sulfasalazine is effective in RA, reactive arthritis, and peripheral joint disease in ankylosing spondylitis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 1 — Mechanisms Underlying Autoimmunity"}],"46":[{"id":"sdl46_q01","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old man with no back pain learns from a genetic ancestry panel that he carries HLA-B27. He asks whether this means he has, or will get, ankylosing spondylitis. Which statement about his result is most accurate?","choices":{"A":"Most HLA-B27 carriers develop ankylosing spondylitis by age 40, so spinal imaging is needed now","B":"Most people who carry HLA-B27 do not develop spondyloarthritis, so the result is not diagnostic","C":"HLA-B27 occurs in fewer than 1 in 10,000 people, so his result strongly predicts disease","D":"HLA-B27 acts by driving rheumatoid factor production, so RF testing will confirm his risk","E":"HLA-B27 is a class II allele, so his risk depends on CD4+ T-cell presentation of joint peptides"},"correct":"B","explanation":"HLA-B27 is present in about 90 percent of patients with ankylosing spondylitis but also in roughly 0.1 to 10 percent of the general population, and the test is nonspecific: many people who test positive have no spondyloarthropathy, so a positive result is not diagnostic. In the course's laboratory table, HLA-B27 is positive in 6 of 100 people without AS, and the likelihood of AS after a positive result is about 1 in 18. Spondyloarthropathies are defined by the absence of rheumatoid factor. HLA-B27 is an MHC class I allele, presumably acting through presentation of antigens. Sources: course SDL 46 reading (ScholarRx, Spondyloarthropathies, p1–3); course SDL 44 reading (Rheumatology Ch 30, Table 30.1, p2); course SDL 32/33/37/38/54–56 reading (Robbins Ch 26, Bones, joints, and soft tissue tumors, ankylosing spondylitis, p84).","boardPrep":"Step 1: HLA-B27 is an MHC class I allele associated with AS, reactive arthritis, psoriatic arthritis, and IBD-associated arthritis (Robbins Ch 26). COMLEX: AS presents with low back pain and spinal immobility in the second and third decades (Robbins Ch 26).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 44 → Obj 1 — sensitivity, specificity, and likelihood ratios"},{"id":"sdl46_q02","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which non-MHC gene polymorphism most significantly alters Th17 cell differentiation and has been identified as a shared risk locus across ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, and inflammatory bowel disease?","choices":{"A":"NOD2 polymorphisms directly reduce intestinal innate immune receptor function through impaired NLR-mediated pathogen sensing, producing gut dysbiosis and bacterial translocation that seeds entheseal inflammation in all three conditions.","B":"ERAP1 polymorphisms reduce aminopeptidase efficiency in peptide trimming for MHC class I loading, increasing misfolded HLA-B27 complex formation in antigen-presenting cells and driving T cell-mediated inflammation in all three conditions.","C":"IL23R polymorphisms alter receptor signaling that governs Th17 cell differentiation, linking shared genetic susceptibility across ankylosing spondylitis, psoriasis, and inflammatory bowel disease through the IL-23/Th17 axis.","D":"TNFRSF1A polymorphisms increase TNF receptor sensitivity on synoviocytes and intestinal epithelium, amplifying synovial inflammation and mucosal injury through enhanced TNF-alpha signaling in all three associated conditions.","E":"CARD15 polymorphisms impair NLR-mediated pathogen recognition in macrophages, directly promoting entheseal seeding from gut-derived bacteria and mucosal immune dysregulation shared across all three disease states."},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL23R polymorphisms are among the most consistently replicated non-MHC risk loci across the spondyloarthritis spectrum, psoriasis, and IBD because the IL-23/Th17 axis is central to all three conditions. IL23R variants alter signaling intensity on Th17 precursor cells, modifying the Th17/Treg balance. ERAP1 is also a SpA risk gene but acts through MHC-I peptide trimming specifically in HLA-B27 contexts. NOD2 mutations are primarily associated with Crohn disease but not the full SpA spectrum. TNFRSF1A mutations cause periodic fever syndromes.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Non-MHC SpA risk genes — IL23R (Th17 axis), ERAP1 (peptide trimming for HLA-B27). COMLEX: The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA is mediated through shared IL-23/IL-17 pathway genetics.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles such as HLA-B27 are among the strongest MHC-I susceptibility alleles known, conferring genetic risk for spondyloarthritis through antigen-presentation and misfolding mechanisms."},{"id":"sdl46_q03","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"The arthritogenic peptide hypothesis is one proposed mechanism by which HLA-B27 contributes to spondyloarthritis pathogenesis. Which statement best describes this hypothesis?","choices":{"A":"HLA-B27 heavy chain homodimerization on cell surfaces activates NK cells and innate lymphoid cells through KIR3DL2 receptor binding, independent of any microbial antigen presentation or CD8+ T cell activation.","B":"HLA-B27 misfolding in the endoplasmic reticulum triggers the unfolded protein response, activating IRE1-alpha and NF-kB to induce IL-23 production that drives downstream Th17 differentiation independent of peptide presentation.","C":"HLA-B27 overexpression on the cell surface increases antigen presentation to regulatory T cells, reducing peripheral tolerance through exhaustion of the Treg compartment and enabling autoimmune entheseal inflammation.","D":"HLA-B27 allelic polymorphisms cause structural changes in the peptide-binding groove that prevent loading of microbial antigens entirely, triggering autoreactive T cell responses against empty MHC complexes on joint synoviocytes.","E":"HLA-B27 presents self-peptides or microbially cross-reactive peptides to autoreactive CD8+ T cells that then target joint tissue due to molecular mimicry between foreign antigens and self-peptides displayed on joint cells."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Three competing but not mutually exclusive hypotheses explain HLA-B27 pathogenicity: (1) Arthritogenic peptide: HLA-B27 presents cross-reactive peptides activating autoreactive CD8+ T cells via molecular mimicry. (2) Misfolding/UPR: HLA-B27 heavy chains misfold in the ER, activating UPR and IL-23 production. (3) Free heavy chain: HLA-B27 homodimers on the cell surface activate innate immune cells via KIR receptors. The arthritogenic peptide hypothesis is supported by evidence of bacterial triggers (Klebsiella, Chlamydia) sharing sequence homology with HLA-B27 or self-peptides it presents.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Know all three HLA-B27 disease mechanisms: arthritogenic peptide (molecular mimicry), UPR/misfolding (IL-23 induction), free heavy chain (innate activation). COMLEX: Seronegative SpA distinguishes from RA by RF negativity and HLA-B27 positivity.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 36 → Obj 2 — HLA alleles such as HLA-B27 are among the strongest MHC-I susceptibility alleles known, conferring genetic risk for spondyloarthritis through antigen-presentation and misfolding mechanisms."},{"id":"sdl46_q04","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which cytokine is the primary upstream driver of Th17 cell differentiation at entheseal sites and represents the key initiating signal in the IL-23/IL-17 axis governing spondyloarthritis inflammation?","choices":{"A":"IL-23 is produced by dendritic cells and macrophages and serves as the critical differentiation and survival signal for Th17 cells, initiating the IL-23/IL-17 inflammatory cascade at entheseal and synovial sites in spondyloarthritis.","B":"IL-6 serves as the primary driver of Th17 differentiation in spondyloarthritis by acting directly on naive T cells without TGF-beta co-stimulation, initiating the SpA inflammatory cascade exclusively at entheseal fibrocartilage insertion sites.","C":"IL-12 is the upstream Th17-polarizing cytokine in spondyloarthritis, activating STAT4 signaling on innate lymphoid cells and promoting IL-17 production through an IFN-gamma-independent pathway at sites of entheseal inflammation.","D":"TNF-alpha serves as the primary initiating cytokine of the IL-23/IL-17 axis by activating dendritic cells to produce IL-17 directly, bypassing the Th17 differentiation step at inflamed joint surfaces in spondyloarthritis.","E":"IFN-gamma drives Th17 differentiation from naive CD4+ T cells in spondyloarthritis, representing the key polarizing cytokine that initiates the downstream IL-17 response at sites of entheseal and sacroiliac joint inflammation."},"correct":"A","explanation":"IL-23 (produced by dendritic cells and macrophages) is the master upstream regulator of Th17 differentiation and survival, acting through STAT3 to promote IL-17A and IL-17F production. IL-23/IL-17 axis is the dominant pathway in SpA entheseal inflammation. IL-6 combined with TGF-beta can also drive Th17 differentiation but is not the primary entheseal initiator. IL-12 drives Th1/IFN-gamma responses, not Th17. IFN-gamma actually inhibits Th17 differentiation. This axis is the target of ustekinumab (anti-p40, blocks IL-12 and IL-23), guselkumab (anti-IL-23 p19), secukinumab, and ixekizumab (anti-IL-17A).","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-23 drives Th17; IL-17A drives entheseal inflammation. IL-12 drives Th1 not Th17. COMLEX: Th17 = joint inflammation; therapeutics target IL-23 (upstream) or IL-17A (downstream).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q05","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"IL-17A drives entheseal inflammation in spondyloarthritis through which primary downstream mechanism at sites of tendon and ligament insertion?","choices":{"A":"IL-17A directly activates osteoclasts through RANK-RANKL upregulation to produce progressive bone erosion exclusively at entheseal insertion sites, independent of secondary cytokine amplification by stromal cells.","B":"IL-17A cross-links complement components C3 and C5 at entheseal surfaces, generating membrane attack complexes that directly destroy fibrocartilage matrix proteins and produce the acute entheseal inflammatory response.","C":"IL-17A activates mast cell degranulation at entheseal sites through high-affinity IL-17RA binding, releasing preformed histamine and prostaglandin D2 granules that produce the primary acute inflammatory response.","D":"IL-17A binds directly to entheseal chondrocytes through a distinct receptor isoform, triggering caspase-3-mediated apoptosis and releasing DAMPs that initiate the sterile inflammatory cascade at fibrocartilage insertion zones.","E":"IL-17A stimulates resident fibroblasts and osteoblasts at entheseal insertion sites to produce TNF-alpha, IL-6, and neutrophil-attracting chemokines such as CXCL1 and CXCL5 that amplify and sustain local inflammation."},"correct":"E","explanation":"IL-17A acts on stromal cells (fibroblasts, osteoblasts, epithelial cells) at entheseal sites, inducing production of TNF-alpha, IL-6, CXCL1, CXCL5, and G-CSF/GM-CSF that recruit and activate neutrophils, amplifying the inflammatory response. This explains the dense neutrophilic infiltrate characteristic of enthesitis. IL-17A does eventually contribute to osteoclast activation and bone erosion, but this is secondary to the stromal cell response. The primary IL-17A receptor complex is IL-17RA/IL-17RC expressed on fibroblasts and epithelial cells.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-17A acts on stromal cells to produce secondary cytokines (TNF, IL-6) and neutrophil chemoattractants. Target of secukinumab and ixekizumab. COMLEX: Enthesitis (tendon/ligament insertion inflammation) is the hallmark of SpA distinguishing it from RA.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q06","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"TNF-alpha plays a central role in spondyloarthritis inflammation. Which statement best characterizes its contribution to structural joint damage in ankylosing spondylitis?","choices":{"A":"TNF-alpha primarily drives new bone formation at entheses by directly activating osteoblasts through the Wnt signaling pathway, producing the syndesmophyte formation and bamboo spine radiographic appearance in advanced AS.","B":"TNF-alpha acts exclusively through TNFR2 receptors on synoviocytes to produce cartilage degradation without contributing to systemic inflammatory markers such as CRP or ESR elevation in active spondyloarthritis.","C":"TNF-alpha directly activates Th17 differentiation from naive CD4+ T cells by upregulating IL-23 receptor expression, creating a positive feedback loop that sustains IL-17 production and entheseal inflammation in spondyloarthritis.","D":"TNF-alpha activates osteoclasts and promotes synoviocyte hyperplasia, driving bone erosion and cartilage degradation at the joint surface while also stimulating hepatic acute-phase reactants that sustain systemic inflammation.","E":"TNF-alpha functions exclusively as a systemic inflammatory mediator in spondyloarthritis with no direct structural role at the enthesis, serving only to maintain the febrile response and constitutional symptoms during flares."},"correct":"D","explanation":"TNF-alpha drives structural joint damage through osteoclast activation (RANK-RANKL upregulation), synoviocyte/pannus hyperplasia, and matrix metalloproteinase induction causing cartilage degradation. It also triggers hepatic CRP and fibrinogen synthesis. Paradoxically, TNF-alpha suppresses the Wnt pathway and BMP pathway, so it actually INHIBITS new bone formation — explaining why TNF inhibitor therapy does not reliably halt radiographic progression of syndesmophytes (new bone forms as the TNF suppression is relieved). This TNF-Wnt paradox is a classic board concept.","boardPrep":"Step 1: TNF inhibitors improve symptoms and inflammatory markers in AS but do not reliably halt syndesmophyte formation. COMLEX: TNF-Wnt paradox — TNF inhibits Wnt, so removing TNF may disinhibit new bone formation.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 35 → Obj 1 — Th17 cells are the primary producers of IL-17 in spondyloarthritis; dendritic cells and macrophages producing IL-23 are the upstream drivers of Th17 differentiation at entheseal sites."},{"id":"sdl46_q07","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Spondyloarthritis is classified as a seronegative arthropathy. Which laboratory finding defines this seronegativity and distinguishes SpA from rheumatoid arthritis?","choices":{"A":"Absence of anti-cyclic citrullinated peptide antibodies defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis and is the most specific laboratory feature distinguishing SpA from rheumatoid arthritis on autoantibody panels.","B":"Absence of antinuclear antibodies on immunofluorescence assay defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis, as ANA positivity is the hallmark of all seropositive inflammatory arthritides including RA.","C":"Absence of anti-double-stranded DNA antibodies in spondyloarthritis serum defines its seronegative classification, distinguishing it from the immune complex-mediated articular inflammation characteristic of rheumatoid disease.","D":"Absence of complement consumption markers C3 and C4 in spondyloarthritis serum defines its seronegative pattern, as RA consumes complement through synovial immune complex deposition while SpA does not activate the complement cascade.","E":"Absence of rheumatoid factor (RF) defines the seronegative classification of spondyloarthritis, distinguishing the entire SpA spectrum (AS, PsA, reactive arthritis, IBD-associated SpA) from seropositive rheumatoid arthritis."},"correct":"E","explanation":"Seronegative arthropathy specifically refers to the absence of rheumatoid factor (RF) in the spondyloarthritis spectrum (AS, PsA, reactive arthritis, IBD-associated SpA, undifferentiated SpA). RF is an IgM antibody directed against the Fc region of IgG and is the defining autoantibody of seropositive RA. Anti-CCP is also negative in SpA but is not what defines the seronegative classification historically. ANA and anti-dsDNA are not part of RA or SpA classification. The seronegative classification reflects the different immune pathogenesis (MHC class I/CD8+/Th17 in SpA versus MHC class II/CD4+/Th1/RF in RA).","boardPrep":"Step 1: Seronegative = RF negative. SpA triad: HLA-B27+, RF-, sacroiliac involvement. COMLEX: Distinguish seropositive RA (RF+, anti-CCP+, symmetric small joints, pannus) from seronegative SpA (RF-, HLA-B27+, axial/entheseal).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q08","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which feature of inflammatory back pain in ankylosing spondylitis most reliably distinguishes it from mechanical low back pain and reflects the underlying cytokine-driven pathophysiology?","choices":{"A":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is provoked by rotational movements and weight-bearing exercise, reflecting sacroiliac joint capsule destruction with asymmetric articular erosion causing mechanical instability at the pelvic girdle level.","B":"Inflammatory back pain in AS radiates into both lower extremities in a dermatomal distribution, reflecting bilateral nerve root compression at L4-L5 levels from inflammatory osteophyte formation at the posterior vertebral elements.","C":"Inflammatory back pain in AS improves with physical activity and worsens with prolonged rest and inactivity, reflecting cytokine-driven entheseal inflammation that responds to movement-induced synovial fluid circulation and anti-inflammatory signaling.","D":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is associated with exquisite point tenderness over individual lumbar vertebral bodies in the midline, reflecting vertebral periostitis from direct hematogenous bacterial seeding at the discovertebral junction.","E":"Inflammatory back pain in AS is exclusively nocturnal and fully resolves upon waking and standing, reflecting circadian TNF-alpha surges during overnight recumbency that are completely suppressed by daytime upright posture."},"correct":"C","explanation":"Inflammatory back pain (IBP) criteria include: onset in young adulthood (AS onset usually 20-40 years, FA p475), insidious onset, improvement with exercise (not rest), no improvement with rest, pain at night with improvement on getting up. The classic mnemonic is \"stiffness improves with movement, worsens with rest\" — the opposite of mechanical back pain. Prolonged morning stiffness is characteristic. This pattern reflects the cytokine-driven inflammation where activity mobilizes synovial fluid and may reduce local cytokine concentrations. IBP is a key diagnostic criterion in the ASAS classification of axial SpA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IBP = improves with exercise, worsens with rest, prolonged morning stiffness, young-adult onset. COMLEX: IBP vs. mechanical: mechanical worsens with activity, improves with rest. Opposite for IBP.","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q09","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":1,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Which extraarticular manifestation is most strongly associated with HLA-B27 positivity across the spondyloarthritis spectrum, occurring in a substantial share of ankylosing spondylitis patients during their disease course?","choices":{"A":"Psoriasis involving the scalp and extensor surfaces is the most characteristic extraarticular manifestation of HLA-B27-positive AS, occurring in approximately 30% of patients and preceding joint disease by several years on average.","B":"Acute anterior uveitis is the most common extraarticular manifestation of spondyloarthritis, presenting as a painful unilateral red eye with photophobia and blurred vision, and is strongly associated with HLA-B27","C":"Pulmonary apical fibrosis is the most strongly HLA-B27-associated extraarticular feature of AS, occurring in approximately 25-35% of patients with long-standing disease and producing a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing.","D":"Aortic regurgitation secondary to aortitis at the aortic root is the most common extraarticular manifestation of HLA-B27-positive spondyloarthritis, present in 30-40% of AS patients early in the disease course.","E":"Secondary amyloidosis affecting renal glomeruli is the most frequent extraarticular complication associated with HLA-B27 positivity in long-standing AS, occurring in 25-40% of patients with persistent active disease."},"correct":"B","explanation":"Acute anterior uveitis (AAU) is the most common extraarticular manifestation of spondyloarthritis, (FA p475 lists uveitis with AS). It is typically unilateral, non-granulomatous, and alternating between eyes over time. It presents with sudden pain, redness, photophobia, and blurred vision. Aortitis with aortic regurgitation and restrictive lung disease from costovertebral ankylosis are less common (FA p475). Psoriasis is a feature of PsA, not primarily AS. AAU can be the presenting feature of SpA before joint symptoms appear.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Acute anterior uveitis = most common extraarticular SpA manifestation; unilateral, alternating, painful. COMLEX: Classic triad of reactive arthritis = conjunctivitis + urethritis + arthritis (not uveitis — distinguish from AS-associated AAU).","crossRef":"📎 SDL 45 → Obj 2 — TNF-alpha inhibitors and anti-IL-17A biologics (secukinumab, ixekizumab) directly target the proinflammatory mediators that drive the major pathogenic elements of spondyloarthritis."},{"id":"sdl46_q10","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 28-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and HLA-B27 positivity undergoes genetic testing that also identifies a co-inherited ERAP1 polymorphism. Researchers propose this combination amplifies disease susceptibility through an additive mechanism. Which function of ERAP1 best explains why its polymorphism interacts with HLA-B27 to increase spondyloarthritis risk?","choices":{"A":"ERAP1 trims peptides in the endoplasmic reticulum to optimal length for HLA class I loading; ERAP1 variants alter the peptide repertoire presented on HLA-B27, increasing display of arthritogenic peptides to autoreactive CD8+ T cells","B":"ERAP1 cleaves the HLA-B27 signal peptide during post-translational processing, and variants impair this cleavage step, causing increased HLA-B27 surface expression density and proportionally enhanced autoreactive T cell activation at joint surfaces.","C":"ERAP1 phosphorylates the cytoplasmic tail of HLA-B27 after peptide loading is complete, and variants impair this phosphorylation, preventing recycling of loaded HLA-B27 complexes from the ER to the cell surface for CD8+ T cell presentation.","D":"ERAP1 catalyzes the final trimming step of antigens loaded onto HLA-DR molecules for MHC class II presentation, and variants alter the CD4+ Th17 helper response that amplifies downstream IL-17 production in spondyloarthritis.","E":"ERAP1 degrades misfolded HLA-B27 homodimers in the endoplasmic reticulum lumen, and ERAP1 variants reduce this degradation efficiency, causing accumulation of homodimers on the cell surface that constitutively activate NK cells via KIR receptors."},"correct":"A","explanation":"ERAP1 (Endoplasmic Reticulum Aminopeptidase 1) trims peptides in the ER to 8-10 amino acid length optimal for HLA class I loading. ERAP1 polymorphisms alter its peptide-trimming specificity and efficiency, changing which peptides are ultimately displayed on HLA-B27. When combined with disease-associated HLA-B27, ERAP1 variants increase presentation of arthritogenic peptides. Importantly, ERAP1 risk variants only confer SpA risk in HLA-B27-positive individuals, consistent with epistatic interaction. ERAP1 does not act on MHC class II, does not phosphorylate HLA, and does not degrade homodimers.","boardPrep":"Step 1: ERAP1 + HLA-B27 epistatic interaction — ERAP1 risk only in HLA-B27+ background. ERAP1 trims peptides for MHC-I loading. COMLEX: Non-MHC SpA genetics: IL23R (Th17) and ERAP1 (peptide trimming) are the two highest-yield non-HLA risk genes.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q11","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 35-year-old man with a 4-year history of inflammatory low back pain is found to be HLA-B27 positive. Analysis of his synovial tissue macrophages reveals increased GRP78 expression, IRE1-alpha activation, and XBP-1 mRNA splicing. Which mechanism best explains these findings in the context of HLA-B27-associated spondyloarthritis?","choices":{"A":"These findings reflect heightened antigen presentation activity in macrophages, where upregulated GRP78 chaperone promotes HLA-B27 peptide-loading efficiency and XBP-1 drives MHC-I transcription to enhance TCR engagement by autoreactive CD8+ T cells.","B":"These findings reflect complement-mediated activation downstream of HLA-B27 surface homodimerization, where IRE1-alpha acts as a complement convertase and spliced XBP-1 promotes MAC assembly proteins at the synovial macrophage surface.","C":"These findings reflect increased Th17 differentiation signaling in synovial macrophages, where GRP78 acts as the IL-23 receptor co-activator and XBP-1 splicing drives IL-17A gene transcription independent of upstream UPR activation.","D":"These findings reflect endoplasmic reticulum stress from HLA-B27 heavy chain misfolding; GRP78 and IRE1-alpha activation represent the unfolded protein response, and XBP-1 splicing promotes IL-23 transcription and downstream Th17 inflammation","E":"These findings reflect mitochondrial dysfunction in HLA-B27-expressing macrophages, where GRP78 and IRE1-alpha redirect oxidative phosphorylation complexes toward the IL-23 synthesis pathway through direct protein-protein interaction."},"correct":"D","explanation":"HLA-B27 heavy chains have an intrinsic tendency to misfold in the ER due to their unique beta-57 conformation. Misfolded HLA-B27 triggers the unfolded protein response (UPR): GRP78 binds misfolded proteins and dissociates from IRE1-alpha, activating it; IRE1-alpha splices XBP-1 mRNA, and the resulting transcription factor promotes IL-23 production. This IL-23 then drives Th17 differentiation and IL-17 production. The UPR hypothesis explains how HLA-B27 can drive inflammation even without peptide presentation to T cells, providing a T cell-independent innate immune pathway to SpA inflammation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: UPR hypothesis: HLA-B27 misfolds → ER stress → GRP78/IRE1-alpha/XBP-1 → IL-23 → Th17 → IL-17. Three HLA-B27 mechanisms = arthritogenic peptide, UPR/misfolding, free heavy chain. COMLEX: ER stress and UPR in autoimmune disease is a recurring theme across multiple rheumatic conditions.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q12","objective":1,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 1 — Discuss the genetic contribution to disease in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 31-year-old woman with Crohn disease develops insidious low back pain and bilateral sacroiliitis on MRI. She tests HLA-B27 negative. Genetic testing identifies an IL23R polymorphism also carried by her HLA-B27-negative affected sibling. Through which mechanism do IL23R variants contribute to spondyloarthritis susceptibility independent of HLA-B27?","choices":{"A":"IL23R polymorphisms alter HLA-B27 surface expression levels on antigen-presenting cells, creating a functionally equivalent MHC class I antigen-presentation defect that drives CD8+ cytotoxic arthritis even in genetically HLA-B27-negative individuals.","B":"IL23R polymorphisms alter Th17 cell differentiation and survival by modifying receptor signaling intensity in response to IL-23, shifting the Th17/Treg balance toward gut and joint tissue inflammation independent of HLA-B27 presentation","C":"IL23R polymorphisms reduce IL-12 receptor signaling competition on Th1 cells, shifting the immune balance from Th1-mediated IFN-gamma responses toward Th17-predominant inflammation in gut-associated lymphoid tissue and entheseal sites.","D":"IL23R polymorphisms constitutively activate NF-kB in intestinal epithelial cells entirely independent of T cell signaling, directly producing the mucosal inflammation and bacterial translocation that seeds entheseal and sacroiliac joint inflammation.","E":"IL23R polymorphisms impair regulatory T cell FoxP3 expression by reducing IL-23-mediated Treg plasticity signaling, eliminating peripheral tolerance mechanisms that normally suppress self-reactive B cells targeting sacroiliac joint proteoglycans."},"correct":"B","explanation":"IL23R is the receptor for IL-23, the master upstream cytokine driving Th17 differentiation and the IL-17 inflammatory axis. IL23R polymorphisms that affect receptor signaling efficiency alter the Th17/Treg balance — with risk alleles promoting excess Th17 activity in both gut mucosa and joint tissue. This explains the shared genetic basis of IBD-associated SpA, AS, and psoriasis even in HLA-B27-negative patients. The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA operates through intestinal dendritic cells producing IL-23 that drives Th17 cells which then traffic to joint tissue via shared adhesion molecules.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL23R risk alleles drive Th17 excess independent of HLA-B27, explaining IBD-SpA overlap in HLA-B27-negative patients. COMLEX: IBD-associated SpA: Crohn disease and ulcerative colitis are often associated with spondyloarthritis (FA p475); sacroiliitis may be asymptomatic.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 36 Obj 2 — Specific HLA alleles and non-MHC genes in autoimmune diseases."},{"id":"sdl46_q13","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 38-year-old man with ankylosing spondylitis and inadequate response to two TNF-alpha inhibitors is started on secukinumab, with significant improvement in BASDAI score and CRP at 16 weeks. What is the precise mechanism of action of this agent that accounts for its efficacy following TNF inhibitor failure?","choices":{"A":"Secukinumab is a monoclonal antibody that selectively neutralizes IL-17A, blocking its binding to the IL-17RA/IL-17RC receptor complex on entheseal fibroblasts and osteoblasts and suppressing downstream chemokine production","B":"Secukinumab is a Janus kinase 1 and 3 inhibitor that blocks cytokine receptor signaling downstream of IL-6 and IL-23, preventing STAT3-mediated Th17 activation and IL-17 transcription at entheseal and synovial sites in TNF-refractory disease.","C":"Secukinumab is a monoclonal antibody targeting the p40 subunit shared by IL-12 and IL-23, simultaneously blocking both Th1-mediated and Th17-mediated inflammatory pathways in patients who have failed TNF inhibitor therapy.","D":"Secukinumab binds and neutralizes IL-23 by targeting its p19 subunit, preventing upstream Th17 cell differentiation and IL-17 production at sites of entheseal inflammation through blockade of the most proximal cytokine in the IL-23/Th17 axis.","E":"Secukinumab is a recombinant IL-17 receptor-Fc fusion protein that acts as a soluble decoy receptor, sequestering circulating IL-17A and IL-17F with equal affinity and preventing both cytokines from binding their cellular receptors at joints."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Secukinumab (and ixekizumab) are anti-IL-17A monoclonal antibodies. They bind IL-17A and block its interaction with the IL-17RA/IL-17RC heterodimeric receptor on stromal cells. This is distinct from: ustekinumab (anti-p40, blocks IL-12 and IL-23), guselkumab/risankizumab (anti-IL-23 p19), tofacitinib/upadacitinib (JAK inhibitors). Secukinumab is approved for AS, PsA, and plaque psoriasis. Bimekizumab targets both IL-17A and IL-17F. Anti-IL-17A efficacy in TNF-refractory SpA is well-established since IL-17A operates downstream of TNF and through independent entheseal pathways.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Secukinumab/ixekizumab = anti-IL-17A. Ustekinumab = anti-p40 (IL-12/23). JAK inhibitors (tofacitinib, upadacitinib) = downstream of cytokine receptors. COMLEX: Treatment ladder: NSAIDs → TNF inhibitors → anti-IL-17A or anti-IL-23 for refractory SpA.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q14","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"Researchers study CD4+ T cells from the gut of patients with ankylosing spondylitis. When naive CD4+ T cells are activated by antigen-presenting cells that secrete IL-1, IL-6 and IL-23, they adopt one fate; when the same cells are activated in the absence of these inflammatory cytokines, they adopt a different fate. Which pairing of outcomes is correct?","choices":{"A":"With these cytokines they become Th1 cells secreting IFN-gamma; without them they become Th2 cells secreting IL-4 and IL-5","B":"With these cytokines they become Th2 cells secreting IL-4 and IL-13; without them they are deleted by Fas-mediated apoptosis","C":"With these cytokines they become cytotoxic CD4+ cells secreting perforin; without them they become Th1 cells making IFN-gamma","D":"With these cytokines they become Th17 cells making IL-17 and IL-22; without them they become Tregs making IL-10 and TGF-beta","E":"With these cytokines they become Th1 cells secreting IL-12; without them they become anergic cells that cannot secrete IL-2"},"correct":"D","explanation":"The fate of an activated CD4+ T cell is set by the cytokines that antigen-presenting cells produce at the time of activation. When APCs produce the inflammatory cytokines IL-1, IL-6 and IL-23, the T cells differentiate into the Th17 subset, which secretes IL-17, IL-22 and chemokines and drives inflammation rich in neutrophils. In the absence of a proinflammatory milieu, activated cells instead become regulatory T cells, which suppress immune activity through IL-10 and TGF-beta. Th1 cells are induced by IL-12 from macrophages and dendritic cells and secrete IFN-gamma; IL-12 is an APC product, not a Th1 product. Th2 cells, which secrete IL-4, IL-5 and IL-13, are induced by IL-25, IL-33 and TSLP from stressed epithelial cells. Perforin is a CD8+ cytotoxic and NK-cell mechanism. In spondyloarthritis, the IL-23/IL-17 axis is the pathway targeted by anti-IL-17A and anti-IL-23 drugs.","boardPrep":"Step 1: APC cytokines set CD4+ fate: IL-12 gives Th1 (IFN-gamma); IL-25, IL-33, TSLP give Th2 (IL-4, IL-5, IL-13); IL-1, IL-6, IL-23 give Th17 (IL-17, IL-22); a noninflammatory milieu gives Tregs (IL-10, TGF-beta). COMLEX: Th17-driven diseases include psoriasis, psoriatic arthritis and ankylosing spondylitis, which respond to IL-17A blockade.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q15","objective":2,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 2 — Identify the proinflammatory mediators associated with the inflammatory response in spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 29-year-old woman with psoriatic arthritis and dactylitis of the right second finger has elevated serum IL-17A and dense neutrophilic infiltration of the tendon sheath on biopsy. Which cytokine produced by Th17 cells directly recruits neutrophils while also inducing stromal cell CXCL chemokine production to sustain the dactylitic tendon sheath inflammatory cycle?","choices":{"A":"IL-21 is the primary Th17-derived cytokine that directly activates neutrophil NADPH oxidase at tendon sheaths and induces fibroblast IL-8 production, making it the dominant amplification signal in dactylitic tendon sheath inflammation in PsA.","B":"IL-22 acts upstream of the neutrophilic tendon sheath response by activating resident fibroblasts to produce defensins and antimicrobial peptides that recruit circulating neutrophils to the site of dactylitic inflammation independent of IL-17A.","C":"IL-17A directly promotes neutrophil survival and recruitment while inducing resident fibroblasts to produce CXCL1, CXCL5, and GM-CSF, creating an amplification loop that sustains the neutrophilic infiltrate in dactylitic tendon sheath inflammation","D":"IL-17F acts upstream of neutrophilic inflammation by binding the IL-17RA/RC receptor complex with approximately 10-fold higher affinity than IL-17A, making it the quantitatively dominant neutrophil-recruiting cytokine at dactylitic tendon sheath sites.","E":"IL-23 directly activates tissue-resident neutrophils at the tendon sheath through a distinct receptor independent of IL-17A, serving as the dominant neutrophilic amplification signal in PsA dactylitis rather than acting through Th17 cell intermediaries."},"correct":"C","explanation":"IL-17A (not IL-17F, not IL-21, not IL-22) is the primary Th17 effector cytokine driving neutrophilic entheseal and tendon sheath inflammation. IL-17A binds IL-17RA/IL-17RC on stromal fibroblasts, inducing CXCL1, CXCL5 (GRO-alpha/ENA-78), and GM-CSF that recruit and prolong neutrophil survival. IL-17A also directly activates neutrophil respiratory burst. This creates a self-sustaining loop. IL-22 (also from Th17 cells) acts on epithelial barrier maintenance and keratinocytes (important in psoriasis). IL-21 amplifies B cell differentiation. The dense neutrophilic synovitis in SpA differs from the predominantly mononuclear infiltrate in RA.","boardPrep":"Step 1: IL-17A = neutrophil recruiter via CXCL chemokines + direct activation. IL-22 = keratinocyte/epithelial barrier. IL-21 = B cell differentiation. COMLEX: Dactylitis (sausage digit) = diffuse inflammation of entire finger including tendon sheath + joints; characteristic of PsA and reactive arthritis.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 35 Obj 1 — Immune cells producing cytokines and specific cytokines each cell type releases."},{"id":"sdl46_q16","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 24-year-old sexually active man presents 3 weeks after treatment for urethral discharge with bilateral knee swelling, unilateral eye redness with photophobia, and dysuria. He is HLA-B27 positive. Synovial fluid cultures are sterile despite visible clouding. Which organism most commonly triggers this clinical syndrome in sexually active young adults in the United States?","choices":{"A":"Chlamydia trachomatis is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing the urethritis component of the classic triad and initiating a post-infectious sterile immune response in the synovium","B":"Shigella flexneri is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing gastrointestinal colitis that seeds joint synovium through bacterial translocation across the inflamed intestinal epithelium.","C":"Salmonella typhimurium is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, entering joint synovium through blood-borne spread following mucosal breach of the inflamed intestinal epithelial barrier.","D":"Yersinia enterocolitica is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing terminal ileitis with Peyer patch involvement that initiates a gut-associated immune response seeding the sacroiliac joint.","E":"Campylobacter jejuni is the most common trigger of reactive arthritis in sexually active adults in the United States, producing acute enteritis that generates cross-reactive synovial antibodies targeting collagen type II at weight-bearing joint surfaces."},"correct":"A","explanation":"Reactive arthritis (formerly Reiter syndrome) follows infection with specific organisms: sexually acquired (Chlamydia trachomatis, most common in the US), or enteric (Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter — more common in endemic regions and after foodborne outbreaks). The classic triad is \"urethritis + conjunctivitis + arthritis\" (cannot see, cannot pee, cannot climb a tree). Synovial fluid is sterile (post-infectious immune-mediated). HLA-B27 positivity increases risk and severity. The condition is often self-limited though can become chronic (Robbins Ch 26: episodes wax and wane over months; nearly 50% have recurrent arthritis). C. trachomatis is more common in the US while enteric organisms predominate in developing countries.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Reactive arthritis triad: urethritis + conjunctivitis + arthritis. Sexually acquired = Chlamydia trachomatis (US). Enteric = Shigella, Salmonella, Yersinia, Campylobacter. HLA-B27 predisposes. COMLEX: \"Cannot see, cannot pee, cannot climb a tree\" — classic mnemonic for Reiter triad.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."},{"id":"sdl46_q17","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A 34-year-old woman with a 5-year history of Crohn disease develops bilateral sacroiliitis confirmed on MRI and insidious onset inflammatory back pain. She tests HLA-B27 negative. Colonoscopy shows active ileitis with granulomatous inflammation. Which mechanism best explains the gut-joint axis driving IBD-associated spondyloarthritis in this patient?","choices":{"A":"Intestinal barrier breach in Crohn disease allows direct bacterial seeding of sacroiliac joints via the portal venous system and Batson vertebral venous plexus, producing low-grade infectious sacroiliitis that mimics the radiographic appearance of inflammatory SpA.","B":"Activated Th1 cells from the inflamed Crohn mucosa home to sacroiliac joint synovium by following IL-8 gradients from activated synoviocytes, where IFN-gamma drives a CD4+ T cell-mediated entheseal inflammation pattern characteristic of IBD-associated SpA.","C":"Intestinal dendritic cells activated by dysbiotic microbiota in the inflamed IBD mucosa produce IL-23, driving Th17 differentiation in mesenteric lymph nodes; these Th17 cells then traffic to joint tissue via shared mucosal addressin expression","D":"Complement activation in the inflamed Crohn mucosa generates circulating C5a and IgG immune complexes that deposit in sacroiliac joint synovium, producing a Type III hypersensitivity-driven sacroiliitis with complement consumption and low C3/C4 levels.","E":"B cells activated by gut luminal bacterial antigens in Crohn disease produce cross-reactive IgG antibodies targeting sacroiliac joint proteoglycans, creating immune complex-mediated entheseal inflammation with RF-positive serology in IBD-SpA patients."},"correct":"C","explanation":"The gut-joint axis in IBD-associated SpA operates through: (1) dysbiotic gut microbiota → mucosal dendritic cell IL-23 production → Th17 differentiation in mesenteric lymph nodes → joint trafficking via alpha4-beta7 integrin and MAdCAM-1 (shared mucosal addressins expressed on both gut and joint endothelium). This explains why vedolizumab (anti-alpha4-beta7), which blocks gut-specific trafficking, can improve gut disease but may not control joint disease as well. Sacroiliac involvement in IBD-SpA correlates with gut disease activity independently of HLA-B27 status. This is not a Type III hypersensitivity or infectious process.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Gut-joint axis: IL-23 from gut mucosa → Th17 → joint via shared mucosal addressins. IBD-SpA can be HLA-B27 negative. COMLEX: IBD-associated SpA: sacroiliitis often correlates with gut flares. Vedolizumab treats gut but may not fully control joint manifestations.","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."},{"id":"sdl46_q18","objective":3,"objectiveLabel":"Objective 3 — Recognize the major elements in the pathogenesis of spondyloarthritis.","batch":2,"isHighYield":true,"stem":"A researcher proposes that some cases of HLA-B27-associated ankylosing spondylitis may be initiated by prior Klebsiella pneumoniae exposure. Through which mechanism does the molecular mimicry hypothesis explain this proposed bacterial trigger for joint inflammation in genetically susceptible individuals?","choices":{"A":"Klebsiella pneumoniae lipopolysaccharide directly binds HLA-B27 on synoviocytes, activating TLR4-mediated NF-kB signaling that triggers direct IL-17 production from innate immune synoviocytes and initiates the early entheseal inflammatory cascade in susceptible individuals.","B":"Klebsiella nitrogenase shares a peptide sequence with HLA-B27 or joint self-peptides; antibodies and T cells generated against this bacterial epitope cross-react with HLA-B27 or self-peptides presented on joint stromal cells, initiating the autoimmune entheseal inflammation of AS.","C":"Klebsiella pneumoniae directly invades the intestinal mucosa of HLA-B27-positive individuals exclusively, generating reactive oxygen species that modify collagen type II to create immunogenic neoantigens that drive autoimmune sacroiliac joint destruction.","D":"Klebsiella pneumoniae produces a urease enzyme that alkalinizes the intestinal lumen, selectively promoting growth of an IL-23-activating microbiome that produces bacteriocins capable of directly triggering synovial macrophage NLRP3 inflammasome activation.","E":"Klebsiella pullulanase contains a structural domain that mimics the IL-23 receptor extracellular portion, generating cross-reactive antibodies that constitutively activate IL-23 receptor signaling and drive uncontrolled Th17 expansion in HLA-B27-positive hosts."},"correct":"B","explanation":"The molecular mimicry hypothesis for AS proposes that a Klebsiella nitrogenase peptide resembles a sequence on HLA-B27, so immune responses to the bacterium cross-react with HLA-B27-expressing or joint tissues. Immune responses to Klebsiella generate cross-reactive T cells and antibodies that target synovial and skeletal tissue in HLA-B27-positive individuals. This is supported by elevated anti-Klebsiella antibodies in active AS and increased Klebsiella colonization rates in active disease. Molecular mimicry is a broad mechanism in autoimmunity where pathogen-derived epitopes resemble self-antigens, linking infection to autoimmune initiation.","boardPrep":"Step 1: Molecular mimicry in SpA — Klebsiella nitrogenase shares sequence homology with HLA-B27; supports arthritogenic peptide hypothesis. COMLEX: Molecular mimicry is a key concept across rheumatic diseases — also applies to reactive arthritis (Chlamydia cross-reaction with self-antigens in HLA-B27+ hosts).","crossRef":"📖 SDL 45 Obj 2 — Classes and mechanisms of biologic and conventional DMARDs."}]};
